var e={notes:[{id:`notre-dame-de-la-garde`,term:`Notre-Dame de la Garde`,kind:`place`,chapter:1,text:`The hilltop church above Marseilles harbour. Its height made it the port's lookout post, from which approaching ships were signalled to the town.`,quote:`the look-out at Notre-Dame de la Garde`,placeId:`notre-dame-de-la-garde`},{id:`supercargo`,term:`supercargo`,kind:`word`,chapter:1,text:`The officer on a merchant ship in charge of the cargo, its buying and selling, and the accounts: the owner's business agent at sea, not a navigator.`,quote:`here is your supercargo, M. Danglars`},{id:`brain-fever`,term:`brain-fever`,kind:`word`,chapter:1,text:`An old catch-all diagnosis for a sudden high fever with delirium. Today it might be called meningitis or encephalitis.`,quote:`he died of brain-fever in dreadful agony`},{id:`burial-at-sea`,term:`burial at sea`,kind:`custom`,chapter:1,text:`A sailor who died on a voyage was sewn into his hammock or a canvas sack, weighted with cannon shot so that it would sink, and lowered into the sea after prayers.`,quote:`sewn up in his hammock with a thirty-six-pound shot`},{id:`cross-of-honor`,term:`cross of honor`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`The Legion of Honour, France's highest decoration, created by Napoleon in 1802. Leclere won his through long service, the 'ten years' of war against Britain under the Revolution and Empire.`,quote:`his sword and cross of honor`},{id:`francs`,term:`francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:1,text:`France's basic currency. In 1815 a labourer might earn one or two francs a day, so 25,000 francs, the profit on a single voyage, was a very large sum.`,quote:`not to take 25,000 francs for the profits`},{id:`elba`,term:`Elba`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`A small island off the coast of Tuscany. Napoleon abdicated in April 1814 and was given Elba to rule in exile. In February 1815 he was living there, a few days' sail from France.`,quote:`a day and a half at the Island of Elba`},{id:`porto-ferrajo`,term:`Porto-Ferrajo`,kind:`place`,chapter:1,text:`Portoferraio, the chief port and town of Elba, where Napoleon and his small court lived.`,quote:`his landing at Porto-Ferrajo`,placeId:`elba`},{id:`pardieu-peste`,term:`Pardieu! / Peste!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:1,text:`Mild French oaths. 'Pardieu' means 'by God!'; 'peste', literally 'plague!', is more like 'good heavens!'`,quote:`Pardieu! and that is true!`,seeAlso:[7]},{id:`phoceens`,term:`Phocéens`,kind:`history`,chapter:1,text:`Marseilles was founded around 600 BC by Greek colonists from Phocaea in Asia Minor, so its people are poetically called Phocaeans. La Canebière is its proud main street, running down to the harbour.`,quote:`the modern Phocéens are so proud`},{id:`louis`,term:`louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:2,text:`A gold coin worth 20 francs. A hundred louis, or 2,000 francs a year, was handsome pay for a young captain, and many times a labourer's earnings.`,quote:`a captain at twenty, with a hundred louis pay`},{id:`sixty-francs`,term:`sixty francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:2,text:`Sixty francs for three months comes to about two-thirds of a franc a day: barely enough for bread. That is why the cupboards are empty.`,quote:`you have lived for three months on sixty francs`},{id:`smuggled-coffee-and-tobacco`,term:`smuggled coffee and tobacco`,kind:`custom`,chapter:2,text:`Sailors routinely brought in a little contraband for themselves. Tobacco was a heavily taxed state monopoly in France, and coffee carried high duties, so neither was thought of as a real crime.`,quote:`some smuggled coffee and most capital tobacco`},{id:`ma-foi`,term:`Ma foi!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:2,text:`French: 'my faith!', meaning 'upon my word!' or 'well, really!'`,quote:`“Ma foi! it will be as well if he is not,”`},{id:`the-catalane`,term:`the Catalane`,kind:`word`,chapter:2,text:`The French feminine of 'Catalan': a Catalan woman, here Mercédès.`,quote:`is he still in love with the Catalane?`},{id:`pere-pamphile`,term:`Père Pamphile`,kind:`title`,chapter:2,text:`'Père' ('Father') was a familiar title for an older tradesman, here the keeper of La Réserve, a tavern by the harbour. It does not mean he is a priest.`,quote:`Père Pamphile had seen Dantès pass`},{id:`posada`,term:`posada`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:3,text:`Spanish: an inn. The Catalans' houses are whitewashed inside like a Spanish inn.`,quote:`coated with whitewash, like a Spanish posada`},{id:`provencal`,term:`Provençal`,kind:`word`,chapter:3,text:`The language of Provence. It is a Romance language distinct from French, and in 1815 it was the everyday speech of most people in Marseilles.`,quote:`One of its chiefs, who understood Provençal`},{id:`arlesian-venus`,term:`Arlesian Venus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:3,text:`The Venus of Arles, a Roman marble statue found at Arles in 1651 and now in the Louvre. It was a classic standard of female beauty.`,quote:`modelled after those of the Arlesian Venus`},{id:`a-sacred-law-to-intermarry`,term:`a sacred law to intermarry`,kind:`custom`,chapter:3,text:`Close-knit communities like the Catalans expected their young people to marry within the group, and marriage between cousins was common. Mercédès points out that this is a custom, not a law.`,quote:`it is among the Catalans a sacred law to intermarry`},{id:`conscription`,term:`conscription`,kind:`history`,chapter:3,text:`Napoleonic France drafted young men by annual levy. Even with peace restored, Fernand is on the rolls and could be called up at any time.`,quote:`You are included in the conscription`},{id:`dirk`,term:`dirk`,kind:`word`,chapter:3,text:`A short stabbing dagger of the kind sailors carried.`,quote:`cross your Catalan knife with his dirk`},{id:`bandilleros`,term:`bandilleros`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:3,text:`Spanish banderilleros: the bullfighters who plant barbed darts in the bull's shoulders to goad it into charging.`,quote:`as the bull is by the bandilleros`},{id:`the-grand-marshal`,term:`the grand marshal`,kind:`title`,chapter:3,text:`Grand Marshal of the Palace, the head of Napoleon's household. Marshal Bertrand held the post and followed him to Elba.`,quote:`the letter which the grand marshal gave him`},{id:`seek-and-you-shall-find`,term:`seek, and you shall find`,kind:`reference`,chapter:4,text:`Christ's words in the Gospel of Matthew (7:7), which Danglars borrows to nudge Fernand.`,quote:`in the words of the gospel, seek, and you shall find`},{id:`tous-les-mechants-sont-buveurs-d-eau`,term:`Tous les méchants sont buveurs d’eau`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:4,text:`French drinking song: 'All wicked men drink water; the Flood proves it.' Caderousse uses it to excuse his drinking.`,quote:`Tous les méchants sont buveurs d’eau`},{id:`king-s-procureur-king-s-attorney`,term:`king’s procureur / king’s attorney`,kind:`title`,chapter:4,text:`The procureur du roi was the state prosecutor for a district, responsible for criminal cases including political ones. A deputy (substitut) acted in his absence.`,quote:`denounce him to the king’s procureur`,seeAlso:[6,7]},{id:`bonapartist`,term:`Bonapartist`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`A supporter of Napoleon. Under the restored Bourbon king Louis XVIII, plotting for the exiled emperor was treason, and royalist officials were quick to suspect it.`,quote:`as a Bonapartist agent`,seeAlso:[5,6]},{id:`murat`,term:`Murat`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`Joachim Murat, Napoleon's brother-in-law and dashing cavalry marshal, made King of Naples in 1808 and still on that throne in 1815. A letter 'from Murat' suggests a plot reaching from Naples to Elba.`,quote:`has been intrusted by Murat with a letter`},{id:`the-usurper`,term:`the usurper`,kind:`history`,chapter:4,text:`Royalists' name for Napoleon, implying that he had stolen the throne from the rightful Bourbon kings.`,quote:`a letter for the usurper`,seeAlso:[44,72,89]},{id:`the-accoules`,term:`the Accoules`,kind:`place`,chapter:4,text:`The bell tower of the old church of Notre-Dame des Accoules, a landmark of Marseilles' old quarter.`,quote:`go up into the belfry of the Accoules`,placeId:`accoules`},{id:`the-mayor-at-the-city-hall`,term:`the Mayor at the city hall`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`Since the Revolution a French marriage became legal only before the mayor at the town hall. Banns had to be published first and a waiting period observed. Morrel's influence, and a fee, let the couple skip the delay.`,quote:`We have purchased permission to waive the usual delay`},{id:`the-contract-the-settlement`,term:`the contract—the settlement`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`Families with property drew up a marriage contract before a notary, fixing the bride's dowry and what each spouse brought. Dantès jokes that with nothing on either side, theirs was quick and cheap.`,quote:`the other formalities—the contract—the settlement?`},{id:`the-aged-dandies-of-1796`,term:`the aged dandies of 1796`,kind:`history`,chapter:5,text:`After the Terror, flamboyant young Parisians (the Incroyables) paraded their extravagant clothes in the city's gardens. Old Dantès's finery is a generation out of date.`,quote:`one of the aged dandies of 1796`},{id:`echinus-clovis`,term:`echinus / clovis`,kind:`word`,chapter:5,text:`The echinus is the sea urchin; clovis are small clams. Together with the lobsters and prawns they make up the 'fruits of the sea' (fruits de mer).`,quote:`the echinus with its prickly outside`},{id:`official-scarf`,term:`official scarf`,kind:`custom`,chapter:5,text:`A sash worn by French magistrates and police commissaries as a sign of office when acting in the name of the law.`,quote:`a magistrate, wearing his official scarf`},{id:`the-abdication-of-napoleon`,term:`the abdication of Napoleon`,kind:`history`,chapter:5,text:`Napoleon gave up his throne in April 1814 after the allied armies took Paris. Regretting his fall, or serving the 'other government' as Morrel's uncle did, made a man politically suspect.`,quote:`regretting the abdication of Napoleon`},{id:`palais-de-justice`,term:`Palais de Justice`,kind:`place`,chapter:5,text:`The law courts of Marseilles, which housed the prosecutors' offices and connected to a holding prison.`,quote:`in the direction of the Palais de Justice`,seeAlso:[8],placeId:`palais-de-justice-marseilles`},{id:`cross-of-saint-louis`,term:`cross of Saint Louis`,kind:`title`,chapter:6,text:`A royal military order founded by Louis XIV and revived at the Restoration. Its cross marked an officer loyal to the old monarchy.`,quote:`decorated with the cross of Saint Louis`},{id:`hartwell`,term:`Hartwell`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`Hartwell House in Buckinghamshire, England, where Louis XVIII lived in exile from 1809 until he took the throne in 1814.`,quote:`the patient exile of Hartwell`},{id:`reign-of-terror`,term:`Reign of Terror`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`1793–94, when the Revolutionary government guillotined thousands of suspected enemies, many of them nobles. Exiled nobles' lands were seized and sold off cheaply.`,quote:`for a mere trifle during the Reign of Terror`},{id:`girondin`,term:`Girondin`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`A member of the moderate republican faction of the Revolution. Many Girondins were themselves purged and guillotined by the radical Jacobins in 1793. Some had voted for Louis XVI's execution, which is why Villefort says his father did not.`,quote:`the son of a Girondin`},{id:`the-9th-thermidor-and-the-4th-of-april`,term:`the 9th Thermidor and the 4th of April`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`9 Thermidor (27 July 1794, by the Revolutionary calendar) was the day Robespierre fell, ending the Terror. Early April 1814 saw Napoleon forced to abdicate. Royalists celebrated both dates.`,quote:`the 9th Thermidor and the 4th of April`},{id:`place-louis-quinze-place-vendome`,term:`Place Louis Quinze / Place Vendôme`,kind:`place`,chapter:6,text:`Two Paris squares. The Place Louis XV (now the Place de la Concorde) held the guillotine that executed Louis XVI and later Robespierre. In the Place Vendôme stands a bronze column, cast from captured cannon, that celebrates Napoleon's victories.`,quote:`his scaffold in the Place Louis Quinze`},{id:`half-pay-officers`,term:`half-pay officers`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`When the army was cut back in 1814, thousands of Napoleon's officers were put on half pay. Idle, poor and resentful, they were a natural pool of Bonapartist feeling.`,quote:`Marseilles is filled with half-pay officers`},{id:`holy-alliance`,term:`Holy Alliance`,kind:`history`,chapter:6,text:`A pact of Russia, Austria and Prussia, formally signed only in September 1815, so Dumas uses the name loosely for the victorious allies. Saint Helena, where they talk of sending Napoleon, is a tiny British island in the remote South Atlantic.`,quote:`the Holy Alliance purpose removing him from thence`},{id:`league`,term:`league`,kind:`money`,chapter:6,text:`An old measure of distance, about 4 km (2.5 miles). Two thousand leagues is roughly 8,000 km.`,quote:`at least two thousand leagues from here`,seeAlso:[82,85,112]},{id:`cedant-arma-tog`,term:`Cedant arma togæ`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:6,text:`Latin, from Cicero: 'let arms yield to the toga', meaning that civil authority should rule over the military. Villefort means that the magistrate's robe now counts for more than the soldier's sword.`,quote:`“Cedant arma togæ,” said Villefort with a bow.`},{id:`commissary-of-police`,term:`commissary of police`,kind:`title`,chapter:7,text:`The commissaire de police, the local police official who made arrests and gathered evidence for the prosecutor.`,quote:`he met the commissary of police`,seeAlso:[82,96,98]},{id:`fifty-thousand-crowns`,term:`fifty thousand crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:7,text:`A crown (écu) is reckoned here at three francs, so Renée's dowry is about 150,000 francs, with half a million more to come. Either sum was a fortune when a labourer earned a franc or two a day.`,quote:`The dowry of his wife amounted to fifty thousand crowns`},{id:`the-third-heaven`,term:`the third heaven`,kind:`reference`,chapter:7,text:`Saint Paul's phrase (2 Corinthians 12:2) for the highest heaven, used here to mean the height of bliss.`,quote:`recalled Villefort from the third heaven to earth`},{id:`carbonari`,term:`Carbonari`,kind:`history`,chapter:7,text:`Secret revolutionary societies that began in the Kingdom of Naples (the name means 'charcoal burners'). To a royalist the word stood for any conspiracy.`,quote:`a member of some Carbonari society`},{id:`gendarmes`,term:`gendarmes`,kind:`title`,chapter:7,text:`France's armed military police, used for arrests, escorts and guarding prisoners.`,quote:`full of police agents and gendarmes`},{id:`the-trial-justice`,term:`the trial justice`,kind:`title`,chapter:7,text:`The juge d'instruction, or examining magistrate, who formally investigated a case before any trial. Villefort claims he must consult him.`,quote:`I must consult the trial justice`},{id:`frioul`,term:`Frioul`,kind:`place`,chapter:8,text:`The Frioul islands off Marseilles (Pomègue, Ratonneau and the little Île d'If) and the waters around them, just outside the harbour mouth.`,quote:`they were, as Dantès knew, in the Frioul`,placeId:`chateau-dif`},{id:`like-a-tuscan-like-a-castilian`,term:`like a Tuscan … like a Castilian`,kind:`custom`,chapter:8,text:`Tuscan Italian and Castilian Spanish were the prestige forms of each language, so Dantès speaks both like a well-spoken native.`,quote:`He spoke Italian like a Tuscan, and Spanish like a Castilian`},{id:`ten-sous`,term:`ten sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:8,text:`A sou was a twentieth of a franc, so ten sous is half a franc a day, the allowance the jailer draws for each prisoner.`,quote:`every prisoner is worth ten sous a day`},{id:`a-hundred-crowns`,term:`a hundred crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:8,text:`The jailer works out a hundred crowns as three hundred francs, a big bribe but small beside his post, worth 2,000 francs a year.`,quote:`I will give you a hundred crowns`},{id:`abbe`,term:`abbé`,kind:`title`,chapter:8,text:`The courtesy title of a French secular cleric, often a scholar or tutor rather than a parish priest. It does not mean the abbot of a monastery.`,quote:`that an abbé became mad`,seeAlso:[14]},{id:`brutus`,term:`Brutus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:9,text:`Lucius Junius Brutus, legendary founder of the Roman Republic, who sentenced his own sons to death for plotting to bring back the kings. He became a byword for stern duty.`,quote:`Guardian of the State, Royalist, Brutus`},{id:`the-corsican-ogre`,term:`the Corsican ogre`,kind:`history`,chapter:9,text:`A royalist nickname for Napoleon, who was born in Corsica in 1769. It comes from propaganda that showed him as a monster devouring France's young men.`,quote:`Has the Corsican ogre broken loose?`,seeAlso:[11,12]},{id:`the-funds`,term:`the funds`,kind:`money`,chapter:9,text:`French government bonds (rentes), traded on the Paris Bourse. Their price swung with political news, so selling 'at the market price' before bad news broke would save a fortune.`,quote:`All my fortune is in the funds`},{id:`keeper-of-the-seals`,term:`keeper of the seals`,kind:`title`,chapter:9,text:`The Garde des Sceaux, France's minister of justice, who outranked every prosecutor and had the right to see the king at any hour.`,quote:`address yourself to the keeper of the seals`},{id:`virgil-s-wounded-hero`,term:`Virgil’s wounded hero`,kind:`reference`,chapter:9,text:`From Book 4 of Virgil's Aeneid: the deer that runs on with the hunter's arrow still in its side, an image for a wound that cannot be escaped.`,quote:`like Virgil’s wounded hero, he carried the arrow`},{id:`postilions`,term:`postilions`,kind:`word`,chapter:9,text:`Riders who guided post-horses. Changing to fresh horses at relay stations every few leagues let a traveller cross France fast, and quicker still for extra fees.`,quote:`ordering the postilions to drive`},{id:`hoffmann`,term:`Hoffmann`,kind:`reference`,chapter:9,text:`E. T. A. Hoffmann (1776–1822), German author of eerie, fantastical tales, said to have written fuelled by punch.`,quote:`spectres such as Hoffmann strews over his punch-drenched pages`},{id:`the-tuileries`,term:`the Tuileries`,kind:`place`,chapter:10,text:`The royal palace in Paris beside the Louvre, the seat of government for Napoleon and the Bourbons alike. It burned in 1871.`,quote:`enter at the Tuileries the little room`,placeId:`tuileries`},{id:`louis-philippe`,term:`Louis Philippe`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`King of the French from 1830 to 1848, and on the throne when Dumas wrote the novel in 1844. Dumas is reminding readers that the same study served three rulers.`,quote:`and now of Louis Philippe`},{id:`the-seven-fat-kine-and-the-seven-lean-kine`,term:`the seven fat kine and the seven lean kine`,kind:`reference`,chapter:10,text:`Pharaoh's dream in Genesis 41, which Joseph explains as seven years of plenty followed by seven years of famine. 'Kine' is an old plural of 'cow'.`,quote:`the seven fat kine and the seven lean kine`},{id:`canimus-surdis`,term:`Canimus surdis`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:10,text:`Latin: 'we sing to the deaf'. The king is saying that warnings would fall on deaf ears.`,quote:`“Canimus surdis,” replied the king`},{id:`pastor-quum-traheret`,term:`Pastor quum traheret`,kind:`reference`,chapter:10,text:`The opening words of Horace's Odes 1.15: 'When the shepherd [Paris] was carrying off [Helen]'. In this ode the sea god Nereus warns Paris of the war his crime will bring. 'Mala ducis avi domum' ('with evil omen you lead her home') and 'molli fugiens anhelitu' ('fleeing, softly panting') come from the same poem.`,quote:`a delightful note on the Pastor quum traheret`},{id:`bella-horrida-bella`,term:`bella, horrida bella`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:10,text:`Latin, from Virgil's Aeneid (6.86): 'wars, terrible wars', the Sibyl's prophecy to Aeneas.`,quote:`bella, horrida bella`},{id:`marengo-or-austerlitz`,term:`Marengo or Austerlitz`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`Two of Napoleon's most famous victories, over the Austrians in 1800 and the Austrians and Russians in 1805.`,quote:`as if he had gained another Marengo or Austerlitz`},{id:`virgil-s-shepherds`,term:`Virgil’s shepherds`,kind:`reference`,chapter:10,text:`In Virgil's Eclogues, pairs of shepherds compete in song by answering each other verse for verse, just as the king and his minister take turns here.`,quote:`spoke alternately, like Virgil’s shepherds`},{id:`telegraph`,term:`telegraph`,kind:`history`,chapter:10,text:`The Chappe optical telegraph: a chain of hilltop towers whose movable arms relayed coded signals. It could carry a message across France in hours, but only in daylight and clear weather.`,quote:`we have a telegraph which transmits messages in three or four hours`,seeAlso:[11]},{id:`justum-et-tenacem-propositi-virum`,term:`Justum et tenacem propositi virum`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:10,text:`Latin, from Horace (Odes 3.3): 'the man who is just and firm of purpose'. 'Tenax', the motto the king jokingly offers Blacas, means 'tenacious'.`,quote:`“Justum et tenacem propositi virum.”`},{id:`the-gulf-of-juan`,term:`the Gulf of Juan`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`Golfe-Juan, a bay near Antibes on the Riviera, where Napoleon came ashore on 1 March 1815. He then took the mountain road north through Gap and Sisteron, still known as the Route Napoléon.`,quote:`near Antibes, in the Gulf of Juan`},{id:`seven-conjoined-and-allied-armies`,term:`seven conjoined and allied armies`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`The coalition of European powers, including Russia, Prussia, Austria and Britain, that defeated Napoleon in 1813–14 and put Louis XVIII on the throne after his 25 years in exile.`,quote:`seven conjoined and allied armies overthrew that man`},{id:`learnt-nothing-forgotten-nothing`,term:`learnt nothing, forgotten nothing`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`A famous jibe at the Bourbons and their returning émigrés, usually credited to Talleyrand. It meant that exile had taught them nothing, and that they had forgotten none of their old privileges or grudges.`,quote:`We have learnt nothing, forgotten nothing!`},{id:`the-scaffold-of-my-brother-louis-xvi`,term:`the scaffold of my brother, Louis XVI.`,kind:`history`,chapter:11,text:`Louis XVIII's elder brother, King Louis XVI, was guillotined by the Revolution in January 1793.`,quote:`mount the scaffold of my brother, Louis XVI.`},{id:`secret-service-money`,term:`secret service money`,kind:`money`,chapter:11,text:`A million and a half francs a year for spies and informers, an enormous budget at a time when a skilled worker might earn under a thousand francs a year.`,quote:`fifteen hundred thousand francs for secret service money`},{id:`officer-of-the-legion-of-honor`,term:`officer of the Legion of Honor`,kind:`title`,chapter:11,text:`The Legion has grades. A knight (chevalier) wears a plain red ribbon, while an officer wears a rosette and a cross of higher rank, more than a young provincial magistrate could expect. A brevet is the official certificate of the award.`,quote:`this is an officer’s cross`},{id:`hackney-coach`,term:`hackney-coach`,kind:`word`,chapter:11,text:`A horse-drawn cab for hire, the taxi of its day.`,quote:`looking about him for a hackney-coach`,seeAlso:[92]},{id:`the-mountaineers`,term:`the mountaineers`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`The Montagnards ('the Mountain'), the radical Jacobin deputies who sat on the high benches of the Revolutionary assembly. In 1793 they drove out and hunted down the Girondins.`,quote:`proscribed by the mountaineers`},{id:`the-sin-which-ruined-our-first-parents`,term:`the sin which ruined our first parents`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`Curiosity: Eve and Adam ate the forbidden fruit of knowledge in Genesis. Germain has been listening at the door.`,quote:`the sin which ruined our first parents`},{id:`the-restoration`,term:`the Restoration`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`The return of the Bourbon kings in 1814, when Louis XVIII came back after Napoleon's fall.`,quote:`will the Restoration adopt imperial methods so promptly?`},{id:`the-barrier`,term:`the barrier`,kind:`word`,chapter:12,text:`The toll gates in the wall around Paris, where goods were taxed and travellers could be watched.`,quote:`half an hour after you had passed the barrier`},{id:`corsican-ogre-usurper-bonaparte-emperor`,term:`Corsican ogre … usurper … Bonaparte … emperor`,kind:`reference`,chapter:12,text:`This echoes a famous, probably invented, joke about Paris newspapers in March 1815. Their headlines supposedly grew more respectful each day as Napoleon came nearer, from 'the monster has landed' to 'His Majesty has entered Paris'.`,quote:`you call the Corsican ogre, who at Nevers is styled the usurper`},{id:`his-own-eagles`,term:`his own eagles`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`Napoleon's regiments carried standards topped with gilded eagles. His proclamation on landing promised that 'the eagle' would fly from steeple to steeple all the way to Notre-Dame.`,quote:`as rapidly as his own eagles`},{id:`grandson-of-saint-louis`,term:`grandson of Saint Louis`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`Louis IX (reigned 1226–70) was made a saint, and the Bourbon kings proudly claimed descent from him.`,quote:`a grandson of Saint Louis`},{id:`arcola`,term:`Arcola`,kind:`history`,chapter:12,text:`A battle in northern Italy in 1796, famous for the young General Bonaparte leading a charge across a bridge under fire, flag in hand.`,quote:`the man of Arcola, Marengo, Austerlitz`},{id:`the-hundred-days`,term:`The Hundred Days`,kind:`history`,chapter:13,text:`Napoleon's brief return to power in 1815, from his arrival in Paris on 20 March until his final abdication in late June, roughly a hundred days.`,quote:`Twice during the Hundred Days had Morrel renewed his demand`},{id:`waterloo`,term:`Waterloo`,kind:`history`,chapter:13,text:`The battle in Belgium on 18 June 1815 where Wellington and Blücher defeated Napoleon. It ended the Hundred Days, and Louis XVIII returned to the throne in July (the 'second restoration').`,quote:`At last there was Waterloo`,seeAlso:[92]},{id:`fenestrelles-pignerol-sainte-marguerite`,term:`Fenestrelles, Pignerol, Sainte-Marguérite`,kind:`place`,chapter:13,text:`Fortress prisons for state prisoners: two in the Piedmontese Alps and one on an island off Cannes. Pignerol and Sainte-Marguerite famously held the 'Man in the Iron Mask'.`,quote:`taken to Fenestrelles, to Pignerol, or to the Sainte-Marguérite islands`},{id:`since-the-reign-of-louis-xiv`,term:`since the reign of Louis XIV.`,kind:`history`,chapter:13,text:`Under the old monarchy a sealed royal order, the lettre de cachet, could lock someone up indefinitely without trial. Villefort suggests that such 'disappearances' never really stopped.`,quote:`since the reign of Louis XIV.`},{id:`prefect-of-the-department`,term:`prefect of the department`,kind:`title`,chapter:13,text:`The government-appointed head of a département, one of France's administrative regions, with charge of police and prisons.`,quote:`the governors of the prison or the prefect of the department`},{id:`the-south-was-aflame`,term:`the south was aflame`,kind:`history`,chapter:13,text:`After Waterloo, royalist mobs in Provence and Languedoc attacked and murdered suspected Bonapartists, the so-called White Terror of 1815. Helping the family of a 'Bonapartist' took courage.`,quote:`the south was aflame`},{id:`inspector-general-of-prisons`,term:`inspector-general of prisons`,kind:`title`,chapter:14,text:`A senior official sent from Paris to tour prisons, check conditions and hear prisoners' complaints.`,quote:`a visit was made by the inspector-general of prisons`},{id:`charenton`,term:`Charenton`,kind:`place`,chapter:14,text:`The great lunatic asylum just outside Paris.`,quote:`I made some curious observations on this at Charenton`},{id:`archimedes`,term:`Archimedes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`The Greek mathematician, said to have been killed by a Roman soldier at the fall of Syracuse (212 BC) while absorbed in drawing geometric figures.`,quote:`as Archimedes was when the soldier of Marcellus slew him`},{id:`kingdom-of-rome-for-his-infant-son`,term:`kingdom of Rome for his infant son`,kind:`history`,chapter:14,text:`Napoleon annexed Rome to his empire and gave his son, born in 1811, the title 'King of Rome' at birth. Much of Italy was carved into French départements.`,quote:`the emperor had created the kingdom of Rome for his infant son`,seeAlso:[16]},{id:`the-dream-of-machiavelli`,term:`the dream of Machiavelli`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`In The Prince (1513), the Florentine thinker Niccolò Machiavelli called for a strong ruler to unite and free Italy, and held up Cesare Borgia as a model.`,quote:`the dream of Machiavelli and Cæsar Borgia`},{id:`having-eyes-see-not`,term:`having eyes see not`,kind:`reference`,chapter:14,text:`A biblical phrase (Psalm 115; Mark 8:18) for people who refuse to see what is in front of them.`,quote:`having eyes see not, and having ears hear not`},{id:`caligula-or-nero`,term:`Caligula or Nero`,kind:`history`,chapter:14,text:`Roman emperors notorious for wild extravagance. Nero once sent men digging in Africa for the legendary buried gold of Queen Dido.`,quote:`Caligula or Nero, those treasure-seekers`},{id:`the-galleys`,term:`the galleys`,kind:`history`,chapter:15,text:`Convicts 'sent to the galleys' by then served hard labour at the naval prisons of Toulon or Brest. They were branded, dressed in red, and chained in pairs, but they worked in the open air.`,quote:`the infamous costume, the chain, and the brand`,seeAlso:[45,82,83]},{id:`martin-s-babylonian-pictures`,term:`Martin’s Babylonian pictures`,kind:`reference`,chapter:15,text:`John Martin (1789–1854), English painter of vast, apocalyptic scenes of ancient cities, such as Belshazzar's Feast and The Fall of Babylon.`,quote:`in Martin’s Babylonian pictures`},{id:`ugolino`,term:`Ugolino`,kind:`reference`,chapter:15,text:`In Dante's Inferno (cantos 32–33), Count Ugolino was starved to death in a tower with his sons. In hell he gnaws forever at the skull of Archbishop Ruggieri, who betrayed him.`,quote:`as the implacable Ugolino devours the skull of Archbishop Roger`},{id:`mene-mene-tekel-upharsin`,term:`mene, mene, tekel upharsin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:15,text:`The 'writing on the wall' in Daniel 5. A ghostly hand writes these words at King Belshazzar's feast, foretelling his downfall.`,quote:`the mene, mene, tekel upharsin of Belshazzar`,seeAlso:[72]},{id:`tantalus`,term:`Tantalus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:15,text:`In Greek myth, Tantalus was punished in the underworld by standing in water beneath fruit trees, with food and drink always just out of reach. Dantès is a 'voluntary' one because he refuses his own food.`,quote:`like a voluntary Tantalus, he refused himself`},{id:`where-two-or-three-are-gathered-together`,term:`where two or three are gathered together`,kind:`reference`,chapter:15,text:`Christ's promise in Matthew 18:20: 'where two or three are gathered together in my name, there am I in the midst of them.'`,quote:`prayers where two or three are gathered together`},{id:`after-charles-i-cromwell`,term:`After Charles I., Cromwell`,kind:`history`,chapter:16,text:`Faria compares France with England. There, Charles I was executed (1649), Cromwell ruled, the monarchy returned under Charles II, and James II was ousted in 1688 by his son-in-law William of Orange, stadtholder (head of state) of Holland. Parliament's rights then grew.`,quote:`After Charles I., Cromwell; after Cromwell, Charles II.`},{id:`alexander-vi-and-clement-vii`,term:`Alexander VI. and Clement VII.`,kind:`history`,chapter:16,text:`Two Renaissance popes with dreams of power in Italy. Alexander VI (Rodrigo Borgia, pope 1492–1503) was Cesare Borgia's father; Clement VII (Giulio de' Medici, pope 1523–34) belonged to the Florentine banking dynasty.`,quote:`It was the plan of Alexander VI. and Clement VII.`,seeAlso:[18]},{id:`celebrated-cases-of-escape`,term:`celebrated cases of escape`,kind:`history`,chapter:16,text:`Famous jailbreaks. The Duc de Beaufort escaped the fortress of Vincennes in 1648, and Latude got out of the Bastille in 1756 down a rope ladder made from his linen.`,quote:`the escape of the Duc de Beaufort from the Château de Vincennes`},{id:`lavoisier-cabanis`,term:`Lavoisier … Cabanis`,kind:`history`,chapter:16,text:`Antoine Lavoisier, founder of modern chemistry, was guillotined in 1794. Pierre Cabanis (1757–1808) was a celebrated physician and philosopher.`,quote:`I know Lavoisier, and was the intimate friend of Cabanis`,seeAlso:[17]},{id:`quarto`,term:`quarto`,kind:`word`,chapter:16,text:`A large book size, made by folding each printed sheet twice to give four leaves.`,quote:`will make one large quarto volume`},{id:`strada-jornandes`,term:`Strada, Jornandes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:16,text:`The less familiar names on Faria's list. Famiano Strada was a 17th-century Jesuit historian of the Dutch wars; Jordanes was a 6th-century historian of the Goths.`,quote:`Titus Livius, Tacitus, Strada, Jornandes`},{id:`maigre-days`,term:`maigre days`,kind:`custom`,chapter:16,text:`Catholic fast days ('lean' days) when meat was forbidden and fish was served instead. Faria names Wednesday, Friday and Saturday.`,quote:`served to us on maigre days`},{id:`guzerat-and-golconda`,term:`Guzerat and Golconda`,kind:`place`,chapter:17,text:`Gujarat in western India and Golconda near Hyderabad, whose legendary diamond mines and markets made 'Golconda' a byword for fabulous riches.`,quote:`the mines of Guzerat and Golconda`},{id:`to-whom-could-be-in-any-way-advantageous`,term:`to whom … could be in any way advantageous`,kind:`reference`,chapter:17,text:`The old Roman legal question 'cui bono?' ('who benefits?'), a detective's first rule long before detective stories.`,quote:`seek first to discover the person to whom the perpetration`},{id:`twelve-thousand-livres-civil-list`,term:`twelve thousand livres … civil list`,kind:`money`,chapter:17,text:`The livre was the old French money unit, replaced by the nearly equal franc in 1795. The civil list is the yearly sum granted to a monarch for his household. Faria's point is that 12,000 livres matters as much to a clerk as 12 million does to a king.`,quote:`these twelve thousand livres are his civil list`},{id:`descartes-theory`,term:`Descartes’ theory`,kind:`reference`,chapter:17,text:`René Descartes pictured the universe as whirling vortices of matter pressing on one another, an image for the jostling of competing interests.`,quote:`as in Descartes’ theory of pressure and impulsion`},{id:`queen-of-etruria`,term:`Queen of Etruria`,kind:`history`,chapter:17,text:`Napoleon turned Tuscany into the short-lived Kingdom of Etruria (1801–07) for a branch of the Bourbons. Its queen, Maria Luisa of Spain, kept a court in Florence.`,quote:`at the court of the Queen of Etruria`},{id:`romaic`,term:`Romaic`,kind:`word`,chapter:17,text:`Modern (spoken) Greek, as opposed to the ancient language.`,quote:`a little of the Romaic dialect`,seeAlso:[34,49,77]},{id:`catalepsy`,term:`catalepsy`,kind:`word`,chapter:17,text:`A seizure in which the body goes rigid and seems lifeless. Faria's 'attacks', with convulsions and later paralysis, sound to modern ears like epilepsy and strokes.`,quote:`I am about to be seized with a fit of catalepsy`,seeAlso:[28]},{id:`gothic-characters`,term:`Gothic characters`,kind:`word`,chapter:18,text:`Blackletter, the heavy, angular script used in late-medieval manuscripts.`,quote:`traces of Gothic characters`},{id:`romagna`,term:`Romagna`,kind:`history`,chapter:18,text:`A region of north-eastern Italy, nominally papal, which Cesare Borgia conquered in 1499–1503 with French backing from King Louis XII.`,quote:`The great wars of Romagna had ended`},{id:`the-two-hats-to-sell`,term:`the two hats to sell`,kind:`custom`,chapter:18,text:`A cardinal's red hat is the symbol of his rank. Selling church offices (simony) was a notorious scandal of the Borgia papacy.`,quote:`he had the two hats to sell besides`},{id:`legate-a-latere`,term:`legate à latere`,kind:`title`,chapter:18,text:`Latin 'from the side': a papal envoy sent as the pope's personal representative, as if from his own side.`,quote:`it was a legate à latere`},{id:`breviary`,term:`breviary`,kind:`word`,chapter:18,text:`The book of daily prayers, psalms and readings that every priest was bound to recite.`,quote:`my breviary with the gold corners`},{id:`annuity`,term:`annuity`,kind:`money`,chapter:18,text:`Handing over capital in exchange for a fixed income for life. It raises your income, but nothing is left to your heirs.`,quote:`I advised him to invest all he had in an annuity`},{id:`the-arabian-nights`,term:`the Arabian Nights`,kind:`reference`,chapter:18,text:`The Thousand and One Nights, the great collection of Middle Eastern tales, full of genies guarding treasure in caves. It was hugely popular in Europe from the 1700s.`,quote:`like the treasures of the Arabian Nights`,seeAlso:[23]},{id:`sympathetic-ink`,term:`sympathetic ink`,kind:`word`,chapter:18,text:`Invisible ink that shows only when heated or treated, made from lemon juice, for example.`,quote:`traced in mysterious and sympathetic ink`},{id:`two-millions-of-roman-crowns`,term:`two millions of Roman crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:18,text:`The Roman crown (scudo) was a papal silver coin. Faria's 'thirteen millions' in francs was an almost unimaginable fortune when a comfortable family could live on a few thousand francs a year.`,quote:`Two millions of Roman crowns; nearly thirteen millions of our money`},{id:`entail`,term:`entail`,kind:`custom`,chapter:18,text:`A legal arrangement that keeps family property intact and unsellable from generation to generation, so heirs may be 'rich' in jewels they cannot touch.`,quote:`handed down by entail, and which they cannot touch`},{id:`pianosa`,term:`Pianosa`,kind:`place`,chapter:19,text:`A low, flat island south-west of Elba in the Tuscan archipelago.`,quote:`situated twenty-five miles from Pianosa`,placeId:`pianosa`},{id:`terra-firma`,term:`terra firma`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:19,text:`Latin: 'solid ground', dry land.`,quote:`which we take for terra firma`},{id:`galvanic`,term:`galvanic`,kind:`word`,chapter:19,text:`Like an electric shock. The word comes from Luigi Galvani, whose experiments made dead frogs' legs twitch with electricity.`,quote:`The draught produced a galvanic effect`},{id:`let-the-irons-be-heated`,term:`Let the irons be heated`,kind:`custom`,chapter:19,text:`A red-hot iron pressed to the heel was a standard way to confirm death. Fear of being buried alive was widespread in the period.`,quote:`“Let the irons be heated,” said the doctor`},{id:`monomania`,term:`monomania`,kind:`word`,chapter:19,text:`A fashionable 19th-century psychiatric diagnosis: madness on a single subject in someone otherwise sane.`,quote:`the sort of malady which we call monomania`},{id:`requiem`,term:`requiem`,kind:`word`,chapter:19,text:`The Catholic Mass for the dead.`,quote:`he might have had his requiem`},{id:`guillotine`,term:`guillotine`,kind:`history`,chapter:20,text:`France's method of execution from 1792. A capital crime committed in prison, such as killing a guard, would have led to it.`,quote:`then they will guillotine me`},{id:`hand-bier`,term:`hand-bier`,kind:`word`,chapter:20,text:`A flat frame with handles at each end, used for carrying a body.`,quote:`the noise they made in putting down the hand-bier`},{id:`mistral`,term:`mistral`,kind:`word`,chapter:20,text:`The cold, violent north-west wind that roars down the Rhône valley over Provence and out to sea.`,quote:`Dantès knew that the mistral was blowing`,seeAlso:[26]},{id:`planier`,term:`Planier`,kind:`place`,chapter:21,text:`A lighthouse on a rocky islet south-west of Marseilles, which ships steered by.`,quote:`he saw the light of Planier`,placeId:`planier`},{id:`genoese-tartan`,term:`Genoese tartan`,kind:`word`,chapter:21,text:`A tartan is a small Mediterranean coasting vessel with one main mast and a lateen sail, a large triangular sail on a long slanting yard. Craft like this were favoured by traders and smugglers.`,quote:`he knew it to be a Genoese tartan`},{id:`a-vow-to-our-lady-of-the-grotto`,term:`a vow, to our Lady of the Grotto`,kind:`custom`,chapter:21,text:`Mediterranean sailors in peril often made vows to the Virgin Mary, such as leaving their hair and beard uncut for a set time, so Dantès's excuse sounds perfectly natural.`,quote:`I made a vow, to our Lady of the Grotto`},{id:`leghorn`,term:`Leghorn`,kind:`place`,chapter:21,text:`The old English name for Livorno, Tuscany's busy free port and a hub of Mediterranean trade, legal and otherwise.`,quote:`“To Leghorn.”`,seeAlso:[117],placeId:`leghorn`},{id:`fathoms`,term:`fathoms`,kind:`money`,chapter:21,text:`A fathom is six feet (about 1.8 m), so twenty fathoms is about 36 metres: a close shave past the rocks.`,quote:`You shall pass it by twenty fathoms`},{id:`patron`,term:`patron`,kind:`title`,chapter:21,text:`The usual Mediterranean term for the master or skipper of a small trading vessel.`,quote:`said the patron`,seeAlso:[32]},{id:`larboard`,term:`Larboard`,kind:`word`,chapter:21,text:`The old word for the left-hand (port) side of a ship, facing forward.`,quote:`“Larboard your helm,” cried the captain`},{id:`the-alarm-gun`,term:`the alarm gun`,kind:`custom`,chapter:21,text:`A fortress fired a cannon to announce an escape, warning the coast and boats to watch for the fugitive.`,quote:`they are firing the alarm gun`},{id:`titian`,term:`Titian`,kind:`reference`,chapter:22,text:`The great Venetian painter (c. 1488–1576), whose portraits often show grave, full-bearded men.`,quote:`one of Titian’s portraits`},{id:`lugger`,term:`lugger`,kind:`word`,chapter:22,text:`Strictly, a small boat with four-cornered lugsails. Here it is used loosely for the tartan.`,quote:`handled the lugger`},{id:`the-country-of-paoli-and-napoleon`,term:`the country of Paoli and Napoleon`,kind:`history`,chapter:22,text:`Corsica. Pasquale Paoli led the island's fight for independence from Genoa and France (1755–69), and Napoleon was born there.`,quote:`the country of Paoli and Napoleon`},{id:`culverins-shallops`,term:`culverins … shallops`,kind:`word`,chapter:22,text:`Culverins were small, long-barrelled cannon. Shallops were light open boats used for ferrying cargo to shore.`,quote:`mounted two small culverins`},{id:`a-hundred-tuscan-livres`,term:`a hundred Tuscan livres`,kind:`money`,chapter:22,text:`Tuscan lire, worth less than the franc. Eighty francs for one night's work was perhaps a labourer's wages for a month or two.`,quote:`a hundred Tuscan livres, or about eighty francs`},{id:`pain-thou-art-not-an-evil`,term:`Pain, thou art not an evil`,kind:`reference`,chapter:22,text:`Words of the Stoic philosopher Posidonius, who said this while racked by gout, as told by Cicero. The Stoics held that pain does not harm a man's virtue.`,quote:`“Pain, thou art not an evil.”`},{id:`doctor-pangloss`,term:`Doctor Pangloss`,kind:`reference`,chapter:22,text:`The relentlessly optimistic tutor in Voltaire's satire Candide (1759), who insists that all is for the best in the best of all possible worlds.`,quote:`Doctor Pangloss believed it`},{id:`piastres`,term:`piastres`,kind:`money`,chapter:22,text:`Large silver coins (Spanish dollars and their Italian equivalents) used across the Mediterranean, worth roughly five francs each. A hundred piastres is about 500 francs.`,quote:`he had amassed a hundred piastres`,seeAlso:[38]},{id:`mercury`,term:`Mercury`,kind:`reference`,chapter:22,text:`The Roman messenger god, patron of merchants, travellers and thieves alike, hence Dumas's joke about smugglers.`,quote:`Mercury, the god of merchants and robbers`},{id:`lingua-franca`,term:`lingua franca`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:22,text:`The mixed trade language of Mediterranean ports, built from Italian, Spanish, French, Arabic and Greek.`,quote:`jumbled in a lingua franca`},{id:`knots`,term:`knots`,kind:`money`,chapter:23,text:`A knot is a speed of one nautical mile (about 1.85 km) per hour, so ten knots is brisk sailing for a small vessel. 'Knots an hour' is a common slip.`,quote:`They were making nearly ten knots an hour`},{id:`kissed-his-mother-earth`,term:`kissed his mother earth`,kind:`reference`,chapter:23,text:`In Livy, an oracle said the first man to kiss his mother would rule Rome. Lucius Brutus pretended to stumble and kissed the earth, the mother of all.`,quote:`like Lucius Brutus, have “kissed his mother earth.”`},{id:`a-second-pelion`,term:`a second Pelion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:23,text:`Mount Pelion in Greece. In myth, giants piled Pelion on Mount Ossa to storm heaven. Dumas is evoking a wild, rugged mountain.`,quote:`the rocky hills of this second Pelion`},{id:`monte-pulciano`,term:`Monte Pulciano`,kind:`word`,chapter:23,text:`Montepulciano, a Tuscan red wine.`,quote:`a flask of Monte Pulciano`},{id:`nabobs`,term:`nabobs`,kind:`word`,chapter:23,text:`From an Indian title for a provincial governor. It came to mean Europeans who came home from India with vast fortunes and flaunted them.`,quote:`the insolence of nabobs`},{id:`chamois`,term:`chamois`,kind:`word`,chapter:23,text:`A nimble wild goat-antelope of the Alps, famous for leaping from rock to rock.`,quote:`with the boldness of a chamois`},{id:`open-sesame`,term:`Open Sesame`,kind:`reference`,chapter:23,text:`The magic password that opens the robbers' treasure cave in 'Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves'. Dumas confuses Ali Baba, a woodcutter, with the fisherman of another Arabian Nights tale.`,quote:`opened the splendid caverns of Ali Baba to the Arabian fisherman`},{id:`straits-of-bonifacio`,term:`Straits of Bonifacio`,kind:`place`,chapter:24,text:`The narrow channel between Corsica and Sardinia, about 11 km wide, notorious for strong winds and currents.`,quote:`just disappearing in the straits of Bonifacio`,placeId:`straits-of-bonifacio`},{id:`d-dalian-labyrinth`,term:`Dædalian labyrinth`,kind:`reference`,chapter:24,text:`In Greek myth, Daedalus built the Labyrinth for King Minos, a maze so intricate that hardly anyone who entered could find the way out. So: a bewildering puzzle.`,quote:`the Dædalian labyrinth of probabilities`},{id:`saltpetre`,term:`saltpetre`,kind:`word`,chapter:24,text:`Potassium nitrate, the key ingredient of gunpowder. A cloth soaked in it burns slowly and steadily, so it makes a simple fuse.`,quote:`rolling his handkerchief in saltpetre`},{id:`titans`,term:`Titans`,kind:`reference`,chapter:24,text:`In Greek myth, the giant elder gods (and the Giants after them) who made war on Zeus, the 'father of the gods', tearing up mountains to storm heaven.`,quote:`he seemed like one of the ancient Titans`},{id:`alaric`,term:`Alaric`,kind:`history`,chapter:24,text:`The Visigoth king who sacked Rome in 410. He was buried with his treasure in the bed of the Busento river, turned aside for the purpose, and legend says the captives who dug the grave were killed to keep the secret.`,quote:`those who buried Alaric, and were interred with the corpse`},{id:`mephitic`,term:`mephitic`,kind:`word`,chapter:24,text:`Foul-smelling and unhealthy to breathe; used of stale or poisonous air in caves, mines and sewers.`,quote:`the thick and mephitic atmosphere he had expected`},{id:`en-pale`,term:`en pale`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:24,text:`French heraldic term: set upright down the centre of the shield. The cardinal's hat above the shield marks the arms of a churchman.`,quote:`a sword, en pale, on an oval shield`},{id:`twenty-five-thousand-crowns`,term:`twenty-five thousand crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:24,text:`At Dumas's valuation of about 80 francs a coin, the coins alone come to some two million francs, when a labourer earned perhaps 500–600 francs a year. The gold bars and jewels are worth more still. Alexander VI was the Borgia pope (1492–1503).`,quote:`twenty-five thousand crowns, each worth about eighty francs`},{id:`piastre`,term:`piastre`,kind:`money`,chapter:25,text:`A large silver coin worth roughly 5 francs, used all round the Mediterranean (it began as the Spanish peso). Dumas also uses the word for the papal scudo.`,quote:`fifty piastres each`,seeAlso:[33,34,37]},{id:`a-jew-a-dealer-in-precious-stones`,term:`a Jew, a dealer in precious stones`,kind:`history`,chapter:25,text:`Leghorn (Livorno) had one of Europe's largest Jewish communities, invited in by the Medici in the 1590s and prominent in the gem, coral and banking trades. Dumas's bare label 'a Jew' reflects the casual prejudice of his era.`,quote:`the house of a Jew, a dealer in precious stones`},{id:`bill-of-health`,term:`bill of health`,kind:`custom`,chapter:25,text:`A certificate that a ship came from a port free of plague and other contagion. Port officials checked it before anyone could land; without it, a ship could be held in quarantine.`,quote:`to demand his bill of health`,seeAlso:[31]},{id:`english-passport`,term:`English passport`,kind:`custom`,chapter:25,text:`Passports were needed even for travel within France. A rich English 'milord' abroad was assumed to be wealthy and harmless, and had his government's protection, so officials usually waved him through with few questions.`,quote:`presented an English passport he had obtained from Leghorn`},{id:`canebiere`,term:`Canebière`,kind:`place`,chapter:25,text:`Marseilles's broad main street, running up from the Old Port. Its name comes from the hemp (Provençal canebe) once worked into rope there.`,quote:`as he landed on the Canebière`,seeAlso:[112],placeId:`canebiere`},{id:`double-napoleon`,term:`double Napoleon`,kind:`money`,chapter:25,text:`A 40-franc gold coin (a napoleon was 20 francs, the same as a louis). The sailor expected a 2-franc piece and was handed twenty times as much, which was probably several weeks' pay.`,quote:`you gave me a double Napoleon`},{id:`nabob`,term:`nabob`,kind:`word`,chapter:25,text:`From nawab, a governor in Mughal India. In Europe it meant a man who had come home with a huge fortune made in India and spent it lavishly.`,quote:`Some nabob from India`},{id:`seines`,term:`seines`,kind:`word`,chapter:25,text:`Large fishing nets that hang upright in the water, held up by floats and weighted at the bottom. A tender is a small boat that serves a larger one.`,quote:`two seines and a tender`},{id:`porte-d-aix`,term:`Porte d’Aix`,kind:`place`,chapter:25,text:`Marseilles's northern gate, where the road leaves for Aix and the interior, the way to the Rhône valley.`,quote:`leave Marseilles by the Porte d’Aix`},{id:`pont-du-gard`,term:`Pont du Gard`,kind:`place`,chapter:26,text:`A Roman aqueduct bridge (1st century AD) with three tiers of arches over the river Gardon near Nîmes, one of the best-preserved Roman monuments in France. The inn's sign shows it.`,quote:`a grotesque representation of the Pont du Gard`,placeId:`pont-du-gard-inn`},{id:`beaucaire`,term:`Beaucaire`,kind:`place`,chapter:26,text:`A town on the Rhône whose great July fair drew merchants from all over Europe and the Mediterranean. It was one of the biggest trade fairs of its day, with jewellers, cloth dealers and bankers.`,quote:`between the town of Beaucaire and the village of Bellegarde`,seeAlso:[27,29],placeId:`beaucaire`},{id:`a-canal-between-beaucaire-and-aiguemortes`,term:`a canal between Beaucaire and Aiguemortes`,kind:`history`,chapter:26,text:`The Beaucaire canal, finished in the early 19th century, linked the Rhône to the coast. Barges took over traffic from the road, ruining roadside inns like this one.`,quote:`a canal between Beaucaire and Aiguemortes had revolutionized transportation`},{id:`ague`,term:`ague`,kind:`word`,chapter:26,text:`Malarial fever, with recurring fits of shivering and chills. It was common around the marshes of the Camargue and Aigues-Mortes, which is the 'slow fever' wasting La Carconte.`,quote:`went into a fit of ague`},{id:`cahors`,term:`Cahors`,kind:`word`,chapter:26,text:`A dark, strong red wine from the Lot valley in south-west France, sometimes called 'black wine'.`,quote:`one of the few bottles of Cahors still remaining`},{id:`shagreen`,term:`shagreen`,kind:`word`,chapter:26,text:`Rough, grainy leather made from horse hide or the skin of sharks and rays, often dyed and used to cover small cases.`,quote:`a small box covered with black shagreen`},{id:`galleys-of-toulon`,term:`galleys of Toulon`,kind:`history`,chapter:26,text:`The bagne, the convict prison at Toulon naval port. Convicts were still said to be sent 'to the galleys', though by then they did chained hard labour in the dockyards rather than rowing ships.`,quote:`at the galleys of Toulon`},{id:`second-restoration`,term:`second restoration`,kind:`history`,chapter:26,text:`Louis XVIII's return to the throne in July 1815, after Waterloo ended the Hundred Days. The first restoration was in 1814, after Napoleon's first abdication.`,quote:`released from prison during the second restoration`,seeAlso:[27,28]},{id:`fifty-thousand-francs`,term:`fifty thousand francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:26,text:`When a labourer earned perhaps 500–600 francs a year, this was a life-changing sum. Invested at 5%, it would bring in about 2,500 francs a year: a modest, comfortable income, though not riches.`,quote:`It was estimated at fifty thousand francs.`,seeAlso:[27]},{id:`canter`,term:`canter`,kind:`word`,chapter:27,text:`Someone who talks pious cant, i.e. a religious hypocrite. Caderousse's dislike of the Jesuits, a powerful Catholic order, reflects a common anticlerical feeling of the time.`,quote:`I, who am no canter, and hate the Jesuits`},{id:`cochineal-and-indigo`,term:`cochineal and indigo`,kind:`word`,chapter:27,text:`Two valuable dyes: cochineal, a crimson made from dried insects, and indigo, a deep blue made from a plant. Both were expensive imports and made a rich cargo.`,quote:`a cargo of cochineal and indigo`,seeAlso:[30]},{id:`battle-of-ligny`,term:`battle of Ligny`,kind:`history`,chapter:27,text:`16 June 1815: Napoleon's last victory, over the Prussians, two days before Waterloo. On the eve of the campaign the real General Bourmont went over to the enemy with his staff.`,quote:`was at the battle of Ligny`},{id:`spanish-war`,term:`Spanish war`,kind:`history`,chapter:27,text:`In 1823 France invaded Spain to restore the absolute power of King Ferdinand VII. The storming of the Trocadero fort at Cádiz ended the campaign. Supplying the army (the commissariat) made contractors rich.`,quote:`after the taking of Trocadero, he was made colonel`},{id:`speculated-in-the-funds`,term:`speculated in the funds`,kind:`custom`,chapter:27,text:`Trading in government bonds (rentes) on the Paris Bourse. Their prices rose and fell with war and political news, a quick road to riches or ruin for anyone with inside knowledge.`,quote:`he speculated in the funds`,seeAlso:[34]},{id:`baron`,term:`baron`,kind:`title`,chapter:27,text:`The lowest French noble title; a count ranks two steps higher. Napoleon and the restored Bourbons both gave out titles freely, and rich financiers were often made barons, to the scorn of the old nobility.`,quote:`now he is the Baron Danglars`},{id:`greek-war-of-independence`,term:`Greek war of independence`,kind:`history`,chapter:27,text:`The Greeks' revolt against Ottoman Turkish rule (1821–1829). Supporting Greece was a Romantic fashion across Europe, and Lord Byron died there in 1824 as a volunteer.`,quote:`Greece only had risen against Turkey`},{id:`frailty-thy-name-is-woman`,term:`Frailty, thy name is woman`,kind:`reference`,chapter:27,text:`Hamlet's bitter line (Act 1, scene 2) about his mother's hasty remarriage after his father's death.`,quote:`‘Frailty, thy name is woman.’`},{id:`rue-du-helder`,term:`Rue du Helder`,kind:`place`,chapter:27,text:`A smart street in Paris just off the Boulevard des Italiens, in the fashionable new quarter of bankers, officers and society people.`,quote:`No. 27, Rue du Helder, Paris`,seeAlso:[33],placeId:`morcerf-house`},{id:`five-and-twenty-louis`,term:`five-and-twenty louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:27,text:`A louis was a 20-franc gold coin, so this purse held 500 francs. That is five times what Fernand sent by his valet, and close to a year's wages for a labourer.`,quote:`it contained five-and-twenty louis`},{id:`nankeen`,term:`nankeen`,kind:`word`,chapter:28,text:`A sturdy yellowish cotton cloth, originally from Nanjing in China, popular for summer trousers.`,quote:`nankeen trousers, and a white waistcoat`},{id:`bankruptcy`,term:`bankruptcy`,kind:`custom`,chapter:28,text:`Under French commercial law, a merchant who failed to pay a bill on its due date could be declared bankrupt (en faillite). That meant public disgrace, loss of civic rights and exclusion from the Bourse until every creditor was paid.`,quote:`It looks more like bankruptcy!`,seeAlso:[29,30]},{id:`dowry`,term:`dowry`,kind:`custom`,chapter:28,text:`Money or property a bride's family brought to her marriage. In respectable families a match was hard to make without one, and losing it could end an engagement.`,quote:`were the dowry of my daughter`,seeAlso:[30]},{id:`brokerage`,term:`brokerage`,kind:`word`,chapter:28,text:`A fee for arranging a financial deal, normally a small percentage. Boville expects to sell his doubtful debt at a heavy discount, but the Englishman pays the full face value and asks for a favour instead of a fee.`,quote:`I only ask a brokerage.`},{id:`thirty-six-pound-cannon-ball`,term:`thirty-six-pound cannon-ball`,kind:`word`,chapter:28,text:`Guns were named by the weight of shot they fired. A 36-pounder ball weighs about 16 kg, more than enough to drag a body straight to the bottom.`,quote:`after fastening a thirty-six-pound cannon-ball to their feet`},{id:`le-drapeau-blanc`,term:`Le Drapeau Blanc`,kind:`reference`,chapter:28,text:`'The White Flag', an ultra-royalist Paris newspaper named after the Bourbon white flag, the opposite of Napoleon's tricolour. It was fitting reading for an official of the restored monarchy.`,quote:`was reading Le Drapeau Blanc`},{id:`at-the-end-of-his-teeth`,term:`at the end of his teeth`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:28,text:`A literal rendering of the French rire du bout des dents: to laugh only with the teeth, i.e. a forced, mirthless laugh.`,quote:`he laughed as the English do, “at the end of his teeth.”`},{id:`sinbad-the-sailor`,term:`Sinbad the Sailor`,kind:`reference`,chapter:29,text:`Hero of a cycle of tales in the Arabian Nights: a Baghdad merchant who made seven fabulous voyages, survived shipwrecks, giants and monsters, and came home richer each time.`,quote:`a letter signed ‘Sinbad the Sailor.’`,seeAlso:[30,31,33]},{id:`cocles`,term:`Cocles`,kind:`reference`,chapter:29,text:`The clerks' nickname echoes Horatius Cocles, the legendary one-eyed Roman who held a bridge alone against an invading army. In Latin, cocles means 'one-eyed'.`,quote:`an old one-eyed cashier, called “Cocles,”`,seeAlso:[33]},{id:`fourteen-sous`,term:`fourteen sous`,kind:`money`,chapter:29,text:`A sou was 5 centimes, so twenty made a franc and fourteen sous is 70 centimes, perhaps a third of a day's pay. Cocles accounts for every last coin.`,quote:`an overbalance of fourteen sous in his cash`},{id:`belvedere`,term:`belvedere`,kind:`word`,chapter:29,text:`A lookout room or turret on a rooftop, built for the view. From Morrel's, it looks out over the harbour.`,quote:`passes a part of his time in a belvedere`},{id:`bills`,term:`bills`,kind:`custom`,chapter:29,text:`Bills of exchange were signed promises to pay a sum on a fixed date. They could be signed over ('assigned') to someone else, so a merchant's debts could be quietly bought up and presented by a stranger.`,quote:`assigned to our house by the holders`,seeAlso:[30]},{id:`287-500-francs`,term:`287,500 francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:29,text:`A huge sum: a labourer on 500–600 francs a year would need centuries to earn it. For a merchant with his credit gone, there is no hope of raising it quickly.`,quote:`in all, 287,500 francs`,seeAlso:[30]},{id:`cape-blanc-and-cape-boyador`,term:`Cape Blanc and Cape Boyador`,kind:`place`,chapter:29,text:`Headlands on the Atlantic coast of north-west Africa (in today's Mauritania and Western Sahara). They lie on the long route home from India around the Cape of Good Hope.`,quote:`between Cape Blanc and Cape Boyador`},{id:`take-in-two-reefs`,term:`take in two reefs`,kind:`word`,chapter:29,text:`To reef is to shorten a sail by tying part of it up, so the wind has less to push on. Studding-sails and the flying jib are light extra sails. 'Scudding under bare poles' means running before a storm with no sail set at all.`,quote:`Take in two reefs in the top-sails`},{id:`quid`,term:`quid`,kind:`word`,chapter:29,text:`A wad of chewing tobacco, kept in the cheek.`,quote:`Penelon rolled his quid in his cheek`},{id:`rouleau`,term:`rouleau`,kind:`word`,chapter:29,text:`A roll of coins wrapped in paper, here a hundred francs' worth in each hand.`,quote:`a rouleau of a hundred francs in either hand`,seeAlso:[50]},{id:`go-to-protest`,term:`go to protest`,kind:`custom`,chapter:30,text:`When a bill went unpaid, a notary formally recorded the refusal. This 'protest' was a public step toward a declaration of bankruptcy.`,quote:`the bills would go to protest at the end of the month`},{id:`polytechnic-school`,term:`Polytechnic School`,kind:`place`,chapter:30,text:`The École Polytechnique in Paris, founded in 1794: a highly competitive state college training engineers and artillery and military officers.`,quote:`passed brilliantly through the Polytechnic School`},{id:`the-stoic`,term:`the stoic`,kind:`reference`,chapter:30,text:`The Stoics, Greek and Roman philosophers, taught that a wise man does his duty and bears pain and misfortune without complaint. Hence the modern sense of 'stoic'.`,quote:`he thus gained the name of “the stoic.”`,seeAlso:[35]},{id:`club-of-the-phoceens`,term:`club of the Phocéens`,kind:`place`,chapter:30,text:`Marseilles was founded around 600 BC by Greeks from Phocaea in Asia Minor, so its citizens were called Phocaeans. The Sémaphore was the city's commercial newspaper, which listed ship movements.`,quote:`take his coffee at the club of the Phocéens`},{id:`stamped-paper`,term:`stamped paper`,kind:`custom`,chapter:30,text:`Paper bearing an official tax stamp, required by law for wills, contracts and other legal documents. That is why the sight frightens Madame Morrel.`,quote:`her husband was writing on stamped paper`},{id:`secretaire`,term:`secretaire`,kind:`word`,chapter:30,text:`A writing desk with drawers and a fold-down writing flap, usually lockable. It was where wills, money and private papers were kept.`,quote:`You will find my will in the secretaire in my bedroom.`,seeAlso:[37,75]},{id:`blood-washes-out-dishonor`,term:`Blood washes out dishonor`,kind:`custom`,chapter:30,text:`Bankruptcy disgraced a merchant and his whole family. Some men of Morrel's class believed that dying rather than defaulting would save the family name, since people pitied a dead debtor where they shunned a living one.`,quote:`“Blood washes out dishonor,” said Morrel.`},{id:`epaulets`,term:`epaulets`,kind:`custom`,chapter:30,text:`Fringed shoulder ornaments showing an officer's rank. By taking them off, Maximilian shows he will give up his army career to support his mother and sister.`,quote:`he took off his two epaulets, the insignia of his rank`},{id:`tower-of-saint-jean`,term:`tower of Saint-Jean`,kind:`place`,chapter:30,text:`Fort Saint-Jean, the medieval fortress guarding the north side of the entrance to Marseilles's Old Port.`,quote:`in front of the tower of Saint-Jean`,placeId:`fort-saint-jean`},{id:`carnival`,term:`Carnival`,kind:`custom`,chapter:31,text:`The days of feasting and masquerade before Ash Wednesday and the Lenten fast. Rome's Carnival was the most famous in Europe, centred on the Corso with masks, flower-throwing and horse races, and it drew visitors from everywhere.`,quote:`They had agreed to see the Carnival at Rome that year`,seeAlso:[33,34,35,36]},{id:`cicerone`,term:`cicerone`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:31,text:`Italian: a guide who shows visitors the sights. The name jokingly compares these talkative guides to the orator Cicero.`,quote:`should act as cicerone to Albert`,seeAlso:[33,34,41,53,114]},{id:`quarantine`,term:`quarantine`,kind:`custom`,chapter:31,text:`Ships that might have been in contact with disease, or with smugglers and pirates from suspect shores, were held in isolation before anyone could land. Mediterranean ports had enforced this strictly since the plague years.`,quote:`we shall have to perform quarantine for six days`,seeAlso:[84]},{id:`made-a-stiff`,term:`made a stiff`,kind:`custom`,chapter:31,text:`Slang for killing a man. Gaetano is excusing the Corsican vendetta: by island custom, avenging a wrong to one's family was a duty, so the outlaws in Corsica's maquis were often feud-killers rather than robbers.`,quote:`they are pursued for having made a stiff`},{id:`s-accommodi`,term:`S’accommodi`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:31,text:`Italian: roughly 'make yourself at home'. Dumas compares it to the mock-Turkish word in Molière's comedy Le Bourgeois gentilhomme, which supposedly packs a whole speech into a few syllables.`,quote:`S’accommodi`},{id:`raoul-in-the-huguenots`,term:`Raoul in the Huguenots`,kind:`reference`,chapter:31,text:`Meyerbeer's grand opera Les Huguenots (1836) was a Paris sensation. Its hero, Raoul, is led blindfold to a secret meeting with a mysterious lady.`,quote:`those of Raoul in the Huguenots`},{id:`arabian-nights`,term:`Arabian Nights`,kind:`reference`,chapter:31,text:`The Thousand and One Nights, a collection of Middle Eastern tales hugely popular in Europe. Franz's jokes come from it: Ali Baba's treasure cave, opened by a magic word, and Aladdin with his wonderful lamp.`,quote:`the wonders of the Arabian Nights`,seeAlso:[32]},{id:`amphitryon`,term:`Amphitryon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:31,text:`A generous host. The usage comes from Molière's comedy Amphitryon: 'the true Amphitryon is the one who gives the dinner.'`,quote:`replied the singular Amphitryon`},{id:`old-man-of-the-mountain`,term:`Old Man of the Mountain`,kind:`history`,chapter:31,text:`Hassan-i Sabbah (d. 1124), leader of the Nizari Ismaili sect that Europeans called the Assassins. Marco Polo's legend says he drugged followers to give them a taste of paradise. The word 'assassin' may come from hashishin, 'hashish-users'.`,quote:`of the Old Man of the Mountain`},{id:`monsieur-appert-and-the-man-in-the-little-blue-cloak`,term:`Monsieur Appert, and the man in the little blue cloak`,kind:`reference`,chapter:31,text:`Two well-known Paris philanthropists: Benjamin Appert, a campaigner for prison reform, and Edme Champion, nicknamed 'the little man in the blue cloak', who handed out food and clothing to the poor.`,quote:`rival Monsieur Appert, and the man in the little blue cloak`},{id:`bournous`,term:`bournous`,kind:`word`,chapter:32,text:`A burnous: a long hooded woollen cloak worn in North Africa.`,quote:`lying on his bournous in a bed of dry heather`},{id:`porto-vecchio`,term:`Porto-Vecchio`,kind:`place`,chapter:32,text:`A small port on a deep gulf on the south-east coast of Corsica, facing Sardinia.`,quote:`it disappeared in the gulf of Porto-Vecchio`,placeId:`porto-vecchio`},{id:`four-great-events`,term:`four great events`,kind:`custom`,chapter:32,text:`Rome's great festivals: Carnival before Lent, Holy Week before Easter, Corpus Christi with its early-summer processions, and the feast of St Peter on 29 June, when St Peter's dome was lit up.`,quote:`at Rome there are four great events in every year`},{id:`post-horses`,term:`post-horses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:32,text:`Horses kept at relay stations along the main roads, hired by stages to travellers and the mail. Pastrini says the few left are reserved for the official post.`,quote:`but can’t we have post-horses?`,seeAlso:[85,97]},{id:`lire`,term:`lire`,kind:`money`,chapter:32,text:`Dumas reckons Roman prices in lire, each worth roughly a franc. At 25–40 a day, hiring a carriage cost more than a labourer earned in a fortnight.`,quote:`one pays twenty-five lire for common days`},{id:`corso`,term:`Corso`,kind:`place`,chapter:33,text:`The Via del Corso, Rome's long, straight main street, running from the Piazza del Popolo to the Piazza Venezia between rows of palaces. During Carnival its windows and balconies were rented out at enormous prices.`,quote:`In the Corso.`,seeAlso:[34,35,36],placeId:`corso`},{id:`pulchinellos-or-shepherds-of-the-landes`,term:`pulchinellos or shepherds of the Landes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:33,text:`Pulcinella is the hook-nosed clown of Italian comedy (the ancestor of Punch). The shepherds of the Landes, the marshy pine country of south-west France, herded their flocks on tall stilts, hence Albert's joke about seeing Carnival on stilts.`,quote:`monster pulchinellos or shepherds of the Landes`},{id:`boulevard-de-gand`,term:`Boulevard de Gand`,kind:`place`,chapter:33,text:`The fashionable Paris promenade now called the Boulevard des Italiens, nicknamed after Ghent, where Louis XVIII's courtiers waited out the Hundred Days. The Café de Paris was the city's most exclusive restaurant.`,quote:`their walk on the Boulevard de Gand, and the Café de Paris`},{id:`the-decesaris-and-the-gasparones`,term:`the Decesaris and the Gasparones`,kind:`history`,chapter:33,text:`Real brigand chiefs of the hills south of Rome. Antonio Gasbarrone terrorized the border between the Papal States and Naples until he surrendered in 1825. Harsh papal campaigns never managed to wipe out banditry.`,quote:`the Decesaris and the Gasparones were mere children`},{id:`let-him-die-of-corneille`,term:`‘Let him die,’ of Corneille`,kind:`reference`,chapter:33,text:`In Corneille's tragedy Horace (1640), old Horace is told his son fled from three enemies. Asked what the boy should have done, he answers 'Qu'il mourût!', 'He should have died!', a famous line of Roman sternness.`,quote:`worthy the ‘Let him die,’ of Corneille`},{id:`jean-sbogar-or-lara`,term:`Jean Sbogar or Lara`,kind:`reference`,chapter:33,text:`Romantic outlaw heroes: Jean Sbogar, the gentleman-brigand of Charles Nodier's 1818 novel, and Lara, the mysterious, brooding chieftain of Byron's poem (1814).`,quote:`like Jean Sbogar or Lara`,seeAlso:[36]},{id:`sequins`,term:`sequins`,kind:`money`,chapter:33,text:`The sequin (zecchino) was a Venetian gold coin worth about 12 francs. Twenty a day is some 240 francs, nearly half a year's wages for a labourer, for one window.`,quote:`let to a Russian prince for twenty sequins a day`},{id:`roman-crowns`,term:`Roman crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:33,text:`The papal scudo, worth about 5.4 francs, the same coin Dumas elsewhere calls a Roman piastre. Five hundred of them was a fortune to two orphaned farmhands.`,quote:`Five hundred Roman crowns are three thousand lire`,seeAlso:[34,36,37]},{id:`parbleu`,term:`Parbleu!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:33,text:`French: a mild oath, roughly 'by Jove!', a softened par Dieu. Pardieu, morbleu, diable and peste ('plague!') are similar exclamations.`,quote:`Parbleu!`,seeAlso:[34,35,87]},{id:`colosseum`,term:`Colosseum`,kind:`place`,chapter:33,text:`The Flavian Amphitheatre, opened in AD 80, where gladiators fought before some 50,000 spectators. Seeing its ruins by moonlight was an essential experience for Romantic-era travellers.`,quote:`show Albert the Colosseum by moonlight`,seeAlso:[34],placeId:`colosseum`},{id:`transtevere`,term:`Transtevere`,kind:`place`,chapter:34,text:`Trastevere, the old working-class quarter 'across the Tiber'. Its people claimed to be the truest Romans and wore a distinctive costume.`,quote:`wore the Transtevere costume`,seeAlso:[36],placeId:`trastevere`},{id:`castle-of-st-angelo`,term:`Castle of St. Angelo`,kind:`place`,chapter:34,text:`The Emperor Hadrian's huge round tomb beside the Tiber, later turned into a papal fortress and prison.`,quote:`I came here direct from the Castle of St. Angelo`,seeAlso:[36],placeId:`castle-st-angelo`},{id:`mazzolato`,term:`mazzolato`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:34,text:`Italian: executed with a mallet (mazza), a punishment the Papal States reserved for the most savage murderers. Decapitato simply means beheaded.`,quote:`One of the culprits will be mazzolato`,seeAlso:[35]},{id:`parisina`,term:`Parisina`,kind:`reference`,chapter:34,text:`Donizetti's 1833 opera, based on Byron's poem: Duke Azzo learns that his wife Parisina loves Ugo when she murmurs her secret in her sleep. Donizetti also wrote Lucia di Lammermoor.`,quote:`The opera of Parisina was announced`},{id:`viscount`,term:`viscount`,kind:`title`,chapter:34,text:`The rank between baron and count, often used by a count's eldest son as a courtesy title. A title 'dated merely from 1815' was new nobility, unlike old families going back to the Middle Ages.`,quote:`he was a viscount—a recently created one, certainly`},{id:`italian-theatre-boxes`,term:`Italian theatre boxes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:34,text:`In Italian theatres a box was a private drawing room: people chatted, received visitors and listened to the opera only in snatches. A new visitor took the seat of honour and the earlier guest gave way.`,quote:`in obedience to the Italian custom, instantly rose`,seeAlso:[36]},{id:`lord-ruthven`,term:`Lord Ruthven`,kind:`reference`,chapter:34,text:`The pale, fascinating aristocrat-vampire of John Polidori's tale The Vampyre (1819). He was modelled on Byron, and the story was at first published as Byron's own. It was endlessly adapted on the Paris stage.`,quote:`no other than Lord Ruthven himself in a living form`,seeAlso:[36]},{id:`guzla`,term:`guzla`,kind:`word`,chapter:34,text:`A one-stringed bowed instrument from the Balkans, traditionally played to accompany sung epics.`,quote:`the sound of a guzla reached the ears`,seeAlso:[36,77]},{id:`mandaia`,term:`mandaïa`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:35,text:`Italian (mannaia): the Papal States' version of the guillotine. Its crescent-shaped blade drops from a lower height than the French one.`,quote:`the curved knife of the mandaïa`},{id:`the-count-of-chalais`,term:`the Count of Chalais`,kind:`history`,chapter:35,text:`Henri de Talleyrand, Comte de Chalais, was beheaded in 1626 for plotting against Cardinal Richelieu. His friends spirited the executioner away, and the untrained substitute needed some thirty strokes.`,quote:`the soldier who beheaded the Count of Chalais`},{id:`duelling`,term:`duelling`,kind:`custom`,chapter:35,text:`Duels were illegal in France, but juries rarely convicted, and a gentleman who refused a challenge lost his honour. Insults among the upper classes were still settled with swords or pistols.`,quote:`it is to punish them that duelling is tolerated`},{id:`an-eye-for-an-eye`,term:`an eye for an eye`,kind:`reference`,chapter:35,text:`The law of retaliation, found in the Bible (Exodus 21:24) and in older codes such as Hammurabi's. The count credits it to the peoples of the East.`,quote:`an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth`},{id:`torn-to-pieces-by-horses-or-broken-on-the-wheel`,term:`torn to pieces by horses or broken on the wheel`,kind:`history`,chapter:35,text:`Executions under the old French monarchy: quartering by horses for regicides, and the wheel, which broke the victim's limbs, for many murderers. In 1792 the Revolution replaced them with the guillotine, a quick death meant to be the same for everyone.`,quote:`torn to pieces by horses or broken on the wheel`},{id:`al-suo-commodo`,term:`Al suo commodo!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:35,text:`Italian: 'At your convenience!' In other words, breakfast is served.`,quote:`Al suo commodo!`},{id:`castaing`,term:`Castaing`,kind:`history`,chapter:35,text:`Edme-Samuel Castaing, a young Paris doctor guillotined in 1823 for poisoning a friend with morphine, after a sensational trial.`,quote:`I saw Castaing executed`},{id:`confetti`,term:`confetti`,kind:`custom`,chapter:35,text:`Carnival confetti were not paper. They were small sugared sweets or, more often, cheap pellets of plaster and flour, flung by the handful at everyone in sight. Pale costumes hid the white dust.`,quote:`on account of the confetti (sweetmeats)`,seeAlso:[36]},{id:`piazza-del-popolo`,term:`Piazza del Popolo`,kind:`place`,chapter:35,text:`The great oval square inside Rome's northern gate, with an ancient Egyptian obelisk at its centre. It was the usual place for public executions, and the Corso runs from it.`,quote:`the spectacle that the Piazza del Popolo presented`,seeAlso:[36],placeId:`piazza-del-popolo`},{id:`brotherhood-of-penitents`,term:`brotherhood of penitents`,kind:`custom`,chapter:35,text:`Lay religious confraternities whose members wore hoods to stay anonymous. They comforted condemned prisoners, walked with them to the scaffold, and buried them.`,quote:`A brotherhood of penitents, clothed from head to foot`},{id:`domino`,term:`domino`,kind:`word`,chapter:36,text:`A loose hooded cloak, usually worn with a half-mask: the standard disguise at masked balls and carnivals.`,quote:`was a blue domino`},{id:`callot-s-temptation-of-st-anthony`,term:`Callot’s Temptation of St. Anthony`,kind:`reference`,chapter:36,text:`A crowded etching by Jacques Callot (1635) showing the hermit saint besieged by swarms of grotesque demons.`,quote:`as in Callot’s Temptation of St. Anthony`},{id:`the-bear-and-the-pasha`,term:`The Bear and the Pasha`,kind:`reference`,chapter:36,text:`A popular Paris farce by Eugène Scribe (1820) in which a man is dressed up in a bear's skin. Odry was its star comic actor.`,quote:`exactly resembling Odry’s in The Bear and the Pasha`},{id:`calash`,term:`calash`,kind:`word`,chapter:36,text:`A light carriage with a folding hood.`,quote:`that calash filled with Roman peasants`,seeAlso:[46,47]},{id:`m-de-rothschild`,term:`M. de Rothschild`,kind:`history`,chapter:36,text:`James de Rothschild, head of the family bank in Paris and reputedly the richest man in France: a byword for wealth.`,quote:`being confounded with M. de Rothschild`},{id:`bajocco`,term:`bajocco`,kind:`money`,chapter:36,text:`A small papal copper coin, a hundredth of a scudo. 'Not a bajocco' means not a penny.`,quote:`It does not bring him a bajocco.`},{id:`didier-or-anthony`,term:`Didier or Anthony`,kind:`reference`,chapter:36,text:`Brooding heroes of Romantic drama: Didier in Victor Hugo's Marion de Lorme and Antony in Dumas's own play Antony (both 1831). Dumas is slyly quoting himself.`,quote:`two or three exits worthy of Didier or Anthony`},{id:`gregory-xvi`,term:`Gregory XVI`,kind:`history`,chapter:36,text:`Pope from 1831 to 1846, head of the Church and also ruler of the Papal States. He was a firm conservative, famously suspicious of railways and revolutions alike.`,quote:`the venerable and noble old man called Gregory XVI`},{id:`byronic-hero`,term:`Byronic hero`,kind:`reference`,chapter:36,text:`The type Lord Byron made famous: proud, pale, magnetic, and haunted by some secret guilt or sorrow. Manfred and Lara are two of his heroes in this mould.`,quote:`Truly, a Byronic hero!`},{id:`barberi`,term:`barberi`,kind:`custom`,chapter:36,text:`Riderless horses (named after the Barbary breed) raced the length of the Corso each Carnival afternoon, driven on by the crowd and by spiked weights hung on their sides.`,quote:`clear it for the barberi`},{id:`the-colonnas`,term:`the Colonnas`,kind:`history`,chapter:37,text:`One of Rome's oldest princely families, powerful since the Middle Ages, with a pope and many cardinals in its line.`,quote:`one of the last heiresses of the Colonnas`},{id:`torlonia`,term:`Torlonia`,kind:`history`,chapter:37,text:`The Torlonias were Rome's great banking house, the bankers of most foreign visitors. Their fortune bought them princely titles, including the dukedom of Bracciano.`,quote:`Run to Torlonia, draw from him instantly four thousand piastres`},{id:`letter-of-credit`,term:`letter of credit`,kind:`custom`,chapter:37,text:`A letter from a traveller's home bank asking bankers abroad to pay him cash up to a stated limit: the traveller's cheque of its day.`,quote:`take the letter of credit from my pocket-book`,seeAlso:[46,53]},{id:`galop`,term:`galop`,kind:`word`,chapter:37,text:`A fast, breathless ballroom dance with a hopping step, the craze of the 1830s.`,quote:`I was dancing the galop at Torlonia’s`},{id:`appian-way`,term:`Appian Way`,kind:`place`,chapter:37,text:`The Via Appia, ancient Rome's great road south, begun in 312 BC. Roman law banned burial inside the city, so tombs lined the road for miles.`,quote:`the ancient Appian Way, and bordered with tombs`,placeId:`appian-way`},{id:`campagna`,term:`Campagna`,kind:`place`,chapter:37,text:`The Roman Campagna: the empty, rolling plain around Rome, malarial and thinly populated, the country of shepherds and bandits.`,quote:`the irregular and broken surface of the Campagna`,placeId:`campagna`},{id:`catacombs-of-st-sebastian`,term:`catacombs of St. Sebastian`,kind:`place`,chapter:37,text:`Underground galleries on the Appian Way where early Christians buried their dead in niches cut into the walls, row above row. A columbarium is a chamber lined with such niches.`,quote:`waiting for him in the catacombs of St. Sebastian`,placeId:`catacombs-st-sebastian`},{id:`c-sar-s-commentaries`,term:`Cæsar’s Commentaries`,kind:`reference`,chapter:37,text:`Julius Caesar's own brisk accounts of his wars in Gaul and his civil war, classic reading for would-be commanders.`,quote:`Cæsar’s Commentaries`},{id:`never-awaken-me-but-for-bad-news`,term:`Never awaken me but for bad news`,kind:`reference`,chapter:37,text:`A maxim attributed to Napoleon: good news can wait until morning, while bad news needs action at once.`,quote:`Never awaken me but for bad news`},{id:`salite`,term:`Salite!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:37,text:`Italian: 'Come up!'`,quote:`Salite!`},{id:`aguado-and-rothschild`,term:`Aguado and Rothschild`,kind:`history`,chapter:38,text:`Alejandro Aguado and James de Rothschild were the two most famous financiers in 1830s Paris, bywords for colossal wealth and stock-market speculation.`,quote:`the speculations of M. Aguado and M. Rothschild`},{id:`a-huron-or-a-native-of-cochin-china`,term:`a Huron or a native of Cochin-China`,kind:`reference`,chapter:38,text:`Stock examples of the utterly foreign: the Huron were a Native American people the French knew from Canada; Cochin-China is southern Vietnam.`,quote:`no more than a Huron or a native of Cochin-China`},{id:`the-monthyon-prize`,term:`the Monthyon prize`,kind:`history`,chapter:38,text:`Prizes for virtue founded by the philanthropist Baron de Montyon and awarded each year by the French Academy to poor people of exemplary goodness. Albert is joking.`,quote:`to compete for the Monthyon prize`,seeAlso:[40,52]},{id:`the-bandits-of-colomba`,term:`the bandits of Colomba`,kind:`reference`,chapter:38,text:`Corsican 'bandits' were usually men hiding in the hills after a vendetta killing, and Romantic writers admired them. The nod is to Mérimée's Corsican tale Colomba (strictly, published a couple of years after this scene).`,quote:`the bandits of Colomba`},{id:`tablets`,term:`tablets`,kind:`word`,chapter:38,text:`A pocket notebook of ivory or paper leaves for jotting memoranda.`,quote:`taking out his tablets`},{id:`pavilion`,term:`pavilion`,kind:`word`,chapter:38,text:`A separate small building within the grounds of a large house. Albert's gives him a bachelor's independence while living on his parents' property.`,quote:`occupy a pavilion at the farther side of the courtyard`},{id:`concierge`,term:`concierge`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:39,text:`French: the doorkeeper who lives in a lodge at the entrance of a Paris house and watches everyone who comes and goes.`,quote:`close to the lodge of the concierge`,seeAlso:[42,43,46]},{id:`the-sesame-of-ali-baba`,term:`the “Sesame” of Ali Baba`,kind:`reference`,chapter:39,text:`From the Arabian Nights: the robbers' cave in 'Ali Baba and the Forty Thieves' opens only to the password 'Open, Sesame!' Albert's private door opens to a secret knock.`,quote:`at the “Sesame” of Ali Baba`},{id:`fencing-boxing-and-single-stick`,term:`fencing, boxing, and single-stick`,kind:`custom`,chapter:39,text:`The three manly arts of a fashionable 1830s dandy. Single-stick is fencing with a wooden stick; Grisier was the most celebrated Paris fencing master.`,quote:`fencing, boxing, and single-stick`},{id:`chibouques-and-narghiles`,term:`chibouques and narghiles`,kind:`word`,chapter:39,text:`Turkish pipes: the chibouque has a long straight stem; the narghile (hookah) draws smoke through perfumed water. Oriental smoking was the height of fashion.`,quote:`a collection of German pipes, of chibouques`},{id:`don-carlos`,term:`Don Carlos`,kind:`history`,chapter:39,text:`Pretender to the Spanish throne who waged civil war (the First Carlist War, 1833–40) against the child queen Isabella II, whom France supported. Debray jokes about interning him at Bourges.`,quote:`you drive Don Carlos out of Spain`},{id:`the-order-of-charles-iii`,term:`the order of Charles III`,kind:`title`,chapter:39,text:`A Spanish order of knighthood, given here for Debray's work on Spanish affairs; its ribbon is blue. Beauchamp's quip about sowing red and reaping blue plays on the red of revolution and the ribbon's colour.`,quote:`they sent me the order of Charles III.`,seeAlso:[41]},{id:`the-bourse`,term:`the Bourse`,kind:`place`,chapter:39,text:`The Paris stock exchange. News that reached it early could make a speculator a fortune overnight.`,quote:`it had already transpired on the Bourse`,seeAlso:[54],placeId:`bourse`},{id:`the-chamber`,term:`the Chamber`,kind:`history`,chapter:39,text:`The Chamber of Deputies, the elected lower house of parliament under King Louis-Philippe. Deputies like Danglars and journalists like Beauchamp hurry to its sittings.`,quote:`I must go to the Chamber`,seeAlso:[40,46,78,84,86]},{id:`the-bois-de-boulogne`,term:`the Bois de Boulogne`,kind:`place`,chapter:39,text:`The great wooded park on the western edge of Paris, where fashionable people rode and drove to be seen.`,quote:`At the Bois de Boulogne`,placeId:`bois-de-boulogne`},{id:`peste`,term:`Peste!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:39,text:`French: literally 'Plague!', a mild exclamation like 'Hang it!' or 'The deuce!'`,quote:`Peste!`,seeAlso:[42,43]},{id:`peer-of-france-the-luxembourg`,term:`peer of France / the Luxembourg`,kind:`title`,chapter:40,text:`Peers sat for life in the Chamber of Peers, the upper house of parliament, which met in the Luxembourg Palace. Beauchamp jokes about Danglars being 'promoted' there.`,quote:`send him to speak at the Luxembourg`,seeAlso:[41]},{id:`mesalliance`,term:`mésalliance`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:40,text:`French: a marriage beneath one's rank. An old noble family marrying a newly made baron's daughter, however rich, would raise eyebrows.`,quote:`to a mésalliance`},{id:`two-millions`,term:`two millions`,kind:`money`,chapter:40,text:`A dowry of two million francs, invested at the usual 5 per cent, would bring about 100,000 francs a year: a princely income when a labourer earned perhaps 500 to 600 francs a year.`,quote:`I give my daughter two millions`},{id:`martlets`,term:`martlets`,kind:`word`,chapter:40,text:`In heraldry, a martlet is a stylised swallow-like bird with no feet. Debray jokes that Albert could share his seven with a bride and still have plenty.`,quote:`You have seven martlets on your arms`,seeAlso:[41]},{id:`captain-of-spahis`,term:`captain of Spahis`,kind:`title`,chapter:40,text:`The Spahis were light cavalry regiments raised in French-ruled Algeria, mostly North African troopers under French officers, with a half-French, half-Oriental uniform.`,quote:`captain of Spahis`},{id:`the-siege-of-constantine`,term:`the siege of Constantine`,kind:`history`,chapter:40,text:`France's first attempt to take the Algerian city of Constantine (November 1836) failed, followed by a terrible retreat in freezing rain. The city fell the next year.`,quote:`witness the raising of the siege`},{id:`a-slave-becomes-free-in-france`,term:`a slave becomes free in France`,kind:`custom`,chapter:40,text:`An old principle of French law: any slave who set foot on French soil was free. The Count's answer is that his slave speaks only Romaic (modern Greek), so who will tell her?`,quote:`the moment she puts her foot in France your slave becomes free`,seeAlso:[49]},{id:`the-legion-of-honor`,term:`the Legion of Honor`,kind:`title`,chapter:40,text:`France's highest order of merit, founded by Napoleon. Knights wear a red ribbon in the buttonhole; officers and commanders wear rosettes or neck ribbons.`,quote:`decorated with the order of the Legion of Honor`,seeAlso:[41,46,51]},{id:`faubourg-saint-germain-chaussee-d-antin`,term:`Faubourg Saint-Germain / Chaussée d’Antin`,kind:`place`,chapter:40,text:`The two rival smart districts of Paris: the Faubourg Saint-Germain, home of the old aristocracy, and the newer Chaussée d'Antin, home of bankers and new money.`,quote:`you only know your dull and gloomy Faubourg Saint-Germain`,seeAlso:[42,50],placeId:`danglars-house`},{id:`the-politeness-of-kings`,term:`the politeness of kings`,kind:`reference`,chapter:40,text:`'Punctuality is the politeness of kings' is a saying attributed to King Louis XVIII.`,quote:`is the politeness of kings, according to one of your sovereigns`},{id:`leopold-robert`,term:`Léopold Robert`,kind:`reference`,chapter:41,text:`A Swiss painter (1794–1835), hugely popular for idealised scenes of Italian peasants. A portrait by him was a costly treasure; Albert's salon is likewise full of the fashionable modern painters of the 1830s.`,quote:`A single portrait, signed by Léopold Robert`},{id:`a-commandery-of-st-stephen`,term:`a commandery of St. Stephen`,kind:`title`,chapter:41,text:`The Tuscan Order of St. Stephen allowed wealthy men to endow a 'commandery', which brought a title with it. The Count admits his title was bought this way.`,quote:`a commandery of St. Stephen`},{id:`d-hozier`,term:`d’Hozier`,kind:`reference`,chapter:41,text:`The d'Hozier family were royal genealogists for generations, the official judges of whether a pedigree was truly noble.`,quote:`would have greatly edified d’Hozier and Jaucourt`},{id:`marshal-bourmont`,term:`Marshal Bourmont`,kind:`history`,chapter:41,text:`Commander of the French expedition that captured Algiers in 1830, the start of France's conquest of Algeria.`,quote:`under the orders of Marshal Bourmont`},{id:`the-elder-branch-the-revolution-of-july`,term:`the elder branch / the Revolution of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:41,text:`In July 1830 three days of street fighting overthrew Charles X, of the elder Bourbon line, and put his cousin Louis-Philippe of the younger Orléans branch on the throne. Officers who owed their careers to the Bourbons often lost favour.`,quote:`had the elder branch remained on the throne`,seeAlso:[48]},{id:`manfred-lara-werner`,term:`Manfred, Lara, Werner`,kind:`reference`,chapter:41,text:`Brooding, guilt-haunted heroes of Lord Byron's poems and plays: proud aristocratic outcasts with dark secrets, wildly fashionable in the 1830s.`,quote:`some Manfred, some Lara, some Werner`,seeAlso:[54]},{id:`seven-hundred-guineas`,term:`seven hundred guineas`,kind:`money`,chapter:41,text:`The guinea was a British gold coin worth 21 shillings, about 26 francs, so roughly 18,000 francs for a carriage and horses.`,quote:`refused on the previous day seven hundred guineas`},{id:`volatile-salts`,term:`volatile salts`,kind:`word`,chapter:41,text:`Smelling salts: a pungent ammonia preparation sniffed from a little bottle to revive someone who feels faint.`,quote:`the sharp and fragrant odor of volatile salts`},{id:`the-champs-elysees`,term:`the Champs-Élysées`,kind:`place`,chapter:42,text:`In 1838 the avenue was grand but still semi-rural, running out to the edge of Paris between gardens and scattered mansions.`,quote:`as you ascend the Champs-Élysées`,placeId:`count-house-champs-elysees`},{id:`fifty-five-thousand-francs`,term:`fifty-five thousand francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:42,text:`The price of the country house, plus a generous tip for the notary. It was roughly a century of a labourer's wages; the Count's horses cost 20,000 francs each.`,quote:`give fifty-five thousand francs to monsieur`},{id:`notary`,term:`notary`,kind:`title`,chapter:42,text:`In France, a public official who drafts and certifies contracts, wills and property sales.`,quote:`the notary empowered to sell the country house`},{id:`the-palais-royal`,term:`the Palais Royal`,kind:`place`,chapter:42,text:`A palace in central Paris whose arcaded gardens were crowded with shops, jewellers, cafés and gaming houses.`,quote:`the best engraver of the Palais Royal`,seeAlso:[44]},{id:`i-have-been-almost-obliged-to-wait`,term:`“I have been almost obliged to wait”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:42,text:`Louis XIV's famous icy rebuke ('J'ai failli attendre') to a courtier whose carriage was not instantly ready.`,quote:`I have been almost obliged to wait`},{id:`signed-himself-in-the-corsican-manner`,term:`signed himself in the Corsican manner`,kind:`custom`,chapter:43,text:`Making the sign of the cross in the air with the thumb, a folk gesture against evil.`,quote:`signed himself in the Corsican manner`},{id:`maquis`,term:`maquis`,kind:`word`,chapter:43,text:`The dense scrubland covering Corsica's hills, where outlaws hid. Sartène and Corte, also named here, were Corsican towns famous for feuds.`,quote:`This is not a Corsican maquis but an English garden`},{id:`vendetta`,term:`vendetta`,kind:`custom`,chapter:43,text:`The Corsican blood feud: a family's duty to avenge a murdered relative by killing the culprit or one of his kin. It was often formally declared, and could last generations.`,quote:`brooding over some old history of the vendetta`,seeAlso:[44]},{id:`chartreux-or-trappist`,term:`Chartreux or Trappist`,kind:`word`,chapter:43,text:`Monks of two very strict orders, the Carthusians and the Trappists, known for silence and penance.`,quote:`Turn Chartreux or Trappist`},{id:`the-seal-of-confession`,term:`the seal of confession`,kind:`custom`,chapter:43,text:`A Catholic priest may never reveal anything told to him in confession, whatever the crime.`,quote:`only related under the seal of confession`,seeAlso:[45]},{id:`lucia`,term:`Lucia`,kind:`reference`,chapter:43,text:`Donizetti's opera Lucia di Lammermoor (1835), the great hit of the day.`,quote:`humming an air from Lucia`},{id:`the-army-beyond-the-loire`,term:`the army beyond the Loire`,kind:`history`,chapter:44,text:`After Waterloo the defeated imperial army withdrew south of the River Loire and was disbanded; its soldiers made their way home through a hostile country.`,quote:`retired with the army beyond the Loire`},{id:`the-massacres-of-1815`,term:`the massacres of 1815`,kind:`history`,chapter:44,text:`The 'White Terror': in summer 1815 royalist gangs in southern France, worst of all around Nîmes, murdered suspected Bonapartists and Protestants. Trestaillon was a real leader of these gangs.`,quote:`Three brigands, called Trestaillon, Truphemy, and Graffan`},{id:`cap-corse`,term:`Cap Corse`,kind:`place`,chapter:44,text:`The long, narrow peninsula at Corsica's northern tip; Rogliano is one of its villages.`,quote:`the little village of Rogliano, at the extremity of Cap Corse`,placeId:`rogliano`},{id:`the-foundling-asylum`,term:`the foundling asylum`,kind:`custom`,chapter:44,text:`The Paris foundling hospital, where unwanted babies could be left anonymously. A parent who kept a token, such as half of a cloth, could later reclaim the child.`,quote:`an asylum where they receive such creatures`},{id:`a-baron-s-coronet`,term:`a baron’s coronet`,kind:`word`,chapter:44,text:`A small stylised crown drawn above initials or arms, its design showing the owner's rank.`,quote:`surmounted by a baron’s coronet`},{id:`the-beaucaire-fair`,term:`the Beaucaire fair`,kind:`custom`,chapter:44,text:`A great trade fair held each July at Beaucaire on the Rhône, drawing merchants from all over Europe and the Mediterranean.`,quote:`the fair at Beaucaire`},{id:`2-500-livres-of-income`,term:`2,500 livres of income`,kind:`money`,chapter:44,text:`Invested at about 5 per cent, 45,000 francs would bring in some 2,250 to 2,500 francs a year for life: modest comfort, several times a labourer's earnings.`,quote:`that is, 2,500 livres of income`},{id:`crowns-of-six-livres`,term:`crowns of six livres`,kind:`money`,chapter:44,text:`Old pre-Revolution silver coins (écus) worth six livres, roughly six francs: here, the couple's entire savings.`,quote:`two or three crowns of six livres each`},{id:`the-mistral`,term:`the mistral`,kind:`word`,chapter:45,text:`The violent, cold north wind that roars down the Rhône valley, often for days at a time.`,quote:`It’s the mistral`},{id:`assizes`,term:`assizes`,kind:`word`,chapter:45,text:`The Cour d'assises, the court that tried serious crimes before a jury in periodic sessions.`,quote:`at the approaching assizes`,seeAlso:[99,108,109,110]},{id:`the-language-in-which-homer-makes-his-gods-converse`,term:`the language in which Homer makes his gods converse`,kind:`reference`,chapter:45,text:`That is, Greek: Homer's Iliad and Odyssey are written in ancient Greek.`,quote:`that sonorous language in which Homer makes his gods converse`},{id:`flambeau`,term:`flambeau`,kind:`word`,chapter:45,text:`A large torch or candlestick.`,quote:`a rose-colored flambeau`},{id:`lorgnette`,term:`lorgnette`,kind:`word`,chapter:46,text:`A pair of eyeglasses or opera glasses held up on a handle.`,quote:`by means of an excellent lorgnette`},{id:`six-millions`,term:`six millions`,kind:`money`,chapter:46,text:`Six million francs to be spent in one year. For scale: the Count pays his valet 1,500 francs a year, and a family could live comfortably on 25,000 a year. Orders on the treasury were government bonds payable to whoever held them.`,quote:`suppose we say six millions`},{id:`1-500-francs-per-annum`,term:`1,500 francs per annum`,kind:`money`,chapter:46,text:`A valet's wage, plus lodging, food and perks. As the Count says, it was more than many junior officers earned.`,quote:`You receive 1,500 francs per annum`},{id:`montmorency-and-lafayette`,term:`Montmorency and Lafayette`,kind:`history`,chapter:46,text:`In 1790 the revolutionary Assembly abolished hereditary titles; liberal nobles such as Lafayette and Mathieu de Montmorency backed the measure. The Count is needling a man who loves his new barony.`,quote:`the example set you by Messrs. de Montmorency and Lafayette`},{id:`rothschild-baring-lafitte`,term:`Rothschild, Baring, Lafitte`,kind:`history`,chapter:46,text:`The giants of European banking: Baron James de Rothschild in Paris, the Barings of London, and Jacques Laffitte, a Paris banker who had also been prime minister.`,quote:`drawn by Baring of London, upon M. Lafitte`},{id:`corvette`,term:`corvette`,kind:`word`,chapter:46,text:`A small, fast three-masted warship.`,quote:`into which my corvette can enter`},{id:`relays-of-horses`,term:`relays of horses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:46,text:`Fresh horses kept ready at intervals along a road so that a traveller could change teams and keep going at full speed.`,quote:`constant relays of horses at ten leagues apart`},{id:`boucher`,term:`Boucher`,kind:`reference`,chapter:47,text:`François Boucher, 18th-century painter of frothy pastoral scenes with pretty shepherds and shepherdesses.`,quote:`after the style and manner of Boucher`},{id:`the-directory`,term:`the Directory`,kind:`history`,chapter:47,text:`The government of France from 1795 to 1799. Its taste was a showy imitation of Greek and Roman antiquity.`,quote:`as it was understood during the time of the Directory`},{id:`the-races-at-the-champ-de-mars`,term:`the races at the Champ-de-Mars`,kind:`custom`,chapter:47,text:`Fashionable horse races were held on the Champ-de-Mars parade ground in Paris and at Satory near Versailles.`,quote:`the indifferent races at the Champ-de-Mars and Satory`,seeAlso:[53]},{id:`the-italian-opera-is-in-london`,term:`the Italian opera is in London`,kind:`custom`,chapter:47,text:`The fashionable Paris season ran through winter and spring. In summer the Théâtre-Italien's star singers left to perform in London.`,quote:`the Italian opera is in London`},{id:`ranelagh`,term:`Ranelagh`,kind:`place`,chapter:47,text:`A popular park and open-air ballroom at Passy, on the edge of the Bois de Boulogne.`,quote:`Your horses got as far as Ranelagh`,placeId:`ranelagh`},{id:`the-faubourg-saint-honore`,term:`the Faubourg Saint-Honoré`,kind:`place`,chapter:47,text:`An elegant quarter of grand town houses with large gardens on the right bank, west of the Tuileries.`,quote:`her residence in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré`,seeAlso:[51],placeId:`villefort-house-paris`},{id:`the-jockey-club`,term:`the Jockey Club`,kind:`custom`,chapter:47,text:`Founded in Paris in 1834 to promote horse racing, it quickly became the most exclusive gentlemen's club in the city.`,quote:`recounted it at the Jockey Club`,seeAlso:[53]},{id:`doctrinaire`,term:`doctrinaire`,kind:`history`,chapter:48,text:`The Doctrinaires were moderate liberal politicians (Guizot was the best known) who championed constitutional monarchy under Louis-Philippe.`,quote:`whether the government was doctrinaire liberal, or conservative`},{id:`four-successive-revolutions`,term:`four successive revolutions`,kind:`history`,chapter:48,text:`For Villefort's generation: the Bourbon Restoration of 1814, Napoleon's return in 1815, the second Restoration after Waterloo, and the July Revolution of 1830. He has prospered under every regime.`,quote:`Four successive revolutions had built and cemented the pedestal`},{id:`pede-claudo`,term:`pede claudo`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:48,text:`Latin, from Horace: punishment follows the criminal 'with limping foot', slowly but surely.`,quote:`it is the pede claudo of the ancients`},{id:`the-law-of-retaliation`,term:`the law of retaliation`,kind:`custom`,chapter:48,text:`The lex talionis, 'an eye for an eye': punishment that exactly matches the injury.`,quote:`that is, the law of retaliation`},{id:`tobias-and-attila`,term:`Tobias and Attila`,kind:`reference`,chapter:48,text:`In the Book of Tobit, young Tobias travels with the archangel Raphael without knowing he is an angel. Attila the Hun was called 'the Scourge of God'.`,quote:`Tobias took the angel who restored him to light`},{id:`taken-by-satan-into-the-highest-mountain`,term:`taken by Satan into the highest mountain`,kind:`reference`,chapter:48,text:`From the temptation of Christ in the Gospels: the devil shows Jesus all the kingdoms of the world and offers them in exchange for worship.`,quote:`taken by Satan into the highest mountain in the earth`},{id:`non-bis-in-idem`,term:`non bis in idem`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:48,text:`Latin legal maxim: 'not twice for the same thing', meaning no one may be tried twice for one offence. The Count jokes that, having been nearly mad once, he cannot be again.`,quote:`you know the axiom,—non bis in idem`},{id:`apoplexy`,term:`apoplexy`,kind:`word`,chapter:48,text:`The old word for a stroke: sudden bleeding or blockage in the brain that can leave a person paralysed.`,quote:`there is apoplexy—that lightning-stroke`,seeAlso:[52,79]},{id:`ariel-and-caliban`,term:`Ariel and Caliban`,kind:`reference`,chapter:48,text:`From Shakespeare's The Tempest: Ariel is an airy, almost angelic spirit; Caliban a brutish, earthbound creature.`,quote:`like Ariel, verge on the angelic`},{id:`jacobin-carbonaro`,term:`Jacobin, Carbonaro`,kind:`history`,chapter:48,text:`The Jacobins were the most radical club of the French Revolution. The Carbonari were secret revolutionary societies, first Italian, then French in the 1820s, plotting against the Bourbon kings.`,quote:`the old Jacobin, the old senator, the old Carbonaro`},{id:`epirus`,term:`Epirus`,kind:`place`,chapter:49,text:`A mountainous region of north-western Greece and southern Albania, then part of the Ottoman Empire, with the Pindus range at its back.`,quote:`that of the women of Epirus`,placeId:`epirus`},{id:`parian-marble`,term:`Parian marble`,kind:`word`,chapter:49,text:`Flawless white marble from the Greek island of Paros, prized by ancient sculptors.`,quote:`taken for Parian marble`},{id:`pindar`,term:`Pindar`,kind:`reference`,chapter:49,text:`The great ancient Greek lyric poet (5th century BC), whose odes celebrate youth, victory and the gods.`,quote:`these lines of Pindar`},{id:`the-marais`,term:`the Marais`,kind:`place`,chapter:50,text:`An old quarter of Paris once full of noble mansions, by now unfashionable and given over to merchants and the middle classes. Maximilian is teasing his sister.`,quote:`what a fashionable woman of the Marais is`},{id:`la-presse-and-les-debats`,term:`la Presse and les Débats`,kind:`history`,chapter:50,text:`Two Paris dailies: La Presse (1836), the first cheap mass-circulation paper, and the respectable, pro-government Journal des Débats.`,quote:`la Presse and les Débats`},{id:`the-polytechnic-school`,term:`the Polytechnic School`,kind:`title`,chapter:50,text:`The École Polytechnique, France's elite school for military engineers and artillery officers, where Maximilian trained.`,quote:`as they say at the Polytechnic School`},{id:`twenty-five-thousand-francs-a-year`,term:`twenty-five thousand francs a year`,kind:`money`,chapter:50,text:`An income from investments that meant solid upper-middle-class comfort: perhaps forty to fifty times a labourer's yearly earnings, though modest beside a banker's millions.`,quote:`with twenty-five thousand francs a year`},{id:`pyramus-and-thisbe`,term:`Pyramus and Thisbe`,kind:`reference`,chapter:51,text:`In Ovid's story, two young Babylonians whose parents forbid their love whisper to each other through a crack in a garden wall. Shakespeare spoofs it in A Midsummer Night's Dream.`},{id:`man-proposes-yet-money-disposes`,term:`“man proposes,” yet “money disposes.”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:51,text:`A twist on the proverb 'Man proposes, God disposes', from Thomas à Kempis.`,quote:`though “man proposes,” yet “money disposes.”`},{id:`blouse`,term:`blouse`,kind:`word`,chapter:51,text:`The loose smock worn by French working men; a gentleman in one was disguised as a labourer.`,quote:`dressed in a common gray blouse and velvet cap`},{id:`lucern`,term:`lucern`,kind:`word`,chapter:51,text:`Lucerne, or alfalfa: a clover-like crop grown for animal fodder.`,quote:`a scanty crop of lucern`},{id:`arranged-marriage`,term:`arranged marriage`,kind:`custom`,chapter:51,text:`Among the well-to-do, fathers chose their children's spouses for family and financial reasons. Under the Civil Code a woman under twenty-one could not marry without parental consent.`,quote:`from his will there was no appeal`},{id:`food-for-cannon`,term:`“food for cannon”`,kind:`history`,chapter:51,text:`'Chair à canon', cannon fodder: a phrase popularly attributed to Napoleon's view of his conscripts.`,quote:`he called them ‘food for cannon,’ which was precisely`},{id:`the-nobility-of-the-cannon`,term:`the nobility of the cannon`,kind:`custom`,chapter:51,text:`Old families whose nobility went back to medieval knights ('the lance') versus the new titles and fortunes won in Napoleon's wars. By the 1830s the two had begun to intermarry.`,quote:`with the nobility of the cannon`},{id:`aqua-tofana`,term:`aqua Tofana`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`A notorious slow poison, probably arsenic-based, sold to would-be widows in 17th-century Italy and named after the woman said to have made it.`,quote:`the famous aqua Tofana`,seeAlso:[101]},{id:`corpus-christi`,term:`Corpus Christi`,kind:`custom`,chapter:52,text:`A Catholic feast in late spring, celebrated in Italy with flower-strewn streets and processions.`,quote:`on Corpus Christi Day`},{id:`mithridates`,term:`Mithridates`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`King of Pontus (died 63 BC), said to have made himself immune to poison by taking small doses daily. Edward is reciting his Latin school text, the biographer Cornelius Nepos.`,quote:`Mithridates, rex Ponticus`},{id:`brucine-false-angostura`,term:`brucine, false angostura`,kind:`word`,chapter:52,text:`Brucine is a bitter poison closely related to strychnine; 'false angostura' is the bark of the plant that yields it, once passed off as a medicinal bark.`,quote:`Brucine is extracted from the false angostura`},{id:`haroun-al-raschid-and-giaffar`,term:`Haroun-al-Raschid and Giaffar`,kind:`reference`,chapter:52,text:`The caliph of Baghdad and his vizier, who roam the city in disguise in many Arabian Nights tales.`,quote:`are really Haroun-al-Raschids and Giaffars`},{id:`desrues`,term:`Desrues`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`Antoine-François Desrues, a notorious Paris poisoner executed in 1777.`,quote:`Desrues was, however, I must confess, more skilful`},{id:`asmodeus`,term:`Asmodeus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:52,text:`The demon in Lesage's novel The Devil on Two Sticks, who lifts the roofs off houses to show a student the secret lives inside.`,quote:`of whom Asmodeus, if you were holding on`,seeAlso:[106]},{id:`magendie-and-flourens`,term:`Magendie and Flourens`,kind:`history`,chapter:52,text:`Leading French physiologists of the day, famous for their experiments on animals.`,quote:`against M. Magendie or M. Flourens`},{id:`the-mandarin-of-rousseau`,term:`the mandarin of Rousseau`,kind:`reference`,chapter:52,text:`A famous moral puzzle: if you could become rich by killing an unknown mandarin in China with a mere wish, would you? It was often attributed to Rousseau, though it is not found in his works.`,quote:`the mandarin who is killed five hundred leagues off`},{id:`the-academie`,term:`the Académie`,kind:`place`,chapter:52,text:`The Académie Royale de Musique: the official name of the Paris Opéra, home of grand opera and ballet.`,quote:`to escort to the Académie a Greek princess`,seeAlso:[53],placeId:`opera-paris`},{id:`robert-le-diable`,term:`Robert le Diable`,kind:`reference`,chapter:53,text:`Meyerbeer's grand opera (1831), the sensation of the age, about a knight whose father Bertram is secretly a demon. The bass Levasseur created the role of Bertram.`,quote:`made his reappearance in the character of Bertram`},{id:`the-omnibus-box`,term:`the omnibus box`,kind:`custom`,chapter:53,text:`A box at the Opéra rented jointly by a group of fashionable young men, who came and went as they pleased.`,quote:`his right of entry into the omnibus box`},{id:`a-lady-needs-an-escort`,term:`a lady needs an escort`,kind:`custom`,chapter:53,text:`A respectable woman could not appear at the opera with only her daughter; a gentleman had to accompany them.`,quote:`impossible for her to go alone with Eugénie to the opera`},{id:`the-pit`,term:`the pit`,kind:`word`,chapter:53,text:`The parterre: the floor of the theatre behind the orchestra, filled with ordinary paying spectators, who could be noisy critics.`,quote:`cried the pit in chorus`},{id:`diana-and-act-on`,term:`Diana and Actæon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:53,text:`In Greek myth the hunter Actaeon saw the virgin goddess Diana bathing; she turned him into a stag and his own hounds tore him apart.`,quote:`bring on me the fate of Actæon`},{id:`the-coronation-of-the-queen-of-england`,term:`the coronation of the queen of England`,kind:`history`,chapter:53,text:`Queen Victoria was crowned on 28 June 1838. The theft of the jewels of Mademoiselle Mars, the great comic actress, was another real-life sensation Albert lumps in with it.`,quote:`the coronation of the queen of England took place`},{id:`cagliostro`,term:`Cagliostro`,kind:`reference`,chapter:53,text:`An 18th-century adventurer and self-styled magician who claimed secret, even supernatural, knowledge.`,quote:`Being no disciple of Cagliostro`},{id:`featherless-bipeds`,term:`featherless bipeds`,kind:`reference`,chapter:53,text:`Plato reportedly defined man as a 'featherless biped'; the Cynic philosopher Diogenes mocked this by holding up a plucked chicken.`,quote:`featherless bipeds, to quote the late Diogenes`},{id:`ali-tepelini-yanina`,term:`Ali Tepelini / Yanina`,kind:`history`,chapter:53,text:`Ali Pasha of Tepelenë (c.1740–1822), an Albanian warlord who ruled Epirus from Yanina (Ioannina) almost as an independent prince, defying the Ottoman sultan until the sultan's forces brought him down.`,quote:`at the court of Ali Tepelini`},{id:`burnouse`,term:`burnouse`,kind:`word`,chapter:53,text:`A long hooded cloak of the kind worn in North Africa and the East.`,quote:`wrapping herself in her burnouse of white cashmere`},{id:`commissariat`,term:`commissariat`,kind:`word`,chapter:54,text:`The army department that bought food and supplies. Contractors and officials in it often grew rich, honestly or otherwise.`,quote:`M. Danglars in the commissariat department`},{id:`bayard-and-duguesclin`,term:`Bayard and Duguesclin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:54,text:`Two heroes of medieval French chivalry: Bayard, 'the knight without fear and without reproach', and Du Guesclin, famous for his courage and his lack of money.`,quote:`the integrity of a Bayard united to the poverty of a Duguesclin`},{id:`haiti-bonds`,term:`Haiti bonds`,kind:`history`,chapter:54,text:`Bonds linked to the huge indemnity Haiti was forced to pay France in 1825 in return for recognition of its independence. They were notoriously volatile, a favourite for gambling.`,quote:`the last rise in Haiti bonds`},{id:`denaro-e-santita-meta-della-meta`,term:`Denaro e santità, metà della metà`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:54,text:`Italian: 'Money and holiness: half of half' — believe only a quarter of what you hear about anyone's wealth or virtue.`,quote:`Denaro e santità`},{id:`ecarte-bouillotte-whist-boston`,term:`écarté, bouillotte, whist, boston`,kind:`custom`,chapter:54,text:`Card games popular in Paris drawing rooms. Écarté was a fast two-player game for high stakes.`,quote:`Haitians are the écarté of French stock-jobbing`},{id:`telegraphic-despatch`,term:`telegraphic despatch`,kind:`history`,chapter:54,text:`France's optical semaphore telegraph, a chain of signal towers, was reserved for the government. A minister's office could know news hours before the public, a huge temptation for speculators.`,quote:`some telegraphic despatch, of which you alone are in possession`},{id:`henri-iv-at-gabrielle-s`,term:`Henri IV at Gabrielle’s`,kind:`reference`,chapter:54,text:`Henri IV's mistress was Gabrielle d'Estrées, and his bronze statue stands on the Pont-Neuf. Albert's joke is a piece of obviously absurd 'news' that the statue went visiting.`,quote:`Henri IV. was seen yesterday at Gabrielle’s`},{id:`treport`,term:`Tréport`,kind:`place`,chapter:54,text:`A seaside resort on the Normandy coast, made fashionable by King Louis-Philippe's family, who stayed nearby at the château of Eu.`,quote:`we shall be at Tréport`,seeAlso:[82,85,117],placeId:`treport`},{id:`dante-s-tenth-canto`,term:`Dante’s tenth canto`,kind:`reference`,chapter:54,text:`In Canto X of Dante's Inferno, Cavalcante de' Cavalcanti, father of Dante's friend the poet Guido, appears among the heretics. The Cavalcanti were a genuinely ancient Florentine family.`,quote:`whose name Dante has celebrated in the tenth canto`},{id:`lucullus-dines-with-lucullus`,term:`“Lucullus dines with Lucullus”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:54,text:`Lucullus, a Roman general famous for luxury, scolded his cook for serving a plain meal when he dined alone: 'Lucullus dines with Lucullus tonight.' The Count means the dinner must be splendid whoever comes.`,quote:`‘Lucullus dines with Lucullus,’ that is quite sufficient`},{id:`surtout`,term:`surtout`,kind:`word`,chapter:55,text:`A long, close-fitting man's overcoat. The 'frogs' are the ornamental braid fastenings across the front, a military look that was decades out of date in 1838 Paris.`,quote:`dressed in one of the green surtouts`},{id:`lucca`,term:`Lucca`,kind:`place`,chapter:55,text:`A small city-state in Tuscany, in 1838 still an independent duchy. Its old families claimed proud descent, which makes it a handy birthplace for an invented nobleman.`,quote:`a worthy patrician of Lucca`,placeId:`lucca`},{id:`ex-major-in-the-austrian-service`,term:`ex-major in the Austrian service`,kind:`history`,chapter:55,text:`After 1815 Austria ruled Lombardy and Venetia and dominated the rest of Italy, so many Italians served in its army. The French rather looked down on Austrian officers, as the count jokes.`,quote:`Ex-major in the Austrian service?`},{id:`alicante`,term:`Alicante`,kind:`word`,chapter:55,text:`A sweet, dark Spanish dessert wine from the region of Alicante, often taken with a biscuit.`,quote:`Alicante, if you please; it is my favorite wine.`},{id:`perbacco-o-peccato`,term:`perbacco … O peccato!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:55,text:`Italian: 'By Bacchus!' (a mild oath) and 'Oh, what a pity!'`,quote:`Ah, _perbacco_, indispensable, were they?`},{id:`marriage-is-a-civil-affair-in-france`,term:`Marriage is a civil affair in France`,kind:`custom`,chapter:55,text:`Since the Revolution a French marriage has been legally valid only when performed by a civil official (the mayor), backed by birth and identity papers. A church wedding alone counted for nothing in law.`,quote:`Marriage is a civil affair in France`,seeAlso:[74]},{id:`48-000-francs`,term:`48,000 francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:55,text:`A fortune for a shabby old soldier. A Paris labourer earned perhaps 1.5–2 francs a day, so this is decades of wages; very roughly, a franc then bought what €10–20 buys now.`,quote:`credit on you for the further sum of 48,000 francs`},{id:`crosses`,term:`crosses`,kind:`word`,chapter:55,text:`Medals and badges of chivalric orders, worn on the coat on formal occasions. France had mocked such decorations since the Revolution yet still prized them, as the count says.`,quote:`Do not forget your crosses.`},{id:`the-golden-book-at-florence`,term:`the golden book at Florence`,kind:`reference`,chapter:56,text:`Italian cities kept a 'golden book' (libro d'oro) listing their noble families. To be 'inscribed in the golden book' was proof of ancient nobility.`,quote:`inscribed in the golden book at Florence`},{id:`pont-de-beauvoisin`,term:`Pont-de-Beauvoisin`,kind:`place`,chapter:56,text:`A town on the frontier between France and Savoy, which then belonged to the Kingdom of Sardinia. Crossing there meant entering France from Italy.`,quote:`from Pont-de-Beauvoisin to Paris`,placeId:`pont-de-beauvoisin`},{id:`slaves-in-asia-minor`,term:`slaves in Asia Minor`,kind:`history`,chapter:56,text:`In the ancient Roman world educated Greek slaves, many from Asia Minor (modern Turkey), were prized as tutors, doctors and secretaries, and fetched high prices.`,quote:`the slaves were treated in Asia Minor`},{id:`man-proposes-and-god-disposes`,term:`man proposes, and God disposes`,kind:`reference`,chapter:56,text:`A proverb from Thomas à Kempis's devotional classic The Imitation of Christ (15th century): we make plans, but their outcome is not in our hands.`,quote:`man proposes, and God disposes`},{id:`tuscan`,term:`Tuscan`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:56,text:`The dialect of Florence, the basis of literary Italian. Speaking 'very excellent Tuscan' marks Andrea as well schooled, and lets the two men talk privately in French company.`,quote:`he said, in very excellent Tuscan`},{id:`phaeton`,term:`phaeton`,kind:`word`,chapter:56,text:`A light, fast, open four-wheeled carriage with large wheels, driven by its owner: the sports car of a fashionable young man.`,quote:`if you purchase a phaeton`,seeAlso:[84]},{id:`uniform-cross-knee-breeches`,term:`uniform, cross, knee-breeches`,kind:`custom`,chapter:56,text:`Full evening dress for a formal dinner: an officer wore uniform and decorations; civilians of the old school still wore knee-breeches rather than trousers.`,quote:`uniform, cross, knee-breeches`},{id:`lucerne`,term:`lucerne`,kind:`word`,chapter:57,text:`Lucerne is alfalfa, a clover-like fodder crop. The chapter title refers to the field of it outside the Villefort garden, where Maximilian waits by the gate.`},{id:`taking-the-veil`,term:`taking the veil`,kind:`custom`,chapter:57,text:`A woman who became a nun renounced the world and her property. Valentine's fortune would then have passed to her father and, after him, to her half-brother.`,quote:`Now, if I had taken the veil`},{id:`in-reversion`,term:`in reversion`,kind:`word`,chapter:57,text:`A legal term: property that passes to someone after another person's interest in it ends. Here the money would go first to Villefort, then on to Edward.`,quote:`and, in reversion, to his son`},{id:`sabring-right-and-left-among-the-bedouins`,term:`sabring right and left among the Bedouins`,kind:`history`,chapter:57,text:`France had been conquering Algeria since 1830, and cavalry like Maximilian's Spahis fought the Arab and Berber tribes there. Médéah, the name he gives his horse, is an Algerian town taken by the French.`,quote:`sabring right and left among the Bedouins`},{id:`4-500-francs`,term:`4,500 francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:57,text:`The price of the horse: around eight years' pay for a labourer, and a serious sum even for an army captain living on modest means.`,quote:`they told me it was 4,500 francs`},{id:`cabriolet`,term:`cabriolet`,kind:`word`,chapter:57,text:`A light two-wheeled, one-horse carriage with a folding hood: the Paris cab of the day (and the origin of the word 'cab').`,quote:`so I took a cabriolet`},{id:`a-large-glass`,term:`a large glass`,kind:`word`,chapter:58,text:`A mirror. Placed opposite Noirtier, it lets him see the whole room without being able to turn his head.`,quote:`He was placed before a large glass`},{id:`paralytic`,term:`paralytic`,kind:`word`,chapter:58,text:`Noirtier's state, mind intact but body frozen except for the eyes, is what doctors now call locked-in syndrome, usually caused by a stroke in the brainstem.`,quote:`took their places on either side of the paralytic`},{id:`ennui`,term:`ennui`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:58,text:`French: boredom, weariness. Villefort could talk with his father through the eye signals but rarely bothers to.`,quote:`indifference and _ennui_ which prevented him`},{id:`seven-witnesses`,term:`seven witnesses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:59,text:`The notary is describing a sealed (or 'mystic') will under French law: the testator approves the document before seven witnesses, and the notary seals it in their presence.`,quote:`provided it be read in the presence of seven witnesses`},{id:`900-000-francs`,term:`900,000 francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:59,text:`A great fortune. Invested in government stock (rentes) at about 4–5 per cent, it would bring roughly 40,000 a year: perhaps a hundred times a working family's income.`,quote:`You are then in possession of 900,000 francs?`},{id:`in-landed-property-in-stock`,term:`in landed property? … in stock?`,kind:`custom`,chapter:59,text:`The two classic forms of wealth: land, which earned rents, or government bonds and shares. Noirtier keeps his as bank scrip, paper certificates locked in a casket.`,quote:`found 900,000 francs in bank scrip`},{id:`residuary-legatee`,term:`residuary legatee`,kind:`title`,chapter:59,text:`The person who inherits whatever remains of an estate after specific gifts and debts are paid: in effect, the main heir.`,quote:`your residuary legatee?`},{id:`the-law-does-not-allow-a-son-to-be-entirely-deprived`,term:`the law does not allow a son to be entirely deprived`,kind:`custom`,chapter:59,text:`The Napoleonic Code reserves a fixed share of every estate for the children (the réserve). A parent may freely leave only the rest, so a will giving everything away can be challenged in court.`,quote:`the law does not allow a son to be entirely deprived`,seeAlso:[74]},{id:`the-mountain`,term:`The Mountain`,kind:`history`,chapter:60,text:`The radical left of the revolutionary National Convention (1792–95), so called because its members sat on the highest benches. It led the Terror, and was willing to use any means.`,quote:`the terrible theories of The Mountain`},{id:`the-senator-s-robe-which-napoleon-cast-on-his-shoulders`,term:`the senator's robe, which Napoleon cast on his shoulders`,kind:`history`,chapter:60,text:`Napoleon gave many former revolutionaries seats in his Senate, a richly paid and largely ceremonial body. Villefort means the honour bought his father's dignity, not his loyalty.`,quote:`the senator’s robe, which Napoleon cast on his shoulders`},{id:`charles-x`,term:`Charles X`,kind:`history`,chapter:60,text:`Brother of Louis XVIII and King of France from 1824 until the July Revolution of 1830 drove him out. He made many royalist creations of nobility.`,quote:`created Baron d’Épinay by Charles X.`},{id:`a-telegraph`,term:`a telegraph`,kind:`history`,chapter:60,text:`Not the electric telegraph but the Chappe optical system: towers on hilltops, each with jointed black wooden arms whose positions were read by telescope and repeated down the line. A message could cross France in hours.`,quote:`its black arms, bending in every direction`,seeAlso:[61]},{id:`m-duchatel-or-m-montalivet`,term:`M. Duchâtel, or … M. Montalivet`,kind:`history`,chapter:60,text:`Real politicians who served as Minister of the Interior under Louis-Philippe. The telegraph belonged to the government, and only that ministry could send messages on it.`,quote:`a sign from M. Duchâtel, or from M. Montalivet`},{id:`tele-graphein`,term:`_têle_, _graphein_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:60,text:`Greek: 'far' and 'to write'. Together they make 'telegraph', far-writing.`,quote:`two Greek words, _têle_, _graphein_`},{id:`half-a-league-from-the-barriers`,term:`half a league from the Barriers`,kind:`word`,chapter:60,text:`A league was about 4 km (2.5 miles). The Barriers were the gates in the customs wall around Paris, where duty was charged on goods entering the city.`,quote:`only half a league from the Barriers`},{id:`barriere-d-enfer`,term:`Barrière d'Enfer`,kind:`place`,chapter:61,text:`One of the gates in the customs wall on the south side of Paris, leading to the Orléans road. Its name means 'Gate of Hell'; the square there is today's Place Denfert-Rochereau.`,quote:`the Barrière d’Enfer, taking the road to`,placeId:`barriere-d-enfer`},{id:`the-tower-of-montlhery`,term:`the tower of Montlhéry`,kind:`place`,chapter:61,text:`The keep of a ruined medieval castle on a hill about 25 km south of Paris. A real telegraph station was set up on it, on the Paris–Bayonne line toward Spain.`,quote:`reached the tower of Montlhéry`,placeId:`montlhery-tower`},{id:`dormice-as-the-romans-did`,term:`dormice … as the Romans did`,kind:`reference`,chapter:61,text:`The Romans fattened dormice in jars and ate them as a delicacy, preserved in honey. Petronius describes them at a banquet in his comic novel the Satyricon.`,quote:`I have read so in Petronius`},{id:`a-thousand-francs-a-hundred-crowns`,term:`A thousand francs … A hundred crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:61,text:`The operator's pay is under 3 francs a day, with lodging; his future pension is 300 francs a year (a crown, or écu, here meaning 3 francs). The 25,000 the count offers is twenty-five years' salary.`,quote:`And how much is the pension?`},{id:`don-carlos-has-fled-from-bourges`,term:`Don Carlos has fled from Bourges`,kind:`history`,chapter:61,text:`Don Carlos, pretender to the Spanish throne, had lost the Carlist civil war in 1839 and was interned by the French government at Bourges. His escape would mean renewed war, and Spanish government bonds would plunge.`,quote:`Because Don Carlos has fled from Bourges`},{id:`le-messager-le-moniteur`,term:`_Le Messager_ … _Le Moniteur_`,kind:`history`,chapter:61,text:`Le Messager was an evening paper close to the government that printed telegraph news; Le Moniteur was the government's official gazette. A denial in the Moniteur was final.`,quote:`Next morning _Le Moniteur_ contained the following`},{id:`the-funds-rose-one-per-cent`,term:`The funds rose one per cent`,kind:`custom`,chapter:61,text:`'The funds' are government bonds. Prices moved by fractions of a per cent, so a one per cent swing on millions of francs meant a fortune made or lost overnight.`,quote:`The funds rose one per cent higher`},{id:`duc-d-antin`,term:`Duc d'Antin`,kind:`history`,chapter:62,text:`A courtier of Louis XIV famed for flattery: he had a whole avenue of trees felled overnight because it spoiled the king's view.`,quote:`the Duc d’Antin removed in a single night`},{id:`hobbema-murillo`,term:`Hobbema … Murillo`,kind:`reference`,chapter:62,text:`Old masters: Dutch landscape and genre painters (Hobbema, Paulus Potter, Mieris, Gerard Dou), alongside Raphael, Van Dyck and the Spaniards Zurbarán and Murillo. Owning all of these was princely.`,quote:`here are two by Hobbema, a Paul Potter`},{id:`the-museum`,term:`the Museum`,kind:`place`,chapter:62,text:`The Louvre, the national picture gallery. Debray's point is that the government could not afford the painting the count simply bought.`,quote:`it was proposed for the Museum`},{id:`stock`,term:`stock`,kind:`word`,chapter:62,text:`A stiff, wide neckband, fastened behind, worn instead of a cravat, especially by military men.`,quote:`A black satin stock, fresh from the maker’s hands`},{id:`enceinte`,term:`_enceinte_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:62,text:`French: pregnant. Polite English of the period often borrowed the French word.`,quote:`she that was _enceinte_`},{id:`macbeth-uses-to-point-out-banquo`,term:`Macbeth uses to point out Banquo`,kind:`reference`,chapter:62,text:`In Shakespeare's Macbeth the murdered Banquo's ghost appears at a feast, and Macbeth points at it in terror while his guests see nothing.`,quote:`the gesture Macbeth uses to point out Banquo`},{id:`how-many-covers`,term:`How many covers?`,kind:`word`,chapter:62,text:`A 'cover' is a place setting at table; Bertuccio is asking how many guests to lay for.`,quote:`How many covers?`},{id:`apicius`,term:`Apicius`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`A famously extravagant Roman gourmet whose name was attached to the only surviving ancient cookbook.`,quote:`like one of the displays with which Apicius`},{id:`a-thousand-louis`,term:`a thousand louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:63,text:`A louis was a 20-franc gold coin, so this is 20,000 francs for one dinner for ten: several lifetimes of the telegraph man's pay.`,quote:`possible to expend a thousand louis upon a dinner`},{id:`eating-pearls-like-cleopatra-drinking-refined-gold`,term:`eating pearls, like Cleopatra … drinking refined gold`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`Cleopatra is said to have dissolved a priceless pearl in vinegar and drunk it to win a bet with Mark Antony; Lorenzo de' Medici was reputed to take dissolved gold as medicine.`,quote:`eating pearls, like Cleopatra`},{id:`sterlet-lamprey`,term:`sterlet … lamprey`,kind:`word`,chapter:63,text:`The sterlet is a small sturgeon of Russia's Volga, highly prized; the lamprey is an eel-like fish, here from Lake Fusaro near Naples. Moving them alive across Europe in 1838 was absurdly difficult.`,quote:`This one is, I think, a sterlet`},{id:`cupitor-impossibilium`,term:`_cupitor impossibilium_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:63,text:`Latin: 'a lover of the impossible'. The Roman historian Tacitus applied the phrase to Nero.`,quote:`I am like Nero—_cupitor impossibilium_`},{id:`marquise-de-ganges`,term:`Marquise de Ganges`,kind:`history`,chapter:63,text:`A celebrated beauty murdered in 1667 by her brothers-in-law, the Abbé and the Chevalier de Ganges: a notorious true-crime story Dumas himself retold.`,quote:`the chamber of the Marquise de Ganges`},{id:`ugolino-s-tower-tasso-s-prison-francesca-and-paolo`,term:`Ugolino's tower … Tasso's prison … Francesca and Paolo`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`Famous Italian sites of suffering: the tower in Pisa where Count Ugolino starved (Dante's Inferno), the cell in Ferrara where the poet Tasso was confined, and the Rimini room of Dante's doomed lovers.`,quote:`we have at Pisa, Ugolino’s tower`},{id:`the-goddess-lucina`,term:`the goddess Lucina`,kind:`reference`,chapter:63,text:`The Roman goddess of childbirth.`,quote:`a bed visited by the goddess Lucina`},{id:`what-is-done-to-infanticides`,term:`What is done to infanticides`,kind:`custom`,chapter:63,text:`Under the Napoleonic Penal Code, killing a newborn child was punished by death, by guillotine.`,quote:`What is done to infanticides in this country?`},{id:`tilbury`,term:`tilbury`,kind:`word`,chapter:64,text:`A light, open, two-wheeled carriage for one horse, driven by its owner with a groom perched behind: very much the young dandy's vehicle.`,quote:`Andrea Cavalcanti found his tilbury`},{id:`landau`,term:`landau`,kind:`word`,chapter:64,text:`A large four-wheeled carriage with a hood at each end that can be folded back, used for family outings.`,quote:`He offered a seat in his landau`},{id:`nil-admirari`,term:`_nil admirari_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:64,text:`Latin: 'to be astonished at nothing'. Horace's advice for a calm, happy life; also the pose of a true man of the world.`,quote:`faithful to the principle of Horace, _nil admirari_`},{id:`facchino-or-cicerone`,term:`_facchino_ or _cicerone_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:64,text:`Italian: a porter, or a tourist guide.`,quote:`by acting as _facchino_ or _cicerone_`},{id:`m-bossuet`,term:`M. Bossuet`,kind:`reference`,chapter:64,text:`Jacques-Bénigne Bossuet, the great 17th-century bishop and preacher. Caderousse means 'thank you for the sermon'.`,quote:`Do you think so, M. Bossuet?`},{id:`hereditary-rank-is-abolished`,term:`hereditary rank is abolished`,kind:`history`,chapter:64,text:`After the 1830 revolution, seats in the Chamber of Peers stopped being hereditary (1831): a peer of France could no longer pass the title to his son.`,quote:`but then, hereditary rank is abolished`},{id:`no-106`,term:`No. 106`,kind:`custom`,chapter:64,text:`Convicts in the prison hulks and labour camps (bagnes) of Toulon were known by number, not name. An escapee caught without papers would simply be sent back.`,quote:`Then I should become simply No. 106`},{id:`place-louis-xv-pont-de-la-revolution`,term:`Place Louis XV. … Pont de la Révolution`,kind:`place`,chapter:65,text:`Old names for today's Place de la Concorde and Pont de la Concorde. The square changed name with each regime; Parisians often kept using the earlier one.`,quote:`At the Place Louis XV. the three young people separated`},{id:`proprio-motu`,term:`_proprio motu_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:65,text:`Latin: 'of her own accord', without being asked.`,quote:`receive an avowal _proprio motu_`},{id:`pactolus`,term:`Pactolus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:65,text:`A river in ancient Lydia whose sands carried gold, said to be where King Midas washed off his golden touch. Demoustier was an 18th-century writer of popular mythology.`,quote:`My money boxes are my Pactolus`},{id:`the-haitian-funds`,term:`the Haitian funds`,kind:`history`,chapter:65,text:`In 1825 France forced Haiti to pay a huge indemnity, 150 million francs, for recognizing its independence. Haiti borrowed in Paris and often missed payments, so Haitian bonds were a gamble.`,quote:`you were the first who told me of the Haitian funds`},{id:`charles-v-repassed-the-bidassoa`,term:`Charles V. repassed the Bidassoa`,kind:`history`,chapter:65,text:`His supporters called Don Carlos 'Charles V' of Spain. The Bidassoa is the river on the French–Spanish border; crossing it meant leaving Spain, defeated, in 1839.`,quote:`the day Charles V. repassed the Bidassoa`},{id:`pin-money`,term:`pin-money`,kind:`word`,chapter:65,text:`An allowance given to a wife for her personal spending, such as clothes. Danglars's 250,000 francs is a satirical 'allowance'.`,quote:`250,000 francs were paid to you for pin-money`},{id:`theatre-italien`,term:`Théâtre Italien`,kind:`place`,chapter:65,text:`The Paris opera house devoted to Italian opera, the most fashionable in the city, whose star singers were the celebrities of the day.`,quote:`made such a successful appearance at the Théâtre Italien`},{id:`inscribed-his-name-to-speak-against-the-budget`,term:`inscribed his name to speak against the budget`,kind:`custom`,chapter:66,text:`Danglars is a deputy in the Chamber, where members signed up in advance to speak. As an opposition member he attacks the government's budget.`,quote:`inscribed his name to speak against the budget`},{id:`suspends-payment`,term:`suspends payment`,kind:`custom`,chapter:66,text:`A merchant who 'suspends payment' stops honouring his debts: in effect, bankruptcy. Bills of exchange (written orders to pay) drawn on him come back unpaid.`,quote:`my fine Jacopo Manfredi suspends payment`},{id:`magnetism`,term:`magnetism`,kind:`history`,chapter:66,text:`'Animal magnetism', or mesmerism, was a fashionable belief that an invisible fluid linked people and could produce trances and prophetic dreams.`,quote:`It is magnetism, she says`},{id:`the-seven-fat-and-the-seven-lean-kine`,term:`the seven fat and the seven lean kine`,kind:`reference`,chapter:66,text:`In Genesis, Pharaoh dreams of seven fat cows eaten by seven lean ones; Joseph interprets it as seven years of plenty followed by seven of famine.`,quote:`Recollect the seven fat and the seven lean kine`},{id:`ultramontane`,term:`ultramontane`,kind:`word`,chapter:66,text:`Literally 'beyond the mountains': from across the Alps, that is, Italian.`,quote:`these ultramontane millionaires`},{id:`podestas-condottieri`,term:`_podestàs_ … _condottieri_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:66,text:`Italian: chief magistrates of medieval Italian cities, and the mercenary captains who fought their wars.`,quote:`all these old _podestàs_, these ancient _condottieri_`},{id:`el-dorado-and-potosi`,term:`El Dorado, and Potosí`,kind:`reference`,chapter:66,text:`El Dorado was the legendary South American city of gold; Potosí, in Bolivia, was a real mountain of silver that enriched Spain for centuries.`,quote:`an El Dorado, and Potosí`},{id:`dieppe-for-the-benefit-of-sea-air`,term:`Dieppe for the benefit of sea air`,kind:`custom`,chapter:66,text:`Dieppe, on the Normandy coast, was a fashionable sea-bathing resort; doctors prescribed sea air for many complaints.`,quote:`obliged to go to Dieppe for the benefit of sea air`},{id:`parvenus`,term:`parvenus`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:66,text:`French: upstarts, people newly risen to wealth or rank without the background society expects.`,quote:`being both parvenus, both having become noble`},{id:`the-palais-the-salle-des-pas-perdus`,term:`the Palais … the Salle des Pas-Perdus`,kind:`place`,chapter:67,text:`The Palais de Justice, the law courts on the Île de la Cité, entered from the Rue de Harlay. The Salle des Pas-Perdus ('hall of lost footsteps') is its vast waiting hall, always crowded with lawyers and clients.`,quote:`Madame Danglars soon reached the Salle des Pas-Perdus`,placeId:`palais-de-justice-paris`},{id:`deed-of-resilition`,term:`deed of resilition`,kind:`word`,chapter:67,text:`A legal deed cancelling a lease before its term ends.`,quote:`made the tenant sign the deed of resilition`},{id:`four-men-carried-me-from-paris-to-chalons`,term:`Four men carried me from Paris to Châlons`,kind:`custom`,chapter:67,text:`Before railways, an invalid too weak for a jolting coach might be carried in a litter, then sent by river boat down the Saône and Rhône toward the south.`,quote:`Four men carried me from Paris to Châlons`},{id:`dark-lantern`,term:`dark lantern`,kind:`word`,chapter:67,text:`A lantern with a sliding shutter, so its light can be hidden or directed in a narrow beam.`,quote:`I had provided myself with a dark lantern`},{id:`the-foundling-hospital`,term:`the foundling hospital`,kind:`custom`,chapter:67,text:`Paris's Hospice des Enfants-Trouvés took in abandoned babies. Parents often left a token, such as half a torn card or cloth, so they could later prove their claim and take the child back.`,quote:`he had put it in the foundling hospital`},{id:`half-a-baron-s-crown`,term:`half a baron's crown`,kind:`custom`,chapter:67,text:`Noble families marked their linen with the coronet of their rank and an initial. The baron's coronet points to her first husband's title.`,quote:`marked with half a baron’s crown, and the letter H`},{id:`queen-mab-or-titania`,term:`Queen Mab or Titania`,kind:`reference`,chapter:68,text:`Fairy queens from Shakespeare: Queen Mab from Mercutio's speech in Romeo and Juliet, Titania from A Midsummer Night's Dream.`,quote:`if I had taken Queen Mab or Titania as my companion`},{id:`marle-s-or-fossin-s`,term:`Marlé's or Fossin's`,kind:`place`,chapter:68,text:`Fashionable Paris jewellers of the day; Fossin was among the most famous, supplying the royal family.`,quote:`glittered in the window at Marlé’s or Fossin’s`},{id:`the-magnetic-wire`,term:`the magnetic wire`,kind:`word`,chapter:68,text:`The count plays on Albert's 'electric shock' of sympathy: the electric telegraph wire was the newest marvel of science.`,quote:`the magnetic wire I was guiding acted`},{id:`summer-balls-are-fashionable`,term:`Summer balls are fashionable`,kind:`custom`,chapter:68,text:`High society usually left Paris for country estates after the spring season. 'Those who remain in Paris in July' are the truly devoted Parisians, so a July ball is select.`,quote:`Summer balls are fashionable.`},{id:`the-count-saint-germain`,term:`the Count Saint-Germain`,kind:`history`,chapter:68,text:`An 18th-century adventurer at the court of Louis XV who hinted that he was centuries old and owned an elixir of life.`,quote:`Cagliostro or the Count Saint-Germain`},{id:`the-great-d-aguesseau`,term:`the great d'Aguesseau`,kind:`history`,chapter:68,text:`Henri-François d'Aguesseau (1668–1751), Chancellor of France, a byword for stern legal integrity. Albert applies the name to Villefort.`,quote:`We shall try to persuade the great d’Aguesseau`},{id:`the-prefect-of-police`,term:`the prefect of police`,kind:`title`,chapter:69,text:`The head of the Paris police, a powerful government appointee. Boville has risen from inspector of prisons to a senior post under him.`,quote:`from the prefect of police?`},{id:`prie-dieu`,term:`_prie-Dieu_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:69,text:`French: literally 'pray God'. A kneeling desk for private prayer.`,quote:`with a _prie-Dieu_, all its furniture`},{id:`lutheran-liberty-of-conscience`,term:`Lutheran … liberty of conscience`,kind:`history`,chapter:69,text:`France was overwhelmingly Catholic, but the Charter of 1830 guaranteed freedom of worship; a Protestant could not be harassed for his creed.`,quote:`liberty of conscience is established in France`},{id:`a-knight-of-jesus-christ`,term:`a knight of Jesus Christ`,kind:`title`,chapter:69,text:`The Order of Christ, the highest order of chivalry awarded by the pope.`,quote:`has made him a knight of Jesus Christ`},{id:`homer-carrying-his-guide-belisarius-begging`,term:`Homer carrying his guide … Belisarius begging`,kind:`reference`,chapter:69,text:`Popular sentimental engravings: the blind poet Homer, and the Byzantine general Belisarius, said by legend to have been blinded and reduced to begging.`,quote:`Homer carrying his guide`},{id:`kerseymere-nankeen`,term:`kerseymere … nankeen`,kind:`word`,chapter:69,text:`Kerseymere is a fine twilled wool; nankeen a sturdy yellowish cotton, originally from Nanjing. A coat 'in the fashion of 1811' was nearly thirty years out of date.`,quote:`a white kerseymere waistcoat, and nankeen pantaloons`},{id:`the-hulks`,term:`the hulks`,kind:`history`,chapter:69,text:`Old ships stripped of masts and used as floating prisons. Britain packed French prisoners of war into them during the Napoleonic Wars.`,quote:`consigned to the hulks, whence he had escaped`},{id:`navarino-king-otho`,term:`Navarino … King Otho`,kind:`history`,chapter:69,text:`The Greeks rose against Ottoman rule in 1821. At Navarino (1827) British, French and Russian fleets destroyed the Turkish-Egyptian fleet, and in 1832 the Bavarian prince Otto became the first King of Greece.`,quote:`After the battle of Navarino, when the Greek government`},{id:`bagneres-luchon-and-cauterets-badhaus`,term:`Bagnères, Luchon, and Cauterets … _Badhaus_`,kind:`place`,chapter:69,text:`Spa towns in the Pyrenees famous for their mineral springs. Badhaus is German for 'bath-house'.`,quote:`equal to those at Bagnères, Luchon, and Cauterets`},{id:`grisier`,term:`Grisier`,kind:`history`,chapter:69,text:`Augustin Grisier, the most celebrated fencing master in Paris, and Dumas's own teacher.`,quote:`every other day Grisier comes to my house`},{id:`the-waltz-and-galop`,term:`the waltz and galop`,kind:`word`,chapter:70,text:`The galop was a lively, fast ballroom dance in quick time; both it and the waltz were the fashionable dances of the 1830s.`,quote:`the whirl of the waltz and galop`},{id:`myosotis`,term:`myosotis`,kind:`word`,chapter:70,text:`The forget-me-not. In the language of flowers it means faithful remembrance.`,quote:`the other with one of myosotis`},{id:`this-lion`,term:`this 'lion'`,kind:`word`,chapter:70,text:`Slang, borrowed from English, for a fashionable celebrity everyone wants to see.`,quote:`we shall have this ‘lion’`},{id:`elssler-le-diable-boiteux-the-cachucha`,term:`Elssler … _Le Diable boiteux_ … the cachucha`,kind:`history`,chapter:70,text:`Fanny Elssler, the Austrian star ballerina, created a sensation at the Paris Opéra dancing the cachucha, a Spanish dance, in the ballet Le Diable boiteux (1836).`,quote:`Elssler was dancing in _Le Diable`},{id:`the-institute`,term:`the Institute`,kind:`place`,chapter:70,text:`The Institut de France, which unites the national academies. The French Academy judges language and literature; the Academy of Sciences, science. Albert mocks a scientist admitted to the former for his prose style.`,quote:`he immediately laid his discovery before the Institute`},{id:`knight-officer-commander`,term:`knight … officer … commander`,kind:`title`,chapter:70,text:`Grades of the Legion of Honour, France's chief decoration, rising from knight (chevalier) to officer, commander and higher.`,quote:`was only a knight of the Legion`},{id:`the-royalty-of-july`,term:`the royalty of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:70,text:`The July Monarchy of Louis-Philippe, set up after the 1830 revolution. Voting and power went with wealth, which is why a millionaire's title seems 'the finest' to Danglars.`,quote:`the finest title under the royalty of July`},{id:`their-signature-is-worth-five-per-cent`,term:`their signature is worth five per cent`,kind:`custom`,chapter:70,text:`When a bank failed, creditors recovered only a fraction of what was owed. The count means Franck & Poulmann's paper will now fetch five centimes on the franc.`,quote:`their signature is worth five per cent`},{id:`partant-pour-la-syrie`,term:`_Partant pour la Syrie_`,kind:`history`,chapter:70,text:`'Setting out for Syria', a song written by Napoleon's stepdaughter Hortense that became the unofficial Bonapartist anthem.`,quote:`had sung _Partant pour la Syrie_`},{id:`bread-and-salt`,term:`bread and salt`,kind:`custom`,chapter:71,text:`Across the Arab world and the East, eating bread and salt together under someone's roof creates a sacred bond: host and guest become friends, and may not thereafter act as enemies.`,quote:`eaten bread and salt under the same roof`},{id:`muscatel-grapes`,term:`Muscatel grapes`,kind:`word`,chapter:71,text:`Sweet, perfumed dessert grapes, grown here in a heated hothouse to ripen in a northern July.`,quote:`gathered a bunch of Muscatel grapes`},{id:`the-first-stage`,term:`the first stage`,kind:`word`,chapter:71,text:`Long journeys were made in stages between post-houses where horses were changed; 'the first stage' is the first post-house out of Marseilles.`,quote:`took place on the first stage`},{id:`foul-deeds-will-rise`,term:`'Foul deeds will rise …'`,kind:`reference`,chapter:72,text:`From Shakespeare's Hamlet, Act 1: crimes will come to light however deeply buried.`,quote:`Foul deeds will rise`},{id:`postilion`,term:`postilion`,kind:`word`,chapter:72,text:`The rider on one of the lead horses of a post-carriage, who drove in place of a coachman.`,quote:`I stopped the postilion`,seeAlso:[97]},{id:`a-leaden-coffin`,term:`a leaden coffin`,kind:`custom`,chapter:72,text:`A lead-lined coffin was sealed so that a body could be carried a long way, here from Provence to the family vault in Paris.`,quote:`I had him put into a leaden coffin`},{id:`orangeade`,term:`orangeade`,kind:`word`,chapter:72,text:`A drink of orange juice and sweetened water, often given to invalids as a cooling drink.`,quote:`a bottle of orangeade, her usual beverage`},{id:`plebeian-extraction`,term:`plebeian extraction`,kind:`custom`,chapter:72,text:`Of common, non-noble birth. To a proud old aristocrat, a merchant's son, however honourable, was no match for a noble heiress.`,quote:`Morrel was of plebeian extraction`},{id:`the-commander-did-don-juan`,term:`the commander did Don Juan`,kind:`reference`,chapter:73,text:`In the Don Juan legend (and Mozart's Don Giovanni), the statue of a murdered Commander walks with heavy stone steps to drag the seducer to hell.`,quote:`the footsteps of the commander did Don Juan`},{id:`the-signature-of-the-contract`,term:`the signature of the contract`,kind:`custom`,chapter:73,text:`Before the civil wedding, both families signed a marriage contract fixing dowry and property before a notary and witnesses. In practice it was the binding public betrothal.`,quote:`a comedy called the signature of the contract`},{id:`manfred-nor-anthony`,term:`Manfred nor Anthony`,kind:`reference`,chapter:73,text:`Two brooding Romantic heroes: Byron's Manfred (1817), tormented by guilt, and Antony, hero of Dumas's own sensational play Antony (1831).`,quote:`I imitate neither Manfred nor Anthony`},{id:`tetanus`,term:`tetanus`,kind:`word`,chapter:73,text:`Tetanus, or lockjaw, causes violent spasms and rigid limbs. The doctor notes that poisoning by certain plant alkaloids looks almost exactly the same.`,quote:`I discovered symptoms of tetanus`},{id:`six-grains`,term:`six grains`,kind:`money`,chapter:73,text:`A grain was an apothecary's weight of about 65 milligrams, so six grains is under half a gram.`,quote:`there were six grains`},{id:`errare-humanum-est`,term:`_errare humanum est_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:73,text:`Latin: 'to err is human'.`,quote:`this axiom, _errare humanum est_`},{id:`an-inquest`,term:`an inquest`,kind:`custom`,chapter:73,text:`A formal judicial inquiry into a suspicious death, with a post-mortem. For a crown prosecutor, one in his own house would mean public scandal.`,quote:`an inquest will become necessary`},{id:`a-magdalen-by-correggio`,term:`a Magdalen by Correggio`,kind:`reference`,chapter:73,text:`Correggio, the Italian Renaissance painter, was famous for his Penitent Magdalen: Mary Magdalene in tearful, beautiful grief.`,quote:`a head like that of a Magdalen by Correggio`},{id:`pere-lachaise`,term:`Père-Lachaise`,kind:`place`,chapter:74,text:`Paris's great cemetery, opened in 1804 on a hillside east of the city, crowded with the family vaults of the wealthy.`,quote:`the cemetery of Père-Lachaise`,seeAlso:[105],placeId:`pere-lachaise`},{id:`mourning-coaches`,term:`mourning-coaches`,kind:`word`,chapter:74,text:`Black carriages hired for a funeral to carry mourners behind the hearse.`,quote:`a long file of mourning-coaches`},{id:`tenacem-propositi-virum`,term:`_Tenacem propositi virum_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:74,text:`Latin, from Horace's Odes: 'a man steadfast of purpose', whom nothing can shake.`,quote:`_Tenacem propositi virum_`},{id:`the-old-conventionalist-of-93`,term:`the old Conventionalist of '93`,kind:`history`,chapter:74,text:`A member of the revolutionary National Convention of 1793, which voted the king's death. Marengo (1800) and Austerlitz (1805) were two of Napoleon's greatest victories.`,quote:`the old Conventionalist of ’93`},{id:`eo-rus`,term:`_Eo rus_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:74,text:`Latin: 'I am going to the country'. Beauchamp calls death a retreat to a country cottage.`,quote:`‘_Eo rus_, and all will be over.’`},{id:`the-prescribed-three-months`,term:`the prescribed three months`,kind:`custom`,chapter:74,text:`Etiquette laid down fixed periods of mourning for each degree of kinship; during them a family withdrew from society and avoided celebrations such as weddings.`,quote:`during the prescribed three months`},{id:`a-much-more-nervous-than-sanguine-temperament`,term:`a much more nervous than sanguine temperament`,kind:`word`,chapter:74,text:`Old medical theory sorted people into temperaments: the 'sanguine' (ruddy, full-blooded) were thought prone to apoplexy, the 'nervous' (slight, excitable) were not.`,quote:`a much more nervous than sanguine temperament`},{id:`you-know-they-are-my-witnesses`,term:`you know they are my witnesses`,kind:`custom`,chapter:74,text:`A marriage contract and wedding required witnesses, usually close friends of each party.`,quote:`you know they are my witnesses`},{id:`february-5th-1815`,term:`February 5th, 1815`,kind:`history`,chapter:75,text:`At that date Napoleon was still on Elba and Louis XVIII had reigned for less than a year. Bonapartist clubs met in secret, waiting for the emperor's return.`,quote:`held February 5th, 1815`},{id:`state-councillor`,term:`State-Councillor`,kind:`title`,chapter:75,text:`A member of the Council of State, a senior body of government officials: not a man you would expect to find on a coachman's box.`,quote:`we shall be driven by a State-Councillor`},{id:`a-baron-and-a-field-marshal`,term:`a baron and a field-marshal`,kind:`title`,chapter:75,text:`'Field-marshal' here translates maréchal de camp, a general's rank equal to major-general, not the much higher marshal of France.`,quote:`he has made me a baron and a field-marshal`},{id:`the-quai-des-ormes`,term:`the Quai des Ormes`,kind:`place`,chapter:75,text:`A quay on the Right Bank of the Seine near the Hôtel de Ville, where steps led down to the river.`,quote:`at that part of the Quai des Ormes`,placeId:`quai-des-ormes`},{id:`honorable-reparation`,term:`honorable reparation`,kind:`custom`,chapter:75,text:`The language of the duel: an insulted gentleman demanded 'satisfaction' with weapons. Seconds (witnesses) checked the weapons and rules, and could be called on to attest the fight was fair.`,quote:`without demanding honorable reparation`},{id:`the-baths-of-lucca`,term:`the baths of Lucca`,kind:`place`,chapter:76,text:`Bagni di Lucca, a spa town in the Tuscan hills whose casino was one of the first licensed gambling houses in Europe.`,quote:`at the gaming-table of the baths of Lucca`,placeId:`bagni-di-lucca`},{id:`the-quarries-of-saravezza`,term:`the quarries of Saravezza`,kind:`place`,chapter:76,text:`Seravezza, near Carrara, is famous for its marble quarries; Michelangelo took stone from there.`,quote:`buried in the quarries of Saravezza`,placeId:`seravezza`},{id:`the-shield-of-minerva-sappho`,term:`the shield of Minerva … Sappho`,kind:`reference`,chapter:76,text:`Minerva, goddess of wisdom, carried an impenetrable shield; Sappho was the ancient Greek poet of love. Eugénie is armoured against Andrea's sighs.`,quote:`they might have been said to fall on the shield of Minerva`},{id:`tableaux-vivants`,term:`_tableaux vivants_`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:76,text:`French: 'living pictures', a fashionable entertainment in which costumed people posed motionless to recreate a painting.`,quote:`one of the _tableaux vivants_`},{id:`antonia-in-the-cremona-violin`,term:`Antonia in the _Cremona Violin_`,kind:`reference`,chapter:76,text:`A tale by E. T. A. Hoffmann: Antonia has a glorious voice, but a weakness of the chest means that singing will kill her.`,quote:`like Antonia in the _Cremona`},{id:`thalberg`,term:`Thalberg`,kind:`history`,chapter:76,text:`Sigismond Thalberg, a virtuoso pianist of the 1830s and Liszt's great rival in Paris.`,quote:`she plays the piano like Thalberg`},{id:`cavatina-bravo-bravi-brava`,term:`cavatina … Bravo, bravi, brava!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:76,text:`A cavatina is a short, simple operatic song. In Italian 'bravo' praises a man, 'brava' a woman, 'bravi' a group: Danglars shows off by using all three.`,quote:`do you hear that delightful cavatina?`},{id:`as-claudius-said-to-hamlet`,term:`as Claudius said to Hamlet`,kind:`reference`,chapter:76,text:`In Hamlet Act 1, King Claudius tells the grieving prince that losing fathers is the common lot of nature, so mourning should not go on too long.`,quote:`as Claudius said to Hamlet`},{id:`leave-the-spoons-in-their-cups`,term:`leave the spoons in their cups`,kind:`custom`,chapter:76,text:`English tea-table etiquette of the time: leaving the spoon in the cup signalled that you wanted no more tea.`,quote:`in the English fashion, to leave the spoons`},{id:`charles-ix-and-catherine-de-medici`,term:`Charles IX and Catherine de’ Medici`,kind:`history`,chapter:77,text:`The French king Charles IX and his mother Catherine de’ Medici were blamed for the Saint Bartholomew’s Day Massacre of 1572, when thousands of Protestants were killed in Paris. Albert jokingly casts himself as an actor who has just played a part.`,quote:`Charles IX. put to Catherine de’ Medici`},{id:`chibouque`,term:`chibouque`,kind:`word`,chapter:77,text:`A long-stemmed Turkish tobacco pipe. Latakia is a fine, smoky tobacco from Syria.`,quote:`two chibouques filled with excellent latakia`},{id:`byron-s-haydee`,term:`Byron’s Haydée`,kind:`reference`,chapter:77,text:`In Byron’s comic epic Don Juan (1819–24), Haidée is a beautiful Greek island girl who rescues the shipwrecked hero. Readers of the day knew the name from the poem.`,quote:`anywhere but in Byron’s poems`},{id:`dionysius-the-tyrant`,term:`Dionysius the Tyrant`,kind:`reference`,chapter:77,text:`Dionysius II, tyrant of Syracuse, was driven from power in the 4th century BC and is said to have ended his days running a school in Corinth. People cited him as the classic example of fortune’s reversals.`,quote:`How was it that Dionysius the Tyrant became a schoolmaster?`},{id:`yanina`,term:`Yanina`,kind:`place`,chapter:77,text:`Ioannina, a city in Epirus in northwest Greece, then part of the Ottoman Empire. Ali Pasha ruled from its fortress beside the lake, below the Pindus mountains.`,quote:`We were in the palace of Yanina`},{id:`palikares`,term:`Palikares`,kind:`word`,chapter:77,text:`Pallikars: Greek and Albanian irregular soldiers famed for their courage. The word came to mean ‘brave young men’.`,quote:`the sashes of our Palikares`},{id:`seraskier`,term:`seraskier`,kind:`title`,chapter:77,text:`An Ottoman commander-in-chief. Hurshid (‘Kourchid’) Pasha led the sultan’s army against Ali Pasha in 1820–22.`},{id:`firman`,term:`firman`,kind:`word`,chapter:77,text:`A formal decree issued by the Ottoman sultan. ‘The master’ and ‘the emperor’ in Haydée’s story both mean the sultan.`,quote:`today arrives the firman of the master`},{id:`the-bride-of-lammermoor`,term:`The Bride of Lammermoor`,kind:`reference`,chapter:78,text:`Walter Scott’s 1819 novel, made into Donizetti’s popular 1835 opera. Edgar of Ravenswood bursts in just after his beloved has been made to sign a contract to marry another man.`,quote:`like the Laird of Ravenswood`},{id:`300-000-livres`,term:`300,000 livres`,kind:`money`,chapter:78,text:`An income of 300,000 francs a year is immense wealth: a skilled worker might earn around 1,000 francs a year. ‘Livre’ was the old name for the franc and was still used for sums and incomes.`,quote:`an income of 300,000 livres`},{id:`lieutenant-general`,term:`lieutenant-general`,kind:`title`,chapter:78,text:`A senior general’s rank, just below marshal in the French army. Morcerf puts on his uniform and all his decorations (‘crosses’) to make his proposal formal.`,quote:`his uniform of lieutenant-general`},{id:`gosset-s-shooting-gallery`,term:`Gosset’s shooting-gallery`,kind:`place`,chapter:78,text:`A real pistol gallery near the Champs-Élysées where Parisian gentlemen kept their aim sharp. The Allée des Veuves (now the Avenue Montaigne) was then a quiet, shady lane.`,quote:`Gosset’s shooting-gallery`,placeId:`gosset-shooting-gallery`},{id:`second`,term:`second`,kind:`custom`,chapter:78,text:`In a duel each man named seconds, also called witnesses. They delivered the challenge, tried to arrange an apology, and if that failed they settled the place, the weapons and the rules.`,quote:`To be my second.`,seeAlso:[88,90,91]},{id:`retractation`,term:`retractation`,kind:`custom`,chapter:78,text:`A public withdrawal of a printed statement. Under the code of honour, a man who felt insulted in print could demand one, and could challenge the writer if he refused.`,quote:`an entire retractation`},{id:`the-independence-of-the-greeks`,term:`the independence of the Greeks`,kind:`history`,chapter:78,text:`The Greeks rose against Ottoman rule in 1821, and the war ended with an independent Greek kingdom in 1832. Ali Pasha, who was himself in revolt against the sultan, fell in 1822, which is why the count says ‘1822 or 1823’.`,quote:`he fought for the independence of the Greeks`},{id:`coming-of-age`,term:`coming of age`,kind:`custom`,chapter:79,text:`Under French law a person came of age at twenty-one. Until then a daughter needed her father’s consent to marry or to live under someone else’s roof.`,quote:`I shall wait till I am of age`},{id:`deep-mourning`,term:`deep mourning`,kind:`custom`,chapter:79,text:`After a close relative died, families wore black for many months. Valentine is in mourning for her grandparents.`,quote:`her deep mourning dress`},{id:`tartar-emetic`,term:`tartar emetic`,kind:`word`,chapter:79,text:`An antimony compound given to make a patient vomit. Ether and oil of turpentine were also used as stimulants and supposed antidotes.`,quote:`oil of turpentine and tartar emetic`},{id:`syrup-of-violets`,term:`syrup of violets`,kind:`word`,chapter:79,text:`A syrup made from violet petals. Like litmus paper, it changes colour when it meets certain substances, so it served as a simple chemical test.`,quote:`it turns syrup of violets green`},{id:`locusta-and-agrippina`,term:`Locusta and Agrippina`,kind:`history`,chapter:80,text:`Locusta was a notorious professional poisoner in 1st-century Rome. Agrippina, Nero’s mother, is said to have used her to poison the emperor Claudius.`,quote:`Locusta and Agrippina, living at the same time`},{id:`brunhilda-and-fredegund`,term:`Brunhilda and Fredegund`,kind:`history`,chapter:80,text:`Rival Frankish queens of the 6th century whose long feud was carried on through murders and assassinations.`,quote:`Brunhilda and Fredegund`},{id:`seek-whom-the-crime-will-profit`,term:`Seek whom the crime will profit`,kind:`reference`,chapter:80,text:`The old legal maxim ‘is fecit cui prodest’, Latin for ‘he did it who profits by it’. It is the first rule of detection: look for whoever gains.`,quote:`Seek whom the crime will profit`},{id:`polonius`,term:`Polonius`,kind:`reference`,chapter:80,text:`In Hamlet the old courtier Polonius is stabbed through a curtain by Hamlet, who takes him for the king. He dies in someone else’s place.`,quote:`like Shakespeare’s Polonius, he died for another`},{id:`brucine`,term:`brucine`,kind:`word`,chapter:80,text:`A bitter poison closely related to strychnine and taken from the same plant. Doctors prescribed tiny doses for paralysis, but larger ones kill.`,quote:`I have given M. Noirtier brucine`,seeAlso:[94,101,102]},{id:`the-scaffold`,term:`the scaffold`,kind:`custom`,chapter:80,text:`A death sentence. In France criminals were executed in public by guillotine.`,quote:`To the scaffold with the poisoner`,seeAlso:[108]},{id:`a-500-000-franc-dowry`,term:`a 500,000-franc dowry`,kind:`money`,chapter:81,text:`A huge sum, when a modest family might live on 2,000–3,000 francs a year. A dowry was the money a bride brought to her marriage, and the husband managed it.`,quote:`giving my daughter 500,000 francs as her dowry`,seeAlso:[95,96]},{id:`livery`,term:`livery`,kind:`word`,chapter:81,text:`The uniform worn by a household’s male servants. Dressed in his servant’s livery, Andrea passes unnoticed in the streets.`,quote:`You had a new livery yesterday?`},{id:`retired-baker`,term:`retired baker`,kind:`custom`,chapter:81,text:`The stock picture of a small Parisian shopkeeper living comfortably on an annuity in retirement. It is the modest respectability Caderousse claims to want.`,quote:`A retired baker?`},{id:`faubourg-saint-antoine`,term:`Faubourg Saint-Antoine`,kind:`place`,chapter:81,text:`A working-class district of eastern Paris. Picpus and the Rue Ménilmontant lay on the city’s poor eastern edge, far from Andrea’s fashionable haunts.`,quote:`walked down the Faubourg Saint-Antoine`},{id:`table-d-hote`,term:`table d’hôte`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:81,text:`French: ‘the host’s table’. A set meal served at fixed hours to all the guests of a hotel.`,quote:`the table d’hôte of the Hôtel des Princes`},{id:`cafe-de-paris`,term:`Café de Paris`,kind:`place`,chapter:81,text:`A luxurious restaurant on the Boulevard des Italiens, the most fashionable place to dine in Paris.`,quote:`or the Café de Paris`,seeAlso:[97,98]},{id:`codicil`,term:`codicil`,kind:`word`,chapter:81,text:`A later addition to a will that adds to it or changes it.`,quote:`are wills ever made without codicils?`,seeAlso:[90]},{id:`yellow-boys`,term:`yellow boys`,kind:`money`,chapter:81,text:`Slang for gold coins. A louis was a 20-franc gold piece, so twenty-five louis make 500 francs. Caderousse wants silver five-franc pieces instead, because a poor man paying in gold attracts suspicion.`,quote:`Yellow boys?`},{id:`huret-fichet`,term:`Huret & Fichet`,kind:`reference`,chapter:81,text:`A real firm of Paris locksmiths, famous for burglar-proof locks and safes.`,quote:`one of Huret & Fichet’s locks`},{id:`fiesco-and-the-moor`,term:`Fiesco and the Moor`,kind:`reference`,chapter:82,text:`In Schiller’s play Fiesco (1783), set in Genoa, the conspirator Fiesco catches the Moor hired to kill him and then takes him into his own service.`,quote:`over the Moor who would have killed him`},{id:`the-invalides`,term:`the Invalides`,kind:`place`,chapter:82,text:`The Hôtel des Invalides, Louis XIV’s great hospital for old soldiers. Its clock could be heard across western Paris.`,quote:`The clock of the Invalides struck`},{id:`nightingales`,term:`nightingales`,kind:`word`,chapter:82,text:`Thieves’ slang for skeleton keys.`,quote:`which thieves call nightingales`},{id:`louis-xvi-s-coat-of-mail`,term:`Louis XVI’s coat of mail`,kind:`history`,chapter:82,text:`Louis XVI was said to wear a mail vest for fear of assassins. He was guillotined in 1793, so the hatchet is the narrator’s figure of speech.`,quote:`was worn by King Louis XVI.`},{id:`place-de-greve`,term:`Place de Grève`,kind:`place`,chapter:82,text:`The square in front of the Paris Hôtel de Ville, where public executions took place for centuries.`,quote:`to the Place de Grève`},{id:`saint-mandrier`,term:`Saint-Mandrier`,kind:`place`,chapter:82,text:`A peninsula across the harbour from Toulon where convict gangs worked. Hyères is a coastal town a few miles to the east.`,quote:`We were working at Saint-Mandrier, near Toulon`},{id:`deposition`,term:`deposition`,kind:`word`,chapter:83,text:`A signed written statement to be used as evidence.`,quote:`Shall I write your deposition?`,seeAlso:[84]},{id:`guillotined`,term:`guillotined`,kind:`custom`,chapter:83,text:`Murder was a capital crime in France, and the guillotine had been the means of execution since 1792.`,quote:`And he will be guillotined, will he not?`},{id:`transported-for-life`,term:`transported for life`,kind:`custom`,chapter:83,text:`Caderousse’s crime could have cost him his head, but he got hard labour for life in the convict prison instead. A convicted felon lost his civil standing for good.`,quote:`to transport me for life, how merciful!`},{id:`the-morgue`,term:`the morgue`,kind:`place`,chapter:84,text:`The Paris Morgue, near Notre-Dame. Unidentified bodies were laid out there behind glass so that the public could view them and name them.`,quote:`the corpse was conveyed to the morgue`},{id:`registry`,term:`registry`,kind:`word`,chapter:84,text:`The court clerk’s office (the greffe), where evidence was stored.`,quote:`were deposited at the registry`},{id:`peer-of-france`,term:`peer of France`,kind:`title`,chapter:84,text:`A member of the Chamber of Peers, the upper house of parliament. Under King Louis-Philippe the king appointed peers for life.`,quote:`Count of Morcerf, peer of France`,seeAlso:[86]},{id:`visa`,term:`visa`,kind:`custom`,chapter:84,text:`Travellers had their passports stamped by the authorities along the route. Beauchamp’s stamps run from the republic of Geneva, through the Austrian-ruled kingdom of Lombardy–Venetia, to the Ottoman Empire.`,quote:`examine the visa`},{id:`two-million-crowns`,term:`two million crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:84,text:`A colossal bribe. The crown (écu) was an old silver coin worth three to six francs, so this means many millions of francs.`,quote:`surrendered the castle for two million crowns`},{id:`this-revolutionary-period`,term:`this revolutionary period`,kind:`history`,chapter:84,text:`The half-century of upheaval since 1789: the Revolution, the Empire, the Restoration and the July Revolution of 1830. Beauchamp suggests few men came through it with clean hands.`,quote:`this revolutionary period`},{id:`deo-ignoto`,term:`Deo ignoto`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:85,text:`Latin: ‘to the unknown god’. St Paul found an altar with this inscription in Athens (Acts 17:23). Albert will thank a benefactor he cannot name.`,quote:`I will erect an altar`},{id:`like-pilate`,term:`like Pilate`,kind:`reference`,chapter:85,text:`Pontius Pilate washed his hands in front of the crowd to disclaim responsibility for Jesus’s death (Matthew 27:24).`,quote:`like Pilate—washing my hands`},{id:`impresari`,term:`impresari`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:85,text:`Italian: opera and theatre managers. The Teatro Valle is a historic theatre in Rome.`,quote:`the director of the Valle Theatre`},{id:`britzka`,term:`britzka`,kind:`word`,chapter:85,text:`A long covered travelling carriage of Central European origin, with room to stretch out and sleep.`,quote:`a good travelling britzka`,seeAlso:[97,98]},{id:`borromean-islands`,term:`Borromean Islands`,kind:`place`,chapter:85,text:`Picturesque islands in Lake Maggiore in northern Italy, a classic tourist destination. Beauchamp names them to cover where he has really been.`,quote:`the Borromean Islands`},{id:`woman-is-fickle`,term:`Woman is fickle`,kind:`reference`,chapter:85,text:`King Francis I of France is said to have scratched ‘Souvent femme varie’ (‘woman often changes’) on a window at Chambord.`,quote:`Woman is fickle.`},{id:`the-railway-in-france`,term:`the railway in France`,kind:`history`,chapter:85,text:`France was slow to build railways. By the late 1830s it had only a few short lines, far behind Britain.`,quote:`surpass the railway, which would not be very difficult in France`,seeAlso:[95,96]},{id:`or-azure-gules`,term:`or, azure, gules`,kind:`word`,chapter:85,text:`Heraldic terms: or is gold, azure is blue, gules is red. A charge ‘in chief’ sits in the top band of the shield.`,quote:`a mountain _or_, on a sea _azure_`},{id:`the-sin-of-the-father`,term:`the sin of the father`,kind:`reference`,chapter:85,text:`From the Ten Commandments (Exodus 20:5), where God visits ‘the iniquity of the fathers upon the children unto the third and fourth generation’.`,quote:`to the third and fourth generation`,seeAlso:[89]},{id:`government-paper`,term:`government paper`,kind:`history`,chapter:86,text:`Under King Louis-Philippe the press was split between ‘ministerial’ papers that backed the government and opposition papers like Beauchamp’s.`,quote:`one that was well known as a government paper`},{id:`beet-sugar`,term:`beet-sugar`,kind:`history`,chapter:86,text:`A dull but hot political question of the 1830s. French beet sugar competed with colonial cane sugar, and its taxation was fiercely debated.`,quote:`a leading article in the same paper on beet-sugar`},{id:`tribune`,term:`tribune`,kind:`word`,chapter:86,text:`The raised platform from which members address a French legislative chamber.`,quote:`ascended the tribune`},{id:`the-egyptian-campaign`,term:`the Egyptian campaign`,kind:`history`,chapter:86,text:`Napoleon’s expedition to Egypt (1798–1801). Its soldiers and scholars brought back a knowledge of Arabic.`,quote:`the famous Egyptian campaign`},{id:`the-porte`,term:`the Porte`,kind:`title`,chapter:86,text:`The ‘Sublime Porte’: the Ottoman government, named after the gate of the grand vizier’s offices in Constantinople.`,quote:`his infamous bargain with the Porte`},{id:`the-hegira`,term:`the Hegira`,kind:`custom`,chapter:86,text:`The Islamic calendar counts its years from the Hegira, Muhammad’s move to Medina in AD 622. The year 1247 ran from mid-1831 to mid-1832.`,quote:`in the year 1247 of the Hegira`},{id:`grand-primate`,term:`grand primate`,kind:`title`,chapter:86,text:`The senior Orthodox bishop of the region, here certifying a baptism.`,quote:`the grand primate of Macedonia and Epirus`},{id:`virgil-s-goddesses`,term:`Virgil’s goddesses`,kind:`reference`,chapter:86,text:`In the Aeneid, Venus gives herself away as a goddess by the way she walks: ‘vera incessu patuit dea’.`,quote:`which Virgil attributes to his goddesses`},{id:`rue-de-vaugirard`,term:`Rue de Vaugirard`,kind:`place`,chapter:87,text:`The Chamber of Peers met in the Luxembourg Palace, which has an entrance on this Left Bank street.`,quote:`opening into the Rue de Vaugirard`,placeId:`chamber-of-peers`},{id:`babylon`,term:`Babylon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:87,text:`Since the Bible, Babylon has been a byword for a vast, worldly, pleasure-seeking city.`,quote:`this great Babylon of excitement`},{id:`throwing-the-glove`,term:`throwing the glove`,kind:`custom`,chapter:88,text:`Throwing a glove at a man, or in his face, was the classic gesture of challenge and a mortal insult. By taking the glove, the count accepts the challenge.`,quote:`I consider your glove thrown`},{id:`choice-of-arms`,term:`choice of arms`,kind:`custom`,chapter:88,text:`By duelling convention, the insulted party chose the weapons. The count gives up that right.`,quote:`I leave him the choice of arms`},{id:`bois-de-vincennes`,term:`Bois de Vincennes`,kind:`place`,chapter:88,text:`A large wood on the eastern edge of Paris and a favourite place for duels.`,quote:`in the Bois de Vincennes`,seeAlso:[90],placeId:`bois-de-vincennes`},{id:`lara-manfred-lord-ruthven`,term:`Lara, Manfred, Lord Ruthven`,kind:`reference`,chapter:88,text:`Brooding, mysterious heroes of Romantic fiction. Lara and Manfred come from Byron’s poems, and Lord Ruthven is the aristocratic vampire of Polidori’s The Vampyre (1819).`,quote:`a Lara, a Manfred, a Lord Ruthven`},{id:`duprez`,term:`Duprez`,kind:`history`,chapter:88,text:`Gilbert Duprez was the star tenor of the Paris Opera. From 1837 he was famous for the ringing high C he sang at full voice in Rossini’s William Tell.`,quote:`listen how adorably Duprez is singing`,seeAlso:[89]},{id:`william-tell`,term:`William Tell`,kind:`reference`,chapter:88,text:`Rossini’s 1829 grand opera about the Swiss hero. In it the young Arnold sings ‘O Mathilde, idole de mon âme’ (‘O Mathilde, idol of my soul’) to the princess he loves.`,quote:`O Mathilde! idole de mon âme!`},{id:`the-box-between-the-columns`,term:`the box between the columns`,kind:`word`,chapter:88,text:`At the Opera, men sat in the stalls (the ‘orchestra’) or the pit, while society rented boxes. A box between the columns was one of the most prominent in the house.`,quote:`the box between the columns`},{id:`suivez-moi`,term:`Suivez-moi!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:89,text:`French: ‘Follow me!’. It is Arnold’s rousing call to arms in William Tell, the aria where Duprez sang his famous high C.`},{id:`parquet-box`,term:`parquet box`,kind:`word`,chapter:89,text:`A theatre box at the level of the stalls (the parquet), close to the stage.`,quote:`concealed in a parquet box`},{id:`la-reserve`,term:`La Réserve`,kind:`place`,chapter:89,text:`The waterside tavern in Marseilles, from the book’s opening chapters, where the betrothal feast was held.`,quote:`under the arbor of La Réserve`},{id:`stipendiary`,term:`stipendiary`,kind:`word`,chapter:89,text:`Someone who serves for pay: a hired servant or mercenary.`,quote:`a stipendiary of Ali`},{id:`guzerat-and-ophir`,term:`Guzerat and Ophir`,kind:`reference`,chapter:89,text:`Gujarat in India was famed for pearls and gems. Ophir is the land in the Bible from which Solomon’s ships brought gold.`,quote:`the richest pearls of Guzerat and Ophir`},{id:`twenty-millions`,term:`twenty millions`,kind:`money`,chapter:90,text:`Twenty million francs: a staggering fortune, at a time when a family could live decently on 2,000–3,000 francs a year.`,quote:`the sum of twenty millions`},{id:`it-is-good-to-think-of-death`,term:`It is good to think of death`,kind:`reference`,chapter:90,text:`An echo of Ecclesiastes 7 (‘it is better to go to the house of mourning’) and the old maxim memento mori: ‘remember that you must die’.`,quote:`It is good to think of death.`},{id:`tortoni-s`,term:`Tortoni’s`,kind:`place`,chapter:90,text:`A celebrated café and ice-cream parlour on the Boulevard des Italiens, a haunt of fashionable young men.`,quote:`I went to Tortoni’s`},{id:`the-pistol-is-blind`,term:`the pistol is blind`,kind:`custom`,chapter:90,text:`A pistol duel was thought to depend more on luck than a sword fight did. Seconds bargained over the weapons, the distance, and who would fire first.`,quote:`the pistol is blind`},{id:`brutus-at-philippi`,term:`Brutus at Philippi`,kind:`reference`,chapter:90,text:`In Plutarch and in Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar, a ghost (Caesar’s, in Shakespeare) appears to Brutus before the battle of Philippi in 42 BC, where Brutus dies.`,quote:`Brutus the night before the battle of Philippi`},{id:`white-waistcoat`,term:`white waistcoat`,kind:`custom`,chapter:90,text:`A man facing pistols wore dark clothes buttoned to the chin so as not to offer a bright target. Albert’s open coat and white waistcoat look recklessly careless.`,quote:`painted a spot upon his heart`},{id:`a-duel-forsworn`,term:`a duel forsworn`,kind:`custom`,chapter:91,text:`A duel abandoned on the ground. Society tended to read it as cowardice, so the man who backed down could expect sneers, fresh challenges, or both.`,quote:`nothing induces serious duels so much as a duel forsworn`},{id:`feucheres-or-barye`,term:`Feuchères or Barye`,kind:`reference`,chapter:91,text:`Fashionable French sculptors of the day. Antoine-Louis Barye was especially admired for his small animal bronzes.`,quote:`bronzes by Feuchères or Barye`},{id:`rue-des-saints-peres`,term:`Rue des Saints-Pères`,kind:`place`,chapter:91,text:`A modest street on the Left Bank of Paris, a world away from the Morcerf mansion.`,quote:`a small furnished house to let in the Rue des Saints-Pères`,placeId:`rue-des-saints-peres`},{id:`a-hundred-and-fifty-louis`,term:`a hundred and fifty louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:91,text:`3,000 francs. That was a young sailor’s hard-won savings and enough to start a modest household, but a pittance beside the count’s millions.`,quote:`a hundred and fifty louis`},{id:`allees-de-meilhan`,term:`Allées de Meilhan`,kind:`place`,chapter:91,text:`A tree-lined avenue in Marseilles, where the Dantès family’s little house stood.`,quote:`on the Allées de Meilhan`,placeId:`old-dantes-house`},{id:`convent`,term:`convent`,kind:`custom`,chapter:91,text:`Widowed or ruined women of good family often withdrew to a convent, as a nun or as a paying resident. A nun was usually expected to bring a dowry.`,quote:`I shall take with me to some convent`},{id:`barriere-du-trone`,term:`Barrière du Trône`,kind:`place`,chapter:92,text:`One of the toll gates in the wall around Paris, on the road in from Vincennes.`,quote:`At the Barrière du Trône`},{id:`place-royale`,term:`Place Royale`,kind:`place`,chapter:92,text:`Today’s Place des Vosges, an elegant 17th-century square in the Marais district.`,quote:`at the end of the Place Royale`},{id:`the-french-army-in-spain`,term:`the French army in Spain`,kind:`history`,chapter:92,text:`In 1823 France invaded Spain to restore King Ferdinand VII’s absolute power against the Spanish liberals.`,quote:`guide and spy to the French army in Spain`},{id:`panacea`,term:`panacea`,kind:`word`,chapter:93,text:`A cure-all remedy.`,quote:`Grandpapa says it is a panacea.`},{id:`prince-cavalcanti`,term:`Prince Cavalcanti`,kind:`title`,chapter:93,text:`A prince outranks a count. Danglars, a self-styled ‘popular banker’, finds the grander title more useful.`,quote:`Danglars kept up the title of prince.`,seeAlso:[96]},{id:`betrothal-festival`,term:`betrothal festival`,kind:`custom`,chapter:93,text:`A wealthy French wedding was preceded by the signing of the marriage contract, which fixed the dowry and property arrangements, at a grand evening reception.`,quote:`a grand feast, a betrothal festival`,seeAlso:[96]},{id:`exterminating-angel`,term:`exterminating angel`,kind:`reference`,chapter:94,text:`The destroying angel who struck down the firstborn of Egypt (Exodus 12).`,quote:`some exterminating angel`},{id:`vehmgericht`,term:`Vehmgericht`,kind:`history`,chapter:94,text:`The secret ‘Vehmic courts’ of medieval Westphalia, which judged offenders in secret and had them executed.`,quote:`there is no longer a Vehmgericht`},{id:`sterne`,term:`Sterne`,kind:`reference`,chapter:94,text:`Laurence Sterne (1713–68), the English author of Tristram Shandy and A Sentimental Journey.`,quote:`as Sterne said`},{id:`the-house-of-the-atreidae`,term:`the house of the Atreidae`,kind:`reference`,chapter:94,text:`The cursed family of Atreus in Greek myth, the line of Agamemnon and Orestes. For generations its members murdered one another.`,quote:`like the house of the Atreidae`},{id:`algeria`,term:`Algeria`,kind:`history`,chapter:94,text:`France began conquering Algeria in 1830, and the fighting dragged on for years. Maximilian served there as a cavalry officer.`,quote:`the murderous nights of Algeria`},{id:`putting-the-dots-on-the-i`,term:`putting the dots on the i`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:94,text:`A French idiom, ‘mettre les points sur les i’: to spell something out exactly.`},{id:`hotel`,term:`hotel`,kind:`word`,chapter:94,text:`From the French hôtel: a grand private town house, not an inn.`,quote:`the house adjoining the hotel of M. de Villefort`,seeAlso:[99]},{id:`three-six-or-nine-years`,term:`three, six, or nine years`,kind:`custom`,chapter:94,text:`The standard French lease, which the tenant could end at each three-year mark.`,quote:`for three, six, or nine years`},{id:`ph-drus-and-bias`,term:`Phædrus and Bias`,kind:`reference`,chapter:95,text:`‘Nothing too much’ was a Greek maxim carved at Delphi. ‘I carry all my goods with me’ is credited to the sage Bias, who fled his city with nothing. Phaedrus was a Roman writer of fables.`,quote:`one is, I believe, from Phædrus, and the other from Bias`},{id:`porte-saint-martin-and-gaite`,term:`Porte Saint-Martin and Gaîté`,kind:`place`,chapter:95,text:`Paris boulevard theatres famous for melodrama, full of tyrannical stage fathers.`,quote:`the fathers of the Porte Saint-Martin and Gaîté`},{id:`the-provident-law`,term:`the provident law`,kind:`custom`,chapter:95,text:`Under the Napoleonic Code, a fixed share of a parent’s estate had to go to the children. A marriage was not valid without the bride’s own consent.`,quote:`the provident law has deprived you of the power to disinherit me`},{id:`pasta-malibran-grisi`,term:`Pasta, Malibran, Grisi`,kind:`history`,chapter:95,text:`Giuditta Pasta, Maria Malibran and Giulia Grisi were the great opera sopranos of the day, and they commanded enormous fees.`,quote:`like Pasta, Malibran, Grisi`},{id:`law-s-mississippi-scheme`,term:`Law’s Mississippi scheme`,kind:`history`,chapter:95,text:`John Law’s Mississippi Company (1716–20) set off a frenzy of speculation in Paris, then collapsed and ruined thousands.`,quote:`the fantastic Mississippi scheme`},{id:`a-grant-of-a-railway`,term:`a grant of a railway`,kind:`history`,chapter:95,text:`The French state granted private companies concessions to build and run railway lines. In the 1840s this set off a speculative boom.`,quote:`obtained a grant of a railway`,seeAlso:[96]},{id:`drafts-on-the-treasury`,term:`drafts on the treasury`,kind:`money`,chapter:95,text:`Treasury bills: government IOUs, as good as cash.`,quote:`two drafts on the treasury`},{id:`the-mayor-s`,term:`the mayor’s`,kind:`custom`,chapter:95,text:`In France the legally binding marriage is the civil ceremony at the town hall (mairie). The church wedding follows it.`,quote:`on returning from the mayor’s`},{id:`brabantio-s-malediction`,term:`Brabantio’s malediction`,kind:`reference`,chapter:95,text:`Desdemona’s father curses her for marrying against his wishes, in Shakespeare’s Othello and in Rossini’s opera Otello, which Paris loved.`,quote:`Brabantio’s malediction on Desdemona`},{id:`a-thousand-francs-monthly`,term:`a thousand francs monthly`,kind:`money`,chapter:95,text:`Eugénie’s pocket money alone, 12,000 francs a year, is many times a skilled worker’s annual wage.`,quote:`the thousand francs I give you monthly`},{id:`dorante-valere-alceste`,term:`Dorante, Valère, Alceste`,kind:`reference`,chapter:96,text:`Molière’s characters, played at the Théâtre-Français (the Comédie-Française). Dorante and Valère are stock names for smooth young gentlemen, and Alceste is the blunt hero of The Misanthrope.`,quote:`Dorante or Valère reply to Alceste`},{id:`seraglio`,term:`seraglio`,kind:`word`,chapter:96,text:`A harem. The count is sending Andrea up with mock ‘Eastern scruples’.`,quote:`I, who have a seraglio at Cairo`},{id:`signing-the-contract`,term:`signing the contract`,kind:`custom`,chapter:96,text:`At a society wedding, the notaries read the marriage contract aloud at a reception. The two families, then distinguished guests, added their signatures as an honour.`,quote:`the signature of the contract`},{id:`academician`,term:`Academician`,kind:`title`,chapter:96,text:`A member of the Académie française, the official guardian of the French language. The narrator uses the word for a maker of elegant phrases.`,quote:`An Academician would say`},{id:`endymion`,term:`Endymion`,kind:`reference`,chapter:96,text:`The beautiful shepherd of Greek myth, cast into an endless sleep and loved by the Moon. He was a favourite subject for ornate clocks.`,quote:`representing Endymion asleep`},{id:`boileau`,term:`Boileau`,kind:`reference`,chapter:96,text:`Nicolas Boileau, the 17th-century poet and critic who laid down the rules of French classical taste.`,quote:`what Boileau calls`},{id:`qu-rens-quem-devoret`,term:`quærens quem devoret`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:96,text:`Latin: ‘seeking whom he may devour’. The phrase comes from 1 Peter 5:8, where the devil prowls ‘as a roaring lion’.`},{id:`girded-with-his-scarf`,term:`girded with his scarf`,kind:`custom`,chapter:96,text:`A police commissary on official duty wore a tricolour sash as his badge of office.`,quote:`girded with his scarf`},{id:`cholera-morbus`,term:`cholera morbus`,kind:`history`,chapter:97,text:`Cholera swept Paris in 1832 and killed some 18,000 people. It remained a byword for sudden terror and flight.`,quote:`a case of plague or of cholera morbus`},{id:`spanish-funds-haitian-bonds`,term:`Spanish funds, Haitian bonds`,kind:`history`,chapter:97,text:`Foreign government bonds traded on the Paris Bourse. Spanish bonds swung wildly with Spain’s civil war, and Haiti’s 1825 loan, raised to pay France an indemnity for recognition, was notoriously shaky.`,quote:`the rise and fall of Spanish funds, of Haitian bonds`},{id:`passport`,term:`passport`,kind:`custom`,chapter:97,text:`Travel, even inside France, required a passport describing the bearer, and post-houses would not supply horses without one.`,quote:`M. Léon d’Armilly, twenty years of age`,seeAlso:[98]},{id:`hercules-and-omphale`,term:`Hercules and Omphale`,kind:`reference`,chapter:97,text:`In Greek myth the hero Hercules served Queen Omphale as a slave, and the two swapped clothes and roles.`,quote:`I was Hercules, and you only the pale Omphale`},{id:`amazon`,term:`Amazon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:97,text:`The warrior women of Greek legend.`,quote:`You are a perfect Amazon`},{id:`judith-or-a-delilah`,term:`Judith or a Delilah`,kind:`reference`,chapter:97,text:`Bold women of the Bible. Judith beheaded the enemy general Holofernes, and Delilah overcame Samson by cutting his hair.`,quote:`as strong as a Judith or a Delilah`},{id:`twenty-louis`,term:`twenty louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:97,text:`400 francs: a large bribe for a laundress, perhaps half a year’s earnings.`,quote:`she had been promised twenty louis`},{id:`barriere-saint-martin`,term:`Barrière Saint-Martin`,kind:`place`,chapter:97,text:`A toll gate in the wall around Paris, on the road north. Just beyond it lay the village of La Villette.`,quote:`the Barrière Saint-Martin`,placeId:`barriere-saint-martin`},{id:`corbeille`,term:`corbeille`,kind:`custom`,chapter:98,text:`The trousseau is the bride’s own linen and clothes. The corbeille (‘basket’) holds the bridegroom’s gifts to her of jewels, lace and shawls, which were put on display for the guests.`},{id:`valenciennes-lace`,term:`Valenciennes lace`,kind:`word`,chapter:98,text:`Fine, costly bobbin lace from Valenciennes in northern France. Shawls of Indian cashmere were equally prized luxury gifts.`,quote:`cashmere shawls, Valenciennes lace`},{id:`louvres`,term:`Louvres`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`A village on the main road north from Paris towards Senlis and Flanders, not the museum.`,quote:`Towards the Louvres.`},{id:`department-of-the-oise`,term:`department of the Oise`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`The administrative department just north of Paris, around Senlis and Compiègne.`,quote:`the department of the Oise`},{id:`compiegne`,term:`Compiègne`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`A town about 80 km north of Paris, with a royal palace and a great forest.`,quote:`Compiègne, that royal residence and fortified town`,placeId:`compiegne`},{id:`achilles-with-deidamia`,term:`Achilles with Deidamia`,kind:`reference`,chapter:98,text:`In Greek myth Achilles was hidden on Scyros, disguised as a girl among the king’s daughters, and there he loved the princess Deidamia.`,quote:`which represented Achilles with Deidamia`},{id:`brigadier`,term:`brigadier`,kind:`title`,chapter:98,text:`In the gendarmerie, France’s rural military police, a brigadier is a corporal commanding a small post, not a general.`,quote:`who was a brigadier`},{id:`the-assizes`,term:`the assizes`,kind:`custom`,chapter:98,text:`The Court of Assizes, where serious crimes were tried before a jury.`,quote:`an arrest meant the assizes`},{id:`nero-s-wish`,term:`Nero’s wish`,kind:`reference`,chapter:98,text:`The wish that all Rome had a single neck is usually credited to Caligula, not Nero (Suetonius, Life of Caligula).`,quote:`made Nero wish that the Roman world`},{id:`conciergerie`,term:`Conciergerie`,kind:`place`,chapter:98,text:`The old prison inside the Palais de Justice in Paris, where prisoners were held before trial.`,quote:`incarcerated in the Conciergerie`,seeAlso:[108]},{id:`featherless-biped`,term:`featherless biped`,kind:`reference`,chapter:99,text:`Plato is said to have defined man as a two-legged animal without feathers; Diogenes the Cynic mocked him by holding up a plucked chicken: “Here is Plato’s man.” Eugénie, in other words, barely counts Debray as human.`,quote:`animals upon two legs without feathers`},{id:`grisette`,term:`grisette`,kind:`word`,chapter:99,text:`A young working-class Parisian woman, typically a seamstress or shop girl, stereotyped in fiction as pretty, lively and free with her affections.`,quote:`like prosperous grisettes`},{id:`lazaretto`,term:`lazaretto`,kind:`word`,chapter:99,text:`A quarantine hospital where people suspected of carrying plague or other contagious disease were isolated.`,quote:`a lazaretto infected with the plague`},{id:`contempt-of-court`,term:`contempt of court`,kind:`word`,chapter:99,text:`A loose translation of the French contumace: trying and sentencing an accused person who has fled and is absent. Madame Danglars hopes Andrea will escape and be condemned only on paper.`,quote:`that shadow of guilt called contempt of court`},{id:`draught`,term:`draught`,kind:`word`,chapter:100,text:`A dose of liquid medicine, drunk in one go. Valentine’s “night draught” is the calming drink prepared for her each evening.`,quote:`bringing the night draught prepared for the young girl`},{id:`bell-pull`,term:`bell-pull`,kind:`word`,chapter:100,text:`A cord or tasselled strip hanging by the bed or fireplace, connected to a bell in the servants’ quarters.`,quote:`seized the silken bell-pull`},{id:`cambric`,term:`cambric`,kind:`word`,chapter:100,text:`A fine, thin white linen (originally from Cambrai), used for handkerchiefs, nightclothes and good shirts.`,quote:`the embroidered cambric`},{id:`locusta`,term:`Locusta`,kind:`reference`,chapter:101,text:`A notorious professional poisoner of first-century Rome. She was said to have supplied the poison that killed Emperor Claudius and, for Nero, his stepbrother Britannicus. Her name became a byword for a female poisoner.`},{id:`saint-philippe-du-roule`,term:`Saint-Philippe-du-Roule`,kind:`place`,chapter:101,text:`A church in the Faubourg Saint-Honoré, the wealthy Paris district where the Villeforts live; its clock is the one Valentine hears.`,quote:`slower than that of Saint-Philippe-du-Roule`,placeId:`saint-philippe-du-roule`},{id:`200-000-livres-a-year`,term:`200,000 livres a year`,kind:`money`,chapter:101,text:`An annual income, not a lump sum; by this period “livre” was used interchangeably with the franc. At the usual 5% return it implies a fortune of around four million francs, when a labourer might earn perhaps 2 francs a day.`,quote:`you have 200,000 livres a year`},{id:`pastille`,term:`pastille`,kind:`word`,chapter:101,text:`A small lozenge or pill.`,quote:`a pastille about the size of a pea`},{id:`night-light`,term:`night-light`,kind:`word`,chapter:102,text:`A simple night lamp: a floating wick burning on a thin layer of oil poured over water, giving a dim glow for hours until the oil runs out.`,quote:`last drops of oil which floated on the surface of the water`},{id:`germain-pillon-s-graces`,term:`Germain Pillon’s “Graces”`,kind:`reference`,chapter:102,text:`Germain Pillon (c. 1525–1590) was a leading French Renaissance sculptor. His marble Three Graces, which once held the heart of King Henri II, is in the Louvre and is famous for its graceful figures.`,quote:`Germain Pillon’s “Graces,”`},{id:`nitric-acid-test`,term:`nitric acid test`,kind:`history`,chapter:102,text:`A real chemical test of the time: nitric acid turns brucine and related plant poisons a vivid red, so d’Avrigny can identify the poison from the dregs.`,quote:`a small bottle of nitric acid`},{id:`doctor-of-the-dead`,term:`doctor of the dead`,kind:`custom`,chapter:103,text:`In Paris a municipal doctor had to examine every body and certify the death before a burial permit was issued, partly to prevent burying anyone alive and partly to spot crimes.`,quote:`is expressly named “the doctor of the dead.”`},{id:`chloride-of-lime`,term:`chloride of lime`,kind:`word`,chapter:103,text:`Bleaching powder, widely used in the nineteenth century as a disinfectant, especially after touching the sick or the dead.`,quote:`in chloride of lime`},{id:`cassock`,term:`cassock`,kind:`word`,chapter:103,text:`The long, close-fitting black robe worn by Catholic priests.`,quote:`a man in a cassock`},{id:`winding-sheet`,term:`winding-sheet`,kind:`word`,chapter:104,text:`A shroud: the cloth in which a body was wrapped for burial.`,quote:`wrapped the corpse in the winding-sheet`},{id:`spanish-haitian-or-neapolitan-bonds`,term:`Spanish, Haitian, or Neapolitan bonds`,kind:`history`,chapter:104,text:`Foreign government loans traded on the Paris Bourse. Spain and Haiti in particular had a record of missed payments, so the count’s question is a polite jab at risky paper.`,quote:`Are they Spanish, Haitian, or Neapolitan bonds?`},{id:`payable-to-bearer`,term:`payable to bearer`,kind:`money`,chapter:104,text:`Paper that anyone holding it can cash, like banknotes. Each of these five notes is worth a million francs: together, about the yearly wages of several thousand labourers.`,quote:`payable to bearer`},{id:`cr-sus`,term:`Crœsus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:104,text:`King of Lydia in the sixth century BC, so famously rich that “rich as Croesus” became a proverb.`,quote:`what a Crœsus you are!`},{id:`rothschild-and-lafitte`,term:`Rothschild and Lafitte`,kind:`history`,chapter:104,text:`The two great Paris banking houses of the day: James de Rothschild’s, and that of Jacques Laffitte, banker and briefly prime minister in 1830–31.`,quote:`one on Rothschild, one on Lafitte`,seeAlso:[106]},{id:`receiver-general-of-the-charities`,term:`Receiver-General of the charities`,kind:`title`,chapter:104,text:`The official who collected and held the funds of the Paris hospitals and poor-relief charities.`,quote:`Receiver-General of the charities`},{id:`discount`,term:`discount`,kind:`money`,chapter:104,text:`A bill payable elsewhere or later could be sold at once to a banker for slightly less than its face value; the difference was the banker’s fee. Danglars suggests losing 5,000–6,000 francs to get the money early.`,quote:`a discount of 5,000 or 6,000 francs`},{id:`robert-macaire`,term:`Robert Macaire`,kind:`reference`,chapter:104,text:`A swaggering, cynical swindler from the melodrama L’Auberge des Adrets (1823), turned into a comic hit by the actor Frédérick Lemaître. His name became shorthand for a brazen crook.`,quote:`Robert Macaire represented by Frédérick`},{id:`abelard-and-heloise`,term:`Abélard and Héloïse`,kind:`reference`,chapter:105,text:`The twelfth-century philosopher Peter Abelard and his brilliant pupil Héloïse, whose love affair ended in his castration and their separation into religious life. Their remains were moved to Père-Lachaise in 1817, and the tomb became a shrine for lovers.`,quote:`the tomb of Abélard and Héloïse`},{id:`malherbe-to-du-perier`,term:`Malherbe to Du Périer`,kind:`reference`,chapter:105,text:`François de Malherbe’s famous poem of consolation (c. 1598) to a friend whose young daughter had died, with its line that she lived as roses live, “the space of a morning”. Funeral orators borrowed from it endlessly.`,quote:`the stanzas of Malherbe to Du Périer`},{id:`coupe`,term:`coupé`,kind:`word`,chapter:105,text:`A small closed carriage for two. In a public stagecoach, the coupé was the front compartment, the most comfortable and expensive seats.`,quote:`alighting from his _coupé_`,seeAlso:[106]},{id:`jointure`,term:`jointure`,kind:`word`,chapter:106,text:`Property or income settled on a wife by her marriage contract, meant to provide for her. Debray suggests she make a show of giving hers up.`,quote:`giving up your jointure`},{id:`a-wife-s-property`,term:`a wife’s property`,kind:`custom`,chapter:106,text:`Under the Napoleonic Code a married woman could not own or manage property in her own right without her husband’s authority, which is why Debray has hidden her winnings in cash.`,quote:`you have no right to possess anything independent of your husband`},{id:`diligence`,term:`diligence`,kind:`word`,chapter:106,text:`The big public stagecoach that carried passengers and mail between towns, with several compartments at different prices.`,quote:`the diligences and steamboats`},{id:`chalons`,term:`Châlons`,kind:`place`,chapter:106,text:`Chalon-sur-Saône, where travellers from Paris could board steamboats down the Saône and Rhône to Lyons and Avignon: the fastest route south in the 1830s.`,quote:`the _coupé_ to Châlons`,seeAlso:[112],placeId:`chalon-sur-saone`},{id:`3-000-francs`,term:`3,000 francs`,kind:`money`,chapter:106,text:`Albert’s budget is a useful scale: 35 francs for a coach seat, 114 for his mother’s whole journey to Marseilles, and about 2,000 francs a year for a modest life. Compare Debray’s million.`,quote:`capital 3,000 francs`},{id:`substitute`,term:`substitute`,kind:`custom`,chapter:106,text:`French conscription was by lot, and a man drawn to serve could legally pay someone else to take his place. Albert has sold himself as such a substitute.`,quote:`engaged myself as substitute in the Spahis`},{id:`spahis`,term:`Spahis`,kind:`history`,chapter:106,text:`Light cavalry raised in French-occupied Algeria in the 1830s, largely from local horsemen under French officers. France had invaded Algiers in 1830 and was still fighting for control of the country.`,quote:`substitute in the Spahis`},{id:`lamoriciere-changarnier-bedeau`,term:`Lamoricière, Changarnier, Bedeau`,kind:`history`,chapter:106,text:`Young French officers who were making their names in the Algerian campaigns. Ney, Napoleon’s marshal, was the proverbial model of battlefield courage.`,quote:`Has Lamoricière, that Ney of the South, been killed?`},{id:`deniers`,term:`deniers`,kind:`money`,chapter:106,text:`The denier was an old, tiny coin, a twelfth of a sou; “a few deniers” means next to nothing.`,quote:`rich with a few deniers`},{id:`la-force`,term:`La Force`,kind:`place`,chapter:107,text:`A notorious Paris prison in the Marais district, used for those awaiting trial. It was demolished in the 1840s.`,quote:`One division of La Force`,placeId:`la-force`},{id:`barrier-saint-jacques`,term:`barrier Saint-Jacques`,kind:`history`,chapter:107,text:`From 1832 public executions by guillotine in Paris took place at the Barrière Saint-Jacques, on the city’s southern edge.`,quote:`the barrier Saint-Jacques`},{id:`la-savate`,term:`La savate`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:107,text:`French: “the old shoe”. The narrator explains the beating it names; the anguille (“eel”) is a sandbag cosh.`,quote:`_“La savate—La savate!”_`},{id:`examining-magistrate`,term:`examining magistrate`,kind:`title`,chapter:107,text:`The juge d’instruction, who investigates a case before trial, questions the accused and witnesses, and decides whether to send it to court.`,quote:`the examining magistrate is waiting for the prisoner`,seeAlso:[108]},{id:`we-burn`,term:`we burn`,kind:`custom`,chapter:107,text:`From children’s hide-and-seek games: “you’re burning”, like “you’re getting warm”, means close to the hidden thing.`,quote:`we burn, as they say at the game of pincette`},{id:`as-m-racine-says`,term:`as M. Racine says`,kind:`reference`,chapter:107,text:`An echo of Racine’s tragedy Phèdre (1677), where Phèdre, confessing her forbidden love, tells her nurse: “It is you who have named him.”`,quote:`’Tis you who have named him, as M. Racine says`},{id:`pixerecourt`,term:`Pixérécourt`,kind:`reference`,chapter:107,text:`Guilbert de Pixérécourt (1773–1844), the father of French melodrama, whose plays were full of long-lost children tearfully recognising their parents.`,quote:`like Monsieur Pixérécourt`},{id:`salad-basket`,term:`salad basket`,kind:`word`,chapter:107,text:`Slang (panier à salade) for the police van carrying prisoners, from its grated, basket-like sides.`,quote:`which they call “the salad basket.”`},{id:`tarquin`,term:`Tarquin`,kind:`reference`,chapter:108,text:`Tarquin the Proud, last king of Rome, answered his son’s messenger by silently striking off the tallest poppies in his garden: a signal to kill the leading men of a conquered town.`,quote:`Tarquin lopped off the tallest poppies`},{id:`the-palais`,term:`the Palais`,kind:`place`,chapter:108,text:`The Palais de Justice on the Île de la Cité, the main law courts of Paris, where the assizes sit.`,quote:`To the Palais.`,seeAlso:[109,111]},{id:`cafe-de-paris-boulevard-de-gand`,term:`Café de Paris, Boulevard de Gand`,kind:`place`,chapter:109,text:`Haunts of fashionable young Paris. The Boulevard de Gand was the dandies’ nickname for the Boulevard des Italiens, and the Café de Paris was its smartest restaurant.`,quote:`Frequenting the Café de Paris, the Boulevard de Gand`,seeAlso:[115]},{id:`dante-for-a-genealogist`,term:`Dante for a genealogist`,kind:`reference`,chapter:109,text:`The Cavalcanti were a real noble family of Florence: the poet Guido Cavalcanti was Dante’s friend, and his father appears in Dante’s Inferno.`,quote:`who could boast of Dante for a genealogist`},{id:`phrenology`,term:`phrenology`,kind:`history`,chapter:109,text:`A popular pseudo-science claiming that character could be read from the shape of the skull. The skulls of executed criminals were eagerly studied for “organs” of murder and cunning.`,quote:`experiments that will be made on his phrenological organs`},{id:`saint-simon`,term:`Saint-Simon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:109,text:`The Duc de Saint-Simon (1675–1755), whose vast, vivid memoirs of Louis XIV’s court are full of unusual words.`,quote:`you’ll find the word in Saint-Simon`},{id:`beaumarchais`,term:`Beaumarchais`,kind:`reference`,chapter:109,text:`The playwright of The Barber of Seville and The Marriage of Figaro, whose trial scene features a shrill-voiced court usher.`,quote:`ever since the days of Beaumarchais`},{id:`the-president`,term:`the president`,kind:`title`,chapter:110,text:`The presiding judge of the assize court. In French procedure he questions the accused directly, starting with name, age and birthplace.`,quote:`“Gendarmes,” said the president`},{id:`indictment`,term:`indictment`,kind:`custom`,chapter:110,text:`The acte d’accusation, written by the prosecutor and read aloud at the opening of the trial, setting out the crime and the accused’s whole past.`,quote:`The president called for the indictment`},{id:`medusa`,term:`Medusa`,kind:`reference`,chapter:110,text:`The Gorgon of Greek myth, whose face turned anyone who looked at it to stone.`,quote:`the head of Medusa`},{id:`extenuating-circumstances`,term:`extenuating circumstances`,kind:`history`,chapter:110,text:`Since 1832 French juries could find circonstances atténuantes, which reduced the sentence and usually spared the accused from the guillotine.`,quote:`There will be extenuating circumstances`},{id:`garb-of-nessus`,term:`garb of Nessus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:111,text:`The centaur Nessus, dying, tricked Hercules’ wife into keeping his poisoned blood; the shirt she later soaked in it burned Hercules alive.`,quote:`a veritable garb of Nessus`},{id:`cholera`,term:`cholera`,kind:`history`,chapter:111,text:`Cholera swept Paris in 1832, killing some 18,000 people, so it was a vivid, recent image of contagion.`,quote:`the typhus fever, the cholera, the plague`},{id:`the-titans-ajax`,term:`the Titans … Ajax`,kind:`reference`,chapter:111,text:`In Greek myth the Titans made war on the gods of Olympus, and Ajax the Lesser, saved from shipwreck, boasted he had escaped despite the gods and was drowned for it.`,quote:`Ajax to defy the gods`},{id:`perrault-s-wicked-fairies`,term:`Perrault’s wicked fairies`,kind:`reference`,chapter:112,text:`In Charles Perrault’s fairy tales (1697), most famously Sleeping Beauty, a fairy left off the guest list arrives at the christening to lay a curse.`,quote:`the wicked fairies in Perrault’s stories`},{id:`villejuif`,term:`Villejuif`,kind:`place`,chapter:112,text:`A hill just south of Paris on the main road to Fontainebleau and the south, with a view back over the whole city.`,quote:`the top of the hill Villejuif`,placeId:`villejuif`},{id:`modern-babylon`,term:`modern Babylon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:112,text:`Babylon, the great city of the Bible and especially the Book of Revelation, is a traditional byword for a vast, rich and corrupt capital.`,quote:`this modern Babylon`},{id:`tyre-and-carthage`,term:`Tyre and Carthage`,kind:`history`,chapter:112,text:`Marseilles was founded around 600 BC by Greek traders from Phocaea; Tyre and Carthage were the great Phoenician trading cities that ruled Mediterranean commerce before it.`,quote:`the younger sister of Tyre and Carthage`},{id:`fort-saint-nicolas`,term:`Fort Saint-Nicolas`,kind:`place`,chapter:112,text:`One of the two forts guarding the entrance to Marseilles’ Old Port. The City Hall on the quay carries carving by Pierre Puget, the city’s great Baroque sculptor.`,quote:`Fort Saint-Nicolas, the City Hall designed by Puget`,placeId:`fort-saint-nicolas`},{id:`the-consigne`,term:`the Consigne`,kind:`place`,chapter:113,text:`The harbour health office at Marseilles, which checked arriving ships for disease; prisoners were also embarked from its quay.`,quote:`advanced to the Consigne`,placeId:`consigne-marseilles`},{id:`revolution-of-july`,term:`revolution of July`,kind:`history`,chapter:113,text:`The July Revolution of 1830, three days of street fighting in Paris that drove out King Charles X and put Louis-Philippe on the throne.`,quote:`since the revolution of July`},{id:`mirabeau`,term:`Mirabeau`,kind:`history`,chapter:113,text:`The great orator of the French Revolution was held in the Château d’If in 1774 at his own father’s request. When he died in 1791, rumours spread that he had been poisoned.`,quote:`the poisoning of Mirabeau`},{id:`meridian`,term:`meridian`,kind:`word`,chapter:113,text:`A line marking the sun’s noon position: Faria’s homemade sundial for telling the time in his cell.`,quote:`the meridian, drawn by the abbé on the wall`},{id:`the-dragons-teeth`,term:`the dragons’ teeth`,kind:`reference`,chapter:113,text:`An adaptation of the Psalms: “thou shalt tread upon the lion and adder: the young lion and the dragon shalt thou trample under feet” (Psalm 91), with an echo of “break their teeth” (Psalm 58).`,quote:`Thou shalt tear out the dragons’ teeth`},{id:`bastia`,term:`Bastia`,kind:`place`,chapter:113,text:`The main port of northern Corsica, the nearest large harbour to the island of Monte Cristo.`,quote:`in the port of Bastia`,placeId:`bastia`},{id:`eurus`,term:`Eurus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:113,text:`The Greek god of the east wind.`,quote:`it will be called the _Eurus_`},{id:`ganymede`,term:`Ganymede`,kind:`reference`,chapter:113,text:`In Greek myth, a beautiful Trojan youth carried up to Olympus by Zeus in the form of an eagle, to serve as cupbearer to the gods.`,quote:`The story of Ganymede, Maximilian, is not a fable`},{id:`legion-of-honor`,term:`Legion of Honor`,kind:`history`,chapter:114,text:`France’s highest decoration, founded by Napoleon in 1802; members wore its red ribbon in the buttonhole.`,quote:`the ribbon of the Legion of Honor`},{id:`the-goddam-of-figaro`,term:`the “goddam” of Figaro`,kind:`reference`,chapter:114,text:`In Beaumarchais’ Marriage of Figaro, Figaro jokes that “God-damn” is the whole basis of the English language: one word gets you anything.`,quote:`like the “goddam” of Figaro`},{id:`la-storta`,term:`La Storta`,kind:`place`,chapter:114,text:`The last post station on the road from Florence, where travellers caught their first sight of Rome and the dome of St. Peter’s.`,quote:`On reaching La Storta`,placeId:`la-storta`},{id:`descendants-of-marius-and-the-gracchi`,term:`descendants of Marius and the Gracchi`,kind:`reference`,chapter:114,text:`An ironic salute to Rome’s street children as heirs of the Republic’s popular heroes: the general Gaius Marius and the reforming Gracchus brothers.`,quote:`Ten young descendants of Marius and the Gracchi`},{id:`papal-states`,term:`Papal States`,kind:`history`,chapter:114,text:`Rome and a broad band of central Italy were ruled by the Pope as a sovereign kingdom until 1870, with their own government and police.`,quote:`little vagabonds from the Papal States`,seeAlso:[116]},{id:`dentro-la-testa`,term:`Dentro la testa!`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:114,text:`Italian: “Heads in!” Danglars also hears Non capisco (“I don’t understand”), Scendi! (“Get down!”), Di quà (“This way”), Avanti! (“Forward!”) and Ecco! (“There!”).`,quote:`“_Dentro la testa!_” answered a solemn and imperious voice`},{id:`the-appian-way`,term:`the Appian Way`,kind:`place`,chapter:114,text:`The ancient road south from Rome, lined with tombs. Beneath the nearby church of San Sebastiano run early Christian catacombs: miles of burial tunnels cut into the soft rock.`,quote:`he must be on the Appian Way`},{id:`plutarch-s-life-of-alexander`,term:`Plutarch’s Life of Alexander`,kind:`reference`,chapter:114,text:`One of the Greek writer Plutarch’s Parallel Lives of famous Greeks and Romans, a favourite of ambitious readers. Vampa likes his heroes: earlier he was reading Caesar’s own war memoirs.`,quote:`attentively reading Plutarch’s _Life of Alexander_`},{id:`8-000-crowns`,term:`8,000 crowns`,kind:`money`,chapter:114,text:`The crown here is the Roman scudo; Danglars reckons it at six livres (francs), so his self-set ransom is 48,000 francs, double Albert’s and less than 1% of what he carries.`,quote:`Eight thousand crowns amounted to 48,000 livres`},{id:`don-quixote`,term:`Don Quixote`,kind:`reference`,chapter:115,text:`Cervantes’ comic novel (1605–15) about a deluded would-be knight, which includes plenty of battered awakenings. That it is the only book Danglars has read says a good deal.`,quote:`He borrowed this from _Don Quixote_`},{id:`breguet-repeater`,term:`Breguet repeater`,kind:`word`,chapter:115,text:`A watch by Abraham-Louis Breguet, the most celebrated watchmaker of the age. A repeater chimes the time when a button is pressed, so it works in the dark.`,quote:`one of Breguet’s repeaters`},{id:`che-cosa`,term:`Che cosa?`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:115,text:`Italian: “What is it?”`,quote:`“_Che cosa?_” asked the bandit.`},{id:`4-999-louis`,term:`4,999 louis`,kind:`money`,chapter:115,text:`A louis was a 20-franc gold coin, so 5,000 louis is 100,000 francs, more than a century of a labourer’s wages, for one chicken.`,quote:`your excellency now owes me 4,999 louis`},{id:`draft-at-sight`,term:`draft at sight`,kind:`money`,chapter:115,text:`A written order to a bank to pay a sum as soon as it is presented. Peppino can cash it at Thomson & French immediately.`,quote:`here is a draft at sight`},{id:`harpagon`,term:`Harpagon`,kind:`reference`,chapter:116,text:`The miser in Molière’s comedy L’Avare (The Miser, 1668), whose howls over his stolen money are famous.`},{id:`usury`,term:`usury`,kind:`word`,chapter:116,text:`Lending money at extortionate interest. Vampa’s joke turns the banker’s own trade back on him.`,quote:`This is a kind of usury, banker`},{id:`25-000-francs-a-bottle`,term:`25,000 francs a bottle`,kind:`money`,chapter:116,text:`A quarter of the price of a chicken, but still, for a single bottle of wine, several decades of a labourer’s wages.`,quote:`Twenty-five thousand francs a bottle.`},{id:`amphitrite`,term:`Amphitrite`,kind:`reference`,chapter:117,text:`The sea goddess, wife of Poseidon. The setting sun is pictured as the fire god sinking into her arms.`,quote:`the bosom of Amphitrite`},{id:`as-calypso-said-to-telemachus`,term:`as Calypso said to Telemachus`,kind:`reference`,chapter:117,text:`From Fénelon’s hugely popular novel Télémaque (1699), in which the nymph Calypso welcomes the shipwrecked son of Ulysses ashore and offers him fresh clothes.`,quote:`as Calypso said to Telemachus`},{id:`he-who-is-about-to-die-salutes-you`,term:`He who is about to die salutes you`,kind:`reference`,chapter:117,text:`The Latin Morituri te salutant, which the Roman historian Suetonius reports men condemned to fight said to the Emperor Claudius.`,quote:`‘He who is about to die salutes you.’`},{id:`the-ancient-romans-condemned-by-nero`,term:`the ancient Romans condemned by Nero`,kind:`history`,chapter:117,text:`Nero often ordered out-of-favour Romans to kill themselves. Some, like the writer Petronius, made a graceful show of it, opening their veins at a banquet among friends.`,quote:`those ancient Romans, who when condemned by Nero`},{id:`caryatides`,term:`Caryatides`,kind:`word`,chapter:117,text:`Carved female figures used in place of columns, as on the Erechtheion in Athens.`,quote:`similar to the Caryatides`},{id:`felucca`,term:`felucca`,kind:`word`,chapter:117,text:`A small, swift Mediterranean sailing boat with triangular (lateen) sails.`,quote:`There is a felucca for you at anchor`},{id:`fac-et-spera`,term:`Fac et spera`,kind:`foreign`,chapter:117,text:`Latin, literally “Act and hope”. Dumas glosses it as “Wait and hope”.`,quote:`‘_Wait and hope_ (Fac et spera)!’`}],insights:[{id:`1-1`,chapter:1,kind:`craft`,title:`Everything in one morning`,text:`In a single morning Dantès brings the ship home, is promised the captaincy, and hurries off to his father and his bride. Dumas piles up his good fortune on purpose. The more a story gives its hero in its first pages, the more the reader feels how much he has to lose.`,themes:[]},{id:`1-2`,chapter:1,kind:`craft`,title:`Danglars, the watcher`,text:`Notice how Danglars is introduced: he watches, listens at doors and drops hints to Morrel. He never confronts Dantès openly. His resentment is about rank and money, the captaincy he feels should not go to a nineteen-year-old.`,themes:[]},{id:`1-3`,chapter:1,kind:`context`,title:`Why a stop at Elba is dangerous`,text:`In February 1815 Napoleon had been in exile on Elba for ten months, and the Bourbon king was back on the throne. To Dantès the stop is a simple favour to a dying captain. Morrel’s quick warning shows that ordinary people knew which names and places could ruin a man.`,themes:[]},{id:`2-1`,chapter:2,kind:`motif`,title:`Small sums, large meanings`,text:`This chapter is full of small amounts of money: the debt to Caderousse, the few francs old Dantès lived on, the gold Edmond pours onto the table. Each sum shows character. The father starved rather than owe anyone, while Caderousse came to collect from an old man.`,themes:[]},{id:`2-2`,chapter:2,kind:`craft`,title:`Caderousse in between`,text:`Caderousse is not a simple villain. He is envious and sly, but he is also chatty and sometimes almost friendly. Watch how fast his grumbling meets Danglars’s colder hatred: in one short conversation, two grudges become an alliance.`,themes:[]},{id:`2-3`,chapter:2,kind:`question`,title:`An open heart`,text:`Dantès tells everyone his good news: the promotion, the wedding, his plans. He assumes others are glad for him. Is this openness one of his best qualities, or a weakness in a world like this one?`,themes:[]},{id:`3-1`,chapter:3,kind:`craft`,title:`A heroine who says no`,text:`Mercédès is not a prize passed between men. She refuses Fernand plainly, tells him she will love only Edmond, and then makes the two men shake hands. Dumas gives her the strongest will in the scene.`,themes:[]},{id:`3-2`,chapter:3,kind:`motif`,title:`The knife and the hand`,text:`Fernand’s hand keeps going to his knife, and Mercédès makes him offer that hand to Edmond instead. The violence in this chapter is held back by a gesture, not settled. A forced handshake leaves the feeling behind it untouched.`,themes:[]},{id:`3-3`,chapter:3,kind:`craft`,title:`Watching a scheme take shape`,text:`At the arbour, Caderousse simply drinks, but Danglars studies Fernand’s misery and starts to see a use for it. Dumas lets us follow his thinking as it happens. We are not told the plan; we watch a man realise that someone else’s pain could serve him.`,themes:[]},{id:`4-1`,chapter:4,kind:`craft`,title:`The left hand`,text:`Danglars writes with his left hand to disguise his writing, and then throws the letter away as a joke. He never posts anything himself. Each man does only a part, so each can tell himself the real deed was someone else’s.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`4-2`,chapter:4,kind:`theme`,title:`Betrayal at the same table`,text:`The plotters are a shipmate, a neighbour and the bride’s cousin. Nothing they feel is monstrous: envy over a promotion, jealousy in love, resentment of a luckier man. Dumas shows betrayal growing out of ordinary feelings among people who know their victim well.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`4-3`,chapter:4,kind:`context`,title:`Denunciation as a weapon`,text:`After Napoleon’s fall, the restored monarchy was nervous and suspicious. An anonymous letter calling someone a Bonapartist agent could get them arrested without much proof. Danglars is not inventing a new trick; he is using a tool his times made easy.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`4-4`,chapter:4,kind:`question`,title:`The drunk who objects`,text:`Caderousse protests that the plan is wicked, but he is too drunk to stop anything and soon lets it go. How much blame belongs to someone who sees a wrong being done and does nothing?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`5-1`,chapter:5,kind:`craft`,title:`Joy, then the knock`,text:`Dumas lingers over the feast: the food, the toasts, the news that the wedding is moved up to that very afternoon. The happiness is stretched out so the soldiers’ arrival hits as hard as possible. Notice how close the wedding is when the door opens.`,themes:[]},{id:`5-2`,chapter:5,kind:`theme`,title:`The arrest as a test`,text:`Watch each guest when the soldiers come in. Fernand goes pale, Danglars stays smooth, Caderousse wavers, and Morrel goes straight off to help. The arrest shows who each person really is.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`5-3`,chapter:5,kind:`theme`,title:`When the law is the weapon`,text:`Dantès has done nothing wrong, yet one letter is enough to set soldiers, magistrates and prisons in motion against him. The men who wronged him gain from his fall. The book now asks a question it has not yet answered: what is an innocent man owed?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`6-1`,chapter:6,kind:`craft`,title:`Two engagement parties`,text:`Dumas cuts to a second betrothal feast at the same hour. One is for a sailor among Catalan fishermen, the other for an ambitious lawyer among aristocrats. Placing them side by side invites us to compare them, and to see the sailor’s future pass into the lawyer’s hands.`,themes:[]},{id:`6-2`,chapter:6,kind:`context`,title:`Families split by politics`,text:`Revolution and empire had divided French families. Villefort has dropped his father’s name, Noirtier, to separate himself from a known Bonapartist, and his bride’s family are fierce royalists. For him, political loyalty is partly a career decision.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`6-3`,chapter:6,kind:`question`,title:`What kind of man is Villefort?`,text:`Villefort talks with Renée about being merciful, and he is clearly clever and capable. He is also very aware of what his career needs. When the two pull in different directions, which do you expect him to follow?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`7-1`,chapter:7,kind:`craft`,title:`A chapter that turns on a name`,text:`For most of the chapter Villefort is fair and even kind, and ready to let Dantès go. Then he reads one name on a letter. Notice how his face changes while his voice stays gentle: from that moment, his kindness is an act.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`7-2`,chapter:7,kind:`theme`,title:`Punished for someone else’s secret`,text:`Dantès is not locked up for anything he did or knew. He is locked up because of what he unknowingly carried. Here justice is decided by who needs protecting, not by who is guilty.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`7-3`,chapter:7,kind:`motif`,title:`Fates decided on paper`,text:`So far every disaster in the book has come from a piece of paper: the denunciation, then the letter to Noirtier. Villefort burns the letter in front of Dantès and gets him to agree that it is for the best. A man’s freedom literally goes up in smoke while he thanks the man destroying it.`,themes:[]},{id:`8-1`,chapter:8,kind:`craft`,title:`Learning the truth with him`,text:`Dumas keeps us inside Dantès’s hopes on the night crossing. He thinks he is going home, picks out the Catalan village, and only slowly realises where the boat is heading. We find out the destination at the same moment he does.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`8-2`,chapter:8,kind:`context`,title:`The Château d’If`,text:`The Château d’If was a real island fortress just off Marseilles, used as a state prison. Political prisoners could be held there on an official’s order, without a trial. That is why Dantès’s demands to see the governor get him nowhere.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`8-3`,chapter:8,kind:`theme`,title:`Freedom within sight`,text:`From the boat Dantès can see Marseilles and the lights near Mercédès’s home. Part of the cruelty of the place is how close it is to everything he loves. He even thinks of throwing himself into the sea.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`9-1`,chapter:9,kind:`craft`,title:`One night, many windows`,text:`Dumas moves around the cast and shows how each person spends the night: Mercédès, Fernand, Morrel, old Dantès, Danglars, Villefort. The reader ends up knowing far more than Dantès, who knows almost nothing. This wide view is a habit of the book worth watching.`,themes:[]},{id:`9-2`,chapter:9,kind:`question`,title:`Guilt that changes nothing`,text:`After Mercédès begs him for news, Villefort, alone, feels a stab of conscience, almost like a physical pain, and then pushes it away. He feels it, but he does not act on it. Does guilt count for anything if it changes nothing?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`10-1`,chapter:10,kind:`craft`,title:`A sudden comic tone`,text:`After the arrest and the prison, the King’s study is almost comedy. Louis XVIII teases Blacas, quotes Latin and cares more about his reading than the warnings. The light satire sharpens the contrast with a young man in a dungeon.`,themes:[]},{id:`10-2`,chapter:10,kind:`context`,title:`A king returned from exile`,text:`Louis XVIII was the brother of Louis XVI, who was executed in the Revolution. He spent over twenty years in exile and came back in 1814 to rule a country the Revolution and Napoleon had remade. His easy confidence here is the confidence of a man who believes the danger is over.`,themes:[]},{id:`11-1`,chapter:11,kind:`craft`,title:`Praised by the king`,text:`The king rewards Villefort for his loyalty, and only the reader knows that this triumph was built on an innocent man. Each honour he receives is in effect paid for by Dantès. Dumas lets us enjoy the scene and feel uneasy about it at once.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`11-2`,chapter:11,kind:`theme`,title:`Loyalty that changes sides`,text:`News of Napoleon’s landing throws the court into panic, and it is clear that soldiers and officials may not stay loyal to the king. In this world loyalty often follows whoever seems to be winning. Villefort’s success depends on reading the wind correctly.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`12-1`,chapter:12,kind:`craft`,title:`“Father and Son” again`,text:`This chapter has the same title as Chapter 2. Compare the two pairs. Old Dantès went hungry for his son; Noirtier and Villefort spar, each protecting the other mostly to protect himself.`,themes:[]},{id:`12-2`,chapter:12,kind:`motif`,title:`A new face in minutes`,text:`Noirtier shaves off his whiskers, changes his coat and hat, and walks out past the police looking for him. Who you appear to be turns out to be something you can change with a razor. Notice how calmly he does it.`,themes:[]},{id:`12-3`,chapter:12,kind:`theme`,title:`Each the other’s hostage`,text:`Noirtier knows Villefort’s secret and Villefort knows Noirtier’s. Neither can betray the other without harming himself. Family loyalty here is real, but it looks a lot like a balance of threats.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`13-1`,chapter:13,kind:`craft`,title:`Years in a few pages`,text:`Dumas races through Napoleon’s return, Waterloo and the king’s second restoration in one chapter. Outside, history moves at great speed; inside the prison, time has stopped. The difference in pace is itself part of the story.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`13-2`,chapter:13,kind:`theme`,title:`Good intentions turned into a weapon`,text:`Morrel writes his petition out of loyalty to Dantès, trusting Villefort’s advice. Villefort keeps it, ready to use it against the man it was meant to save. Loyalty without knowledge can do as much harm as betrayal.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`13-3`,chapter:13,kind:`theme`,title:`The world closes over him`,text:`Danglars and Fernand leave Marseilles, old Dantès dies, and Mercédès is left waiting. The world adjusts to Dantès’s absence. It is a bleak picture of how quickly a life can be erased from the lives of others.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`14-1`,chapter:14,kind:`craft`,title:`Injustice with no villain in the room`,text:`The inspector is not cruel, only tired and routine-minded, and the governor is polite. No one in this chapter means Dantès harm, yet the system holds him anyway. Dumas shows how an institution can crush someone without anyone feeling wicked.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`14-2`,chapter:14,kind:`theme`,title:`Asking only for a trial`,text:`Dantès does not beg for freedom, only for a trial: even a guilty verdict would be better than not knowing. The inspector’s promise lifts him, and then the note in his file cancels it. Watch how hope here depends on a few words written by someone else.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`14-3`,chapter:14,kind:`question`,title:`Is Faria mad?`,text:`We meet Faria only through the eyes of jailers and officials who laugh at his offer of millions. Everything he says sounds mad to them. How much of the madness is in him, and how much in the people judging him?`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`15-1`,chapter:15,kind:`theme`,title:`A map of despair`,text:`Dumas traces Dantès’s years alone in stages: pride, then prayer, then rage, then a calm decision to die. It is a careful study of how solitude wears a mind down. Notice that the last stage is quiet, not violent.`,themes:[`hope-despair`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`15-2`,chapter:15,kind:`craft`,title:`A sound in the wall`,text:`The turning point is only a sound. Dumas makes us wait while Dantès tests it: is it real, is it a workman or a prisoner, will it stop if he knocks? The slow build mirrors his fear of hoping too soon.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`15-3`,chapter:15,kind:`motif`,title:`Tools from nothing`,text:`Dantès breaks a jug to make a scraper and wins a saucepan handle from the jailer. A prisoner has to turn every scrap into a tool. Purpose comes back to him before any real chance of escape does.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`16-1`,chapter:16,kind:`theme`,title:`A mind that cannot be locked up`,text:`Faria has made pens from fish bones, ink from soot, and paper from linen, and has written a whole book in his cell. The prison holds his body, but not his mind. For Faria, freedom begins inside the head.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`16-2`,chapter:16,kind:`context`,title:`A dream of a united Italy`,text:`In 1815 Italy was a patchwork of kingdoms, duchies and papal lands, mostly under foreign influence. Faria’s dream of uniting it was a real political cause, and a dangerous one. It is why a gentle scholar is in a dungeon.`,themes:[]},{id:`16-3`,chapter:16,kind:`theme`,title:`Hope as a habit`,text:`Faria has spent years digging, only to come out in the wrong place. He shrugs and starts planning again. For him, hope is less a feeling than a daily discipline.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`17-1`,chapter:17,kind:`craft`,title:`A detective in a dungeon`,text:`Faria solves Dantès’s case by asking who gained from his fall. Dantès has lived with the facts for years without seeing them. His innocence was also a kind of blindness, and Faria’s reasoning removes it in an afternoon.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`17-2`,chapter:17,kind:`theme`,title:`Knowledge becomes a vow`,text:`The moment Dantès understands what was done to him, he swears revenge. Notice how fast the change is, and how the gentle young sailor of the first chapters sounds different now. Learning the truth has given him a purpose.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`17-3`,chapter:17,kind:`theme`,title:`The prison as a university`,text:`Faria teaches Dantès languages, history, chemistry and mathematics. A sailor with little schooling is becoming a scholar. A place meant to destroy him becomes, through Faria, the place that remakes him.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`17-4`,chapter:17,kind:`question`,title:`Was Faria right to tell him?`,text:`Faria says he regrets helping Dantès work out the truth, because of what it has put in his heart. Should he have left Dantès ignorant? Is it kinder to know who wronged you, or not to know?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`18-1`,chapter:18,kind:`craft`,title:`A tale inside the tale`,text:`The prison story pauses for a Renaissance tale of popes, poison and a hidden fortune. It reads like one of the Arabian Nights stories Faria loves. Dumas is openly blending history with fairy tale.`,themes:[]},{id:`18-2`,chapter:18,kind:`motif`,title:`Fire that reveals`,text:`In Chapter 7 fire destroyed a letter to keep a secret. Here a half-burnt page, warmed over a flame, gives up a secret it had hidden for centuries. Paper and fire keep deciding what is known and what is lost.`,themes:[]},{id:`18-3`,chapter:18,kind:`theme`,title:`A chosen father`,text:`Faria calls Dantès his son and names him his heir. Dantès has been torn from his own father, and Faria has no family left. Their bond is chosen, not given, and it is the most loyal relationship in the book so far.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`19-1`,chapter:19,kind:`craft`,title:`Dread built by rehearsal`,text:`Faria has said the third attack will kill him, and he has taught Dantès the remedy and given his last instructions. When the attack comes, we already know each step. The rehearsal makes the scene feel inevitable, and worse for it.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`19-2`,chapter:19,kind:`theme`,title:`Alone again`,text:`With the tunnel blocked and his friend dead, Dantès is back in the solitude of Chapter 15. But he is not the same man: he now has knowledge, education and a secret. Compare how he faces this loss with how he faced the first years.`,themes:[`hope-despair`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`20-1`,chapter:20,kind:`craft`,title:`Death and rebirth`,text:`To leave the prison, Dantès must pretend to be dead and sew himself into a shroud. The escape is staged as a burial. It is hard not to read it as the end of one life and the start of another.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`20-2`,chapter:20,kind:`craft`,title:`Seeing nothing`,text:`Once he is in the sack, we experience everything as he does: voices, jokes, wind, the weight tied to his feet. Dumas takes away sight and leaves only sound and touch. The jailers’ jokes make the scene more frightening, not less.`,themes:[]},{id:`20-3`,chapter:20,kind:`theme`,title:`Two aims, side by side`,text:`Sitting by Faria’s body, Dantès moves from wanting to die to a fierce will to live, in order to punish his enemies and reward his friends. Notice that the two aims arrive together. Reward and punishment are joined in his mind from the start.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`20-4`,chapter:20,kind:`motif`,title:`The sea as cemetery`,text:`The chapter’s last line is a shock: the sea is the cemetery of the Château d’If. The sea was Dantès’s trade and his life, and now it is meant to be his grave. Water in this book is both a threat and a way out.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`21-1`,chapter:21,kind:`motif`,title:`Fourteen years to the day`,text:`Jacopo tells him the date, 28 February 1829, the anniversary of his arrest. The coincidence turns lost time into something exact. He went in at nineteen and comes out at thirty-three.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`21-2`,chapter:21,kind:`craft`,title:`A dead man’s cap`,text:`His first acts as a free man are to put on a drowned sailor’s cap and tell a smooth lie about who he is. When the alarm gun fires, he calmly says a prisoner must have escaped and sips his rum. Notice how cool he has become.`,themes:[]},{id:`21-3`,chapter:21,kind:`theme`,title:`The vow renewed`,text:`Free and at sea, Dantès thinks of Mercédès and repeats his oath against Danglars, Fernand and Villefort. Freedom has not softened his purpose. The vow made in the dark holds in daylight.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`22-1`,chapter:22,kind:`motif`,title:`The face in the mirror`,text:`At Leghorn Dantès sees his own face for the first time in fourteen years and barely recognises it. The open young sailor has become pale, stern and watchful. No one from his old life would know him now.`,themes:[]},{id:`22-2`,chapter:22,kind:`theme`,title:`A hardening heart`,text:`When a customs officer is killed in the skirmish, Dantès notices that the death hardly moves him. Dumas has him observe the change in himself. Ask what the prison has taken from him along with his youth.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`22-3`,chapter:22,kind:`theme`,title:`Passing on what he was given`,text:`Jacopo nurses Dantès and refuses to be paid, and Dantès teaches him navigation and the stars as Faria once taught him. Loyalty here is answered with teaching. The friendship is one of the warmest things in the chapter.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`23-1`,chapter:23,kind:`craft`,title:`A performance for friends`,text:`Dantès fakes his fall and his agony so well that the smugglers leave him behind, and Jacopo offers to stay. He is deceiving men he genuinely likes. Notice that he is moved by their friendship even as he plays them.`,themes:[]},{id:`23-2`,chapter:23,kind:`motif`,title:`“Open Sesame!”`,text:`Dantès cries out the words from the Arabian Nights tale Faria told him. Dumas openly hands the story over to fairy tale: a hidden cave, a magic phrase, a fortune. The realistic prison story has turned into something closer to legend.`,themes:[]},{id:`23-3`,chapter:23,kind:`question`,title:`What is a life worth?`,text:`Dantès looks down at the smugglers and reflects that they risk their lives for a few coins, while he hopes for far more. Is this wisdom, or the beginning of contempt? What has his suffering done to his sense of what things are worth?`,themes:[]},{id:`24-1`,chapter:24,kind:`craft`,title:`Suspense by obstacles`,text:`This chapter is almost all one man digging alone. Dumas puts an obstacle at every step: the boulder, the empty first cave, the false wall, a moment when doubt nearly makes him faint. His wry “Perhaps!” captures how much he fears hoping.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`24-2`,chapter:24,kind:`motif`,title:`The teacher’s voice`,text:`Dantès follows the words of the will as if Faria were beside him, and recognises the Spada arms Faria drew for him so often. The dead teacher still guides him. The treasure is Faria’s gift as much as it is a discovery.`,themes:[]},{id:`24-3`,chapter:24,kind:`theme`,title:`Wealth beyond counting`,text:`Faced with the chest, Dantès first runs wildly about the rocks, then kneels and prays. He spends the night guarding it, torn between joy and fear. Money on this scale is not just comfort: it is power, and he does not yet know what he will do with it.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`25-1`,chapter:25,kind:`theme`,title:`A stranger in his own city`,text:`Dantès sails into Marseilles on an English passport as Lord Wilmore and tests his new face on a sailor from the Pharaon, who does not know him. He can now walk among people from his past unrecognised. Notice how much confidence he takes from that.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`25-2`,chapter:25,kind:`craft`,title:`The quietest scene`,text:`With a fortune hidden on his yacht, the most powerful moment in the chapter is Dantès weeping in the room where his father died. Dumas keeps the treasure in the background here. Grief, not wealth, sets the tone of his homecoming.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`25-3`,chapter:25,kind:`theme`,title:`Money as an instrument`,text:`Dantès overpays for the yacht and the house, gives Jacopo a boat, rewards the honest sailor and gives the Catalan family a fishing boat. He has a secret closet built to carry the treasure. Watch how he already uses money to reward and to test.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`26-1`,chapter:26,kind:`craft`,title:`A visitor without a name`,text:`The narrator calls the visitor only “the abbé” and tells us nothing about him. Instead we watch him: how closely he studies Caderousse, and how he jumps up when he hears how old Dantès died. Dumas makes us read the priest from the outside.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`26-2`,chapter:26,kind:`motif`,title:`The diamond as bait`,text:`Caderousse’s wife warns him to keep quiet, and at first he means to. What changes his mind is hearing that Danglars and Fernand might get shares of the diamond. Greed, not conscience, makes him talk.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`26-3`,chapter:26,kind:`context`,title:`Ruined by a canal`,text:`A new canal carried trade away from the old road, leaving roadside inns like Caderousse’s empty. Changes in transport made and broke many small businesses in this period. His bad luck is partly just history passing him by.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`27-1`,chapter:27,kind:`craft`,title:`The truth from a guilty witness`,text:`We hear the whole story from Caderousse, who was there, did nothing and is now full of self-pity. His telling is coloured by excuses: he was drunk, he was afraid. Notice what he admits and what he passes over quickly.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`27-2`,chapter:27,kind:`theme`,title:`The wrong people rewarded`,text:`The betrayers have risen: Danglars is a baron and a millionaire, Fernand a count and a general. Morrel, who pleaded for Dantès again and again, is almost ruined. The world has handed out its rewards the wrong way round.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`27-3`,chapter:27,kind:`theme`,title:`A hope that killed`,text:`Old Dantès would not leave his rooms in case Edmond came back, sold what he owned and finally stopped eating. He died hoping, blessing his son. It is the saddest picture of hope in the book so far.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`27-4`,chapter:27,kind:`question`,title:`Mercédès’s choice`,text:`Mercédès married Fernand, in the very church where she was to marry Edmond. Caderousse believes she is unhappy. What do you make of her choice, given what she knew and what she did not?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`28-1`,chapter:28,kind:`craft`,title:`A grim story told as a joke`,text:`Boville tells the escape as a funny anecdote and roars with laughter, and the Englishman laughs along. We have lived through that night with Dantès, so the laughter sounds very different to us. Dumas makes the reader hold two views of the same event.`,themes:[]},{id:`28-2`,chapter:28,kind:`motif`,title:`The file`,text:`The whole injustice survives as paperwork: the denunciation, the examination, Morrel’s petition, Villefort’s note ordering strict solitary confinement. Paper sent Dantès to prison, and paper still records it. The stranger quietly pockets the most important page.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`28-3`,chapter:28,kind:`theme`,title:`Dead on paper`,text:`The prison register closes the case: Edmond Dantès drowned. In the eyes of the state he no longer exists. Notice what kind of freedom that could give someone, and that the visitor asks for nothing but a look at the records.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`29-1`,chapter:29,kind:`craft`,title:`Decline in small details`,text:`Dumas shows Morrel’s ruin through details: the empty offices, two remaining employees, the family jewels quietly sold, a man of fifty with white hair. Nothing is announced; we read the decline in what is missing. Compare it with the busy firm of Chapter 1.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`29-2`,chapter:29,kind:`theme`,title:`A loyal house`,text:`Emmanuel and Cocles stay when everyone else leaves, and the shipwrecked sailors offer to wait for their wages. Morrel’s house is poor but full of loyalty. Set it beside the arbour where the plotters met in Chapter 4.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`29-3`,chapter:29,kind:`motif`,title:`Sinbad the Sailor`,text:`The stranger tells Julie to expect a letter signed “Sinbad the Sailor,” a name from the Arabian Nights. Once again the story borrows from those tales. Fairy-tale names are entering a world of bills and bankruptcy.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`30-1`,chapter:30,kind:`craft`,title:`Racing the clock`,text:`Dumas cuts between Morrel waiting with the pistol as the clock moves towards eleven and Julie running with the purse. It is a classic last-minute rescue, built on a deadline set in the previous chapter. Notice how exactly the timing is arranged.`,themes:[]},{id:`30-2`,chapter:30,kind:`motif`,title:`The red purse comes home`,text:`Morrel’s red silk purse, the one he left to pay old Dantès’s debts and funeral, comes back to him holding his own paid bill. His kindness is repaid with the very object he gave. The rescue is also a thank-you note.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`30-3`,chapter:30,kind:`theme`,title:`Honour worth more than life`,text:`Morrel would rather die with an honest name than live as a bankrupt, and his son accepts it. Danglars, who owes his start to Morrel, refuses to help him. In this society money and honour are tied together, and losing one means losing the other.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`30-4`,chapter:30,kind:`question`,title:`Standing in for Providence`,text:`The man in the crowd who calls for Jacopo is now clearly Dantès, and he declares that, having rewarded the good, he will now punish the wicked. He speaks as if he were Providence itself. Can any human being take on that role, and what might it cost him?`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`31-1`,chapter:31,kind:`craft`,title:`A stranger’s eyes`,text:`Dumas jumps forward to 1838 and hands the story to Franz, a young man who knows nothing of Dantès. We know far more than he does. We watched the treasure come out of the ground, and we heard the Englishman of the last chapter go by the name Sinbad, so the pleasure here is dramatic irony: we watch an outsider try to make sense of a man we already know.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`31-2`,chapter:31,kind:`craft`,title:`A surname that should ring a bell`,text:`Franz’s travelling companion is the Viscount Albert de Morcerf. Caderousse has already told us who the Count de Morcerf is and whom he married. Dumas does not underline it. He trusts you to make the connection while Franz, who knows nothing of the old story, chats on.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`31-3`,chapter:31,kind:`motif`,title:`The treasure cave becomes a palace`,text:`Sinbad, Aladdin, a mute servant, a hidden door: the whole evening is staged as a tale from the Arabian Nights. The bare grotto where Dantès dug for the chest has become a palace of brocade and marble. Notice that the wealth is not just spent but performed. Its job is to dazzle a visitor.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`31-4`,chapter:31,kind:`question`,title:`Why does he laugh?`,text:`Sinbad talks of vows still to be kept and of handing out his own secret justice. When Franz guesses at revenge, his host laughs it off. Is the laugh a denial, a mask, or a sign that the word is too small for what he means?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`32-1`,chapter:32,kind:`motif`,title:`Dream and waking`,text:`Franz wakes on heather in a plain cave, and the hidden door cannot be found. Smoke marks show that others have searched before him. Dumas leaves it unclear how much of the night was real. Part of this man’s power is that nobody can check up on him.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`32-2`,chapter:32,kind:`theme`,title:`On the side of hunted men`,text:`Instead of sailing for Malaga, Sinbad goes out of his way to land two Corsican bandits safely. Gaetano says he fears neither God nor devil and will always help a man on the run. Think of who he once was: a man the law locked up without a trial. Now his sympathy lies with the people the law is chasing.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`32-3`,chapter:32,kind:`craft`,title:`What money cannot buy`,text:`Albert goes to bed sure that a full purse will find him a carriage in Carnival week. Pastrini says it cannot be done. In the same breath we learn that a very rich, foreign gentleman has taken the rest of the floor. Dumas has set out two kinds of wealth side by side: Albert’s, and something on a much grander scale.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`33-1`,chapter:33,kind:`craft`,title:`A tale inside the tale`,text:`The novel stops for a whole chapter so that an innkeeper can tell a bandit romance. Dumas loves these nested stories. This one is not a detour. Like Dantès, Vampa is a gifted poor boy who teaches himself, steps outside the law and becomes a power in his own right, and the passing traveller called Sinbad ties his story to ours.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`33-2`,chapter:33,kind:`motif`,title:`Gold in Teresa’s eyes`,text:`Teresa refuses Cucumetto’s gold, but her eyes light up at the sight of it. She longs to dance among the nobles. The night of the ball a fire breaks out, and next day Vampa gives her Carmela’s costume and jewels. Dumas never says outright how he came by them. Watch how desire for finery drives the people in this story.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`33-3`,chapter:33,kind:`question`,title:`Is there honor among bandits?`,text:`Carlini stabs the woman he loves rather than leave her to the band. Vampa kills his chief to save Teresa, then puts on the dead man’s clothes and takes his place. The bandits keep a strict code of their own. Is Vampa a hero, a monster, or something the ordinary words do not fit?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`34-1`,chapter:34,kind:`craft`,title:`Behind the pillar`,text:`Franz hides in the moonlit Colosseum and overhears a deal: a cloaked man promises to buy a condemned shepherd’s pardon. We find out what this man can do through things he does not mean Franz to see. Power that can reach into the Pope’s own courts is far beyond a rich man’s whim.`,themes:[`providence`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`34-2`,chapter:34,kind:`motif`,title:`The vampire`,text:`The Countess shudders at his pallor and calls him Lord Ruthven, a vampire. Dumas has stressed this deathly whiteness since the cave. It fits a man who was sewn into a shroud and thrown into the sea, and who climbed out alive. The joke about the undead lands closer to the truth than the Countess knows.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`34-3`,chapter:34,kind:`craft`,title:`The name arrives last`,text:`The chapter ends with visiting cards from a neighbour called the Count of Monte Cristo. Sinbad, the island, the voice in the Colosseum and the pale man at the opera now come together under the title of the place where the treasure lay. Dumas holds back the name until we have met the man three times.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`35-1`,chapter:35,kind:`theme`,title:`An eye for an eye`,text:`Look closely at the Count’s examples. To make his case against quick punishment, he imagines a man whose father, mother or betrothed has been destroyed. Father and betrothed are exactly what Dantès lost. He speaks as if in general, but the hatred in his face shows he is describing himself.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`35-2`,chapter:35,kind:`craft`,title:`One guest eats, one guest watches`,text:`Albert enjoys the breakfast and the cigars. Franz notices that the host barely eats and keeps looking hard at Albert. Dumas splits our attention between two witnesses, the carefree one and the uneasy one. We know something neither of them knows: what Albert’s father once did to their host.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`35-3`,chapter:35,kind:`theme`,title:`A pardon staged like a miracle`,text:`The Count has arranged Peppino’s reprieve and watches it arrive like a god at a play. Then the murderer Andrea rages that his companion must die with him, and the Count laughs at this proof of human nature. For him, envy of another’s luck is what people are really like. He has had reason to learn that.`,themes:[`providence`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`35-4`,chapter:35,kind:`context`,title:`Execution as spectacle`,text:`Rome was then ruled by the Pope, and its executions were public, solemn and crowded, with hooded penitents in procession and the Carnival following close behind. Dumas makes us watch the death in full from a rented window, then plunges straight into the festival. It shocks Franz, and it is meant to shock us.`,themes:[]},{id:`36-1`,chapter:36,kind:`motif`,title:`Everyone in a mask`,text:`Carnival is the one week when everyone in Rome goes in disguise. Albert flirts with a masked woman and is sure she must be a noblewoman. The Count changes into costume as easily as he changes names. In a book about a man in permanent disguise, Dumas has found the perfect setting.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`36-2`,chapter:36,kind:`craft`,title:`From the scaffold to the confetti`,text:`Within hours of watching a man clubbed to death, Franz is pelting strangers with flowers. On the last night the whole Corso plays at blowing out each other’s candles. Dumas sets laughter and death side by side without comment and leaves us to feel the jolt.`,themes:[]},{id:`36-3`,chapter:36,kind:`question`,title:`Why does Franz want to be elsewhere?`,text:`The Count lends them his carriage, talks brilliantly of painting and chemistry, and never once mentions the island. Franz admires him, yet he does not want to be in Paris when the Count arrives. What exactly is he afraid of? Is it the man’s generosity, his silence, or what Franz overheard?`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`37-1`,chapter:37,kind:`theme`,title:`A dandy’s coolness`,text:`Albert is found asleep in a bandits’ lair. He thanks his rescuer politely and lights his cigar from Vampa’s torch. It is comic, and it is also a portrait of a class. A young Parisian aristocrat of 1838 is trained to treat even a death threat as a social occasion.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`37-2`,chapter:37,kind:`question`,title:`The shudder`,text:`Shaking Albert’s hand, the Count shudders. We know Albert’s father and mother. What does it cost this man to be thanked by that young man? And what is he looking for in this young man’s face?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`37-3`,chapter:37,kind:`motif`,title:`Underground again`,text:`The Château d’If dungeon, the treasure grotto, Sinbad’s hidden palace, and now the catacombs: some of this book’s most important scenes take place underground. Notice how calmly the Count walks through a burial chamber full of armed men. The places that once buried him have become places where he rules.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`38-1`,chapter:38,kind:`context`,title:`Written to keep readers waiting`,text:`The novel first came out in installments in the newspaper Journal des Débats in 1844–46, and readers waited for each new part. An appointment fixed to the minute, three months ahead, is a serial writer’s gift. It makes a promise the reader wants to see kept, and the chapters that follow can count down to it.`,themes:[]},{id:`38-2`,chapter:38,kind:`motif`,title:`A hand as cold as a corpse`,text:`When they part, the Count’s hand feels to Franz as cold as a dead man’s. It adds to the pallor and the vampire joke. Dumas keeps reminding us that this man once went into the sea as a corpse, and part of him has never come back to life.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`38-3`,chapter:38,kind:`theme`,title:`Money explains everything`,text:`Franz tells the whole strange story, and Albert explains every part of it away. Rich men buy yachts and titles, bandits are just outlaws, and in any case the man saved his life. To Albert, great wealth makes anything believable. The Count seems to be counting on that.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`39-1`,chapter:39,kind:`craft`,title:`A room as a portrait`,text:`Dumas lists Albert’s rooms in loving detail: every kind of tobacco, a private door, and a studio full of instruments, armor and stuffed birds left from hobbies he picked up and dropped. We learn who Albert is from his things. He is charming and indulged, and he has everything except something to do.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`39-2`,chapter:39,kind:`context`,title:`Paris under the July Monarchy`,text:`By 1838 France had a “citizen king,” Louis-Philippe, and a society that ran on money, newspapers and ministries. Albert’s breakfast guests are a small cross-section of it: a minister’s secretary, an opposition journalist, and soon an old-family nobleman and a soldier. The Count is about to meet the whole city through one table.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`39-3`,chapter:39,kind:`question`,title:`How did the banker know?`,text:`Debray has been up all night on Spanish dispatches, yet Baron Danglars somehow had the news early enough to make a million. In a world where news travels slowly, whoever hears first gets rich. How might a banker come by news before the government has finished writing it up?`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`40-1`,chapter:40,kind:`motif`,title:`The 5th of September`,text:`Morrel keeps the 5th of September every year as the day his father was miraculously saved, and he chose that day to rescue Château-Renaud. We know who sent the red purse and the new Pharaon. A private act of gratitude has become a family feast day, almost a religious one.`,themes:[`providence`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`40-2`,chapter:40,kind:`craft`,title:`On the stroke`,text:`The guests laugh at the idea that the Count will turn up. Then the clock strikes half past ten and he walks in without a sound. Dumas built three months of waiting for this entrance. The Count controls even the timing of his own arrival.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`40-3`,chapter:40,kind:`theme`,title:`The mask slips once`,text:`The Count is cool and polite with everyone until he hears Morrel’s name. Then he colors and his voice trembles. Notice what breaks his composure. It is not anger at an enemy but tenderness for the son of a friend.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`40-4`,chapter:40,kind:`theme`,title:`Outside society’s protection`,text:`The Count says he protects no society that does not protect him. It is a calm statement of a dangerous idea: the social contract runs both ways. Think of the society that let him rot in a cell for years without a trial, and you hear what lies behind his polite tone.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`41-1`,chapter:41,kind:`motif`,title:`The Catalan portrait`,text:`In Albert’s bedroom hangs a young woman in a Catalan fisherwoman’s dress, gazing out to sea. His father dislikes the picture, and his mother rarely looks at it without crying. The village girl of Marseilles survives only in paint, hung in her son’s room and kept away from her husband.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`41-2`,chapter:41,kind:`craft`,title:`One word in a doorway`,text:`Dumas describes the Countess appearing pale and still in the doorway, and then the narrator simply names her: Mercédès. After so many chapters, that one word does all the work. There is no speech and no explanation, just two pale faces and a bow.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`41-3`,chapter:41,kind:`craft`,title:`Flattery with a blade in it`,text:`The Count admires the Morcerf coat of arms “with hidden irony” and flatters the general lavishly. We know this peer of France began as a Catalan fisherman. Every compliment can be read two ways, and Dumas lets us hear the second meaning that his host cannot.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`41-4`,chapter:41,kind:`question`,title:`What does Mercédès know?`,text:`Lying in a darkened room, she asks Albert whether Monte Cristo is a real name and how old the Count is. His answer seems to shock her. She tells her son to be careful. What has she seen in that face, and what is she afraid of?`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`42-1`,chapter:42,kind:`craft`,title:`The notebook on a chain`,text:`For the first time we see the Count alone with his plans. He unlocks a notebook kept on a key round his neck and checks an address: “it is indeed the same.” Until now we have watched him through other people’s eyes. Here Dumas lets us see a mind at work, though not what it is working on.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`42-2`,chapter:42,kind:`question`,title:`A confession extorted`,text:`The Count wonders whether he can trust a confession drawn out by “religious or physical terror.” Whose confession, made to whom? He clearly knows something about his own steward that the steward does not know he knows. What does it say about the Count that he tests the truth rather than simply asking for it?`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`42-3`,chapter:42,kind:`craft`,title:`A house bought unseen`,text:`The Count claims not to know where Auteuil is, signs without a look and overpays. Then he watches Bertuccio turn pale. The purchase is a test as much as a deal. He stages even small scenes so that other people show what they are hiding.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`43-1`,chapter:43,kind:`craft`,title:`A turn to the Gothic`,text:`Night, an empty house, a hidden staircase, a garden with a dreaded spot under a plane tree, and a servant shaking with terror. Dumas switches into the language of the Gothic novel. The house has a history, and the setting makes us feel it before anyone tells it.`,themes:[]},{id:`43-2`,chapter:43,kind:`theme`,title:`Priest and master`,text:`The Count threatens Bertuccio by reminding him that the Abbé Busoni sent him and heard his confession in prison. We know who the abbé of Caderousse’s inn really was. The Count has used a priest’s role to learn men’s secrets, and now he uses those secrets as a master. Is that playing God, or something close to it?`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`providence`]},{id:`43-3`,chapter:43,kind:`theme`,title:`Another man’s vendetta`,text:`Bertuccio confesses that he took Corsican blood revenge in this garden, and that his victim was M. de Villefort. A second man, with no link to Dantès, also had a score to settle with the same magistrate. Revenge in this book is not one man’s story. It runs through a whole society.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`44-1`,chapter:44,kind:`theme`,title:`The same refusal, twice`,text:`In 1815 Villefort waved away Bertuccio’s murdered brother as a soldier of “the usurper” and refused him justice. We have seen him do this before: he judged Dantès by what served the political moment and his career. Bertuccio’s story shows that Dantès was not a single unlucky case but one victim of a pattern.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`44-2`,chapter:44,kind:`motif`,title:`Buried alive`,text:`A box is buried in a garden at night, and inside it a newborn is barely breathing. Bertuccio digs it up and brings it back to life. The image of someone put in the ground alive and pulled out again echoes Dantès sewn into a sack and thrown into the sea.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`44-3`,chapter:44,kind:`craft`,title:`Coincidence or design?`,text:`One Corsican servant turns out to have witnessed Villefort’s secret in Auteuil and Caderousse’s sale of the diamond. Dumas’s plots run on such links, and you can read them as convenience. You can also read them the way the Count does, as a hidden order in which nothing done is ever lost.`,themes:[`providence`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`44-4`,chapter:44,kind:`question`,title:`What did the diamond do?`,text:`We watched the abbé give Caderousse the diamond as a reward for telling his story. Now we hear what Caderousse and his wife did with it. Was the gift meant as a kindness, or as a test of what they were? And if it was a test, who is responsible for the result?`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`45-1`,chapter:45,kind:`motif`,title:`The rain of blood`,text:`Warm drops fall through the floorboards onto Bertuccio’s face. Minutes later he is found soaked in blood and arrested for murders he did not commit. An innocent man, marked by another’s crime and thrown in prison: the shape of Dantès’s own story returns in a new form.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`45-2`,chapter:45,kind:`theme`,title:`The Count passes sentence`,text:`When the story ends, the Count gives his verdict. Bertuccio’s real crime, he says, was not the stabbing but failing to return the child to its mother. He also says the wicked are not so easily gone. He listens like a confessor and judges like a court. He is acting as Providence, the role he has claimed for himself.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`45-3`,chapter:45,kind:`craft`,title:`The garden as a plan`,text:`Alone, the Count studies the garden as a “plan” laid out before him and murmurs that the prosecutor may not be dead. We cannot see the design yet, but we can feel one forming. Dumas shows us a man who looks at a crime scene the way an architect looks at a site.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`46-1`,chapter:46,kind:`theme`,title:`Wealth as a weapon`,text:`Danglars tries to patronize the Count and offers him “even a million.” The Count calmly takes out a million in notes and shows credit on the greatest banks in Europe. He beats the banker with the only thing the banker respects. Money here is not comfort but a way to humiliate.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`46-2`,chapter:46,kind:`craft`,title:`The horses`,text:`The Count turns Danglars away at the door, then has his prized horses bought out from under him at double the price, and arrives at the banker’s house behind them. It is a small, exact cruelty, and very public. Notice how the Count likes his gestures to be symbols that other people have to read.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`46-3`,chapter:46,kind:`question`,title:`A prisoner who owns a slave`,text:`The Count says Ali is not a servant but a slave whose life belongs to him. He speaks to Ali kindly, and Ali owes him his life. Even so, this is a man who spent years as the property of a prison. How does he square that past with owning another human being?`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`47-1`,chapter:47,kind:`craft`,title:`An accident with a rehearsal`,text:`The Count asks Ali whether his lasso could stop two runaway horses and posts him outside the house. At five o’clock the horses bolt past. The characters see a lucky rescue. We have been shown the preparation, so we see a design.`,themes:[`providence`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`47-2`,chapter:47,kind:`theme`,title:`Peace in his hands`,text:`“The domestic peace of this family is henceforth in my hands,” the Count says as he leaves the quarrelling Danglars. He has not touched the banker’s money or his rank, only his marriage. Watch how often the Count works through households rather than through law courts.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`47-3`,chapter:47,kind:`motif`,title:`The red elixir`,text:`One drop of a blood-red liquid brings the boy back to life. The Count warns that some of his liquids are dangerous even to smell, and he notices Madame de Villefort glance at the case. Little escapes the Count’s attention.`,themes:[]},{id:`48-1`,chapter:48,kind:`theme`,title:`Wishing to be Providence`,text:`This is the Count’s clearest statement about himself. Offered the world by Satan, he says, he asked instead to be Providence, because nothing is finer than to reward and punish. So far we have seen him only reward, yet he speaks as if the bargain has been struck and he would strike it again.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`48-2`,chapter:48,kind:`craft`,title:`Judge and judged, face to face`,text:`Villefort, the man of human law, faces the Count, who claims a higher law, and he does not recognize the young sailor he once sent away. The whole conversation is dramatic irony. When the Count speaks of a mission from God, or says he was once “nearly mad,” only we know what he is talking about.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`48-3`,chapter:48,kind:`question`,title:`Who may call misfortune a punishment?`,text:`Villefort calls his father’s stroke God’s punishment of the old man alone, and calls his own children his reward. He reads Providence when it suits him, just as the Count does. Is there any difference between the two men’s claims to know what God intends?`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`48-4`,chapter:48,kind:`theme`,title:`Men who survive every regime`,text:`Villefort has kept his post through four revolutions. Danglars is a baron, and Fernand a general and peer. Each of the men who rose by Dantès’s fall has adapted to every change of government and come out on top. Dumas suggests that in this France, success rewards flexibility, not virtue.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`49-1`,chapter:49,kind:`question`,title:`Free, or his?`,text:`The Count tells Haydée that in France she is free, free even to leave him. She replies by calling him her master and saying she would die if he died. He gives her freedom, and she gives it back. What is freedom worth when one person holds all the power and the other has known nothing else?`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`49-2`,chapter:49,kind:`craft`,title:`Getting ready to be kind`,text:`The Count spends an hour with Haydée to prepare himself before visiting the Morrels. After the duel with Villefort, he needs to find a gentler self. Dumas shows him switching between roles, and suggests that the warm one now takes effort.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`49-3`,chapter:49,kind:`craft`,title:`A secret named but not told`,text:`He asks Haydée never to speak the names of her “illustrious father” and “ill-fated mother.” Dumas tells us that there is a secret and that it matters, but not what it is. The Count is keeping her history in reserve, just as he keeps his own.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`50-1`,chapter:50,kind:`motif`,title:`Relics under glass`,text:`The Morrels keep a silk purse, a letter signed Sinbad and a diamond under a glass cover, as a church keeps a saint’s relics. Julie says God once sent them an angel. The Count stands before the shrine to his own good deed and cannot say a word.`,themes:[`providence`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`50-2`,chapter:50,kind:`theme`,title:`Refusing to be thanked`,text:`He invents a Lord Wilmore who did not believe in gratitude and has left for good. The Count could accept the thanks he has just heard. Instead he pushes it away. Why might a man who longs for justice be unable to bear being thanked?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`50-3`,chapter:50,kind:`craft`,title:`“It was Edmond Dantès!”`,text:`Old Morrel’s last words, which the Count hears now for the first time, see through every disguise. The one person who guessed the truth did it not by evidence but by faith in a young man he had loved. The Count goes deathly pale and leaves, and a dead man’s faith shakes his mask as no living enemy has.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`51-1`,chapter:51,kind:`craft`,title:`Lovers from opposite camps`,text:`Dumas opens a love story between the son of Morrel the Bonapartist shipowner and the daughter of Villefort the royalist prosecutor. The title points to Pyramus and Thisbe, and behind them Romeo and Juliet. Two families on opposite sides of 1815 now have children who meet in secret.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`51-2`,chapter:51,kind:`theme`,title:`The children inherit the quarrel`,text:`At Maximilian’s name, Villefort sneers at the Bonapartist Morrels of 1815, and Valentine thinks Danglars trembled. She has no idea why. We do. The fathers’ past sits in the room unspoken while their children try to plan a future.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`51-3`,chapter:51,kind:`motif`,title:`Talking through a gate`,text:`The lovers can meet only through the planks of a boarded-up gate. Valentine describes a home where her father ignores her and her stepmother smiles and hates. Bars, walls and locked doors run all through this book, and here a rich girl’s home starts to look like one more cell.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`52-1`,chapter:52,kind:`craft`,title:`A chapter made of talk`,text:`Almost nothing happens here except a conversation, and it is about poison. Mithridates, Eastern poisoners and a strychnine-laced cabbage passed along a chain of animals: the Count supplies the lore, and Madame de Villefort keeps steering back to it. He leaves pleased that “the seed sown” has found good soil. Dumas tells us there is a seed but not what will grow.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`52-2`,chapter:52,kind:`question`,title:`What do we owe for what we tell?`,text:`The Count only talks, and offers a recipe. He has watched this woman closely, from Perugia to her glance at his medicine case. How much responsibility does a person bear for knowledge handed to someone whose interests he has studied so carefully?`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`52-3`,chapter:52,kind:`craft`,title:`A family in one scene`,text:`Edward torments a parrot and tears up an album while his mother barely scolds him. Valentine comes in sad, and both children are soon sent away. At Perugia, the Count recalls, Madame de Villefort asked about Valentine’s health. Dumas sketches a whole household of favoritism in a few strokes.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`53-1`,chapter:53,kind:`craft`,title:`The audience is the show`,text:`No one at the opera is watching the opera. They watch each other: who sits in whose box, who escorts whom, whose diamonds catch the light. Dumas uses the Paris Opéra as a stage on which society performs for itself. The Count makes his entrance there with Haydée like a leading actor.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`53-2`,chapter:53,kind:`theme`,title:`Haydée’s cry`,text:`Haydée sees Morcerf’s face beside her guardian’s, cries out, and says this man sold her father to the Turks. We knew Fernand had betrayed Dantès. Now we learn of another betrayal, in another country, on which his fortune was built. Notice too that the Count put his arm round Morcerf at the very moment she looked across.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`53-3`,chapter:53,kind:`theme`,title:`A heroine who does not want a husband`,text:`Eugénie Danglars is handsome, cold, gifted in music and languages, and plainly unimpressed by Albert. In a novel where young women are mostly married off, Dumas gives us one who seems to want her own life. Watch how the others describe her, as if strength in a woman were a defect.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`54-1`,chapter:54,kind:`craft`,title:`A joke that hits home`,text:`Albert jokes that Debray, who gets government news first, should feed Madame Danglars a false report to teach her a lesson. Debray is embarrassed. The Count notices, says something quietly, and Debray answers, “Willingly, count; I accept.” Dumas shows us a deal being made without showing its terms.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`54-2`,chapter:54,kind:`theme`,title:`Marriage as a merger`,text:`Albert does not love Eugénie, and his mother opposes the match without saying why. Their fathers got rich together in Spain, and the marriage would join two fortunes. Franz is promised to Valentine on similar terms. In this Paris, a wedding is a business arrangement between families, and the young people are the last to be asked.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`54-3`,chapter:54,kind:`question`,title:`Who is invited to Auteuil?`,text:`The Count plans a dinner at Auteuil for the Danglars and the Villeforts. He tactfully leaves out the Morcerfs and adds two Italian strangers. We know what happened in that garden. Why might the Count want these particular guests at that particular house?`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`55-1`,chapter:55,kind:`craft`,title:`Directing an actor`,text:`The “major” is not sure he was a major, is surprised by his own wealth, and only believes his son is called Andrea. The Count feeds him every line and produces every document. It is pure comedy, and it shows the Count as a playwright and director, casting and rehearsing his players.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`55-2`,chapter:55,kind:`theme`,title:`A past made of paper`,text:`A marriage certificate, a baptism record, a uniform with medals waiting at the hotel: Cavalcanti’s noble past arrives in a parcel. Dumas makes a sharp point about the society the Count is entering. If papers and money are what count, then rank can be bought and a whole family history forged.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`55-3`,chapter:55,kind:`question`,title:`Why a fake nobleman?`,text:`The Count already has wealth and a title of his own. Why does he need a borrowed Italian aristocrat, a lost son and a story about a kidnapping? What could such a pair do in Paris that the Count could not do himself?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`56-1`,chapter:56,kind:`craft`,title:`The peephole`,text:`The Count watches the “reunion” through a hidden peephole, and the two men embrace like actors on a stage. He is the only audience for his own play. Then they compare notes and drop the act, and we see that neither knows what part he has really been cast in.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`56-2`,chapter:56,kind:`theme`,title:`Providence works with forgers`,text:`The Count calls them two scoundrels and says it is a pity they are not really related. The man who claims to be an agent of Providence has chosen a forger and a fraud as his tools. Is that a contradiction, or does he think the wicked are the right instruments for punishing the wicked?`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`56-3`,chapter:56,kind:`motif`,title:`Sinbad signs again`,text:`Andrea’s letter of introduction is signed “Sinbad the Sailor,” and the Count explains Sinbad away as Lord Wilmore. These are the very names behind the Morrels’ rescue. The same aliases now open doors for an impostor, and the Count’s masks serve both mercy and design.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`57-1`,chapter:57,kind:`theme`,title:`The danger of being an heiress`,text:`Valentine has her mother’s fortune and expects more from her grandparents. Her stepmother quietly encouraged her to become a nun, which would have sent it all toward little Edward. Valentine’s wealth does not protect her. It makes her someone else’s obstacle.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`57-2`,chapter:57,kind:`craft`,title:`Two readings of one man`,text:`Maximilian has known the Count barely ten days but feels bound to him, and thinks he lost at cards on purpose so Maximilian could buy his horse. Valentine distrusts him as her stepmother’s friend, a man who has never looked at her. Each judges from what they have seen, and given what each has seen, each is reasonable. Dumas lets the Count look different from every angle.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`57-3`,chapter:57,kind:`question`,title:`Eugénie’s wish`,text:`Valentine reports that Eugénie almost wishes her father would lose his fortune, so that she could be free to live as an artist. For most people in this novel money is the goal. What does it mean that one young woman sees it as a cage?`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`58-1`,chapter:58,kind:`motif`,title:`Imprisoned in a body`,text:`Noirtier can move only his eyes. Remember Faria, struck down by paralysis in his cell, and Dantès buried alive in the Château d’If. Here is another mind locked in a prison, this time his own body. Yet through blinks, an alphabet and a dictionary, the helpless old man becomes the most forceful will in the room.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`58-2`,chapter:58,kind:`theme`,title:`A name from 1815`,text:`The groom is Franz de Quesnel, Baron d’Épinay, and his father was assassinated in 1815. The name Quesnel should ring a bell from Noirtier’s talk with his son in Paris. Villefort speaks pointedly of an old political hatred, and Noirtier answers with contempt. The quarrels of the Hundred Days are being settled through the grandchildren.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`58-3`,chapter:58,kind:`theme`,title:`Father and son, still at war`,text:`Villefort gave up his father’s name and politics to rise under the king. Now he rarely visits the paralyzed old man and talks over him as if he were furniture. The split between the Bonapartist father and the royalist son, first seen in Marseilles, has become a cold war under one roof.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`59-1`,chapter:59,kind:`craft`,title:`The lawman beaten by law`,text:`Villefort, the great prosecutor, watches his speechless father prove his mind is sound to two notaries and dictate a will. Dumas enjoys turning legal form against the man who has always used it as a weapon. Villefort cannot even contest the will, because a man in his position cannot be seen to go to court against the poor.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`59-2`,chapter:59,kind:`theme`,title:`Money as a voice`,text:`Noirtier cannot speak, walk or write. His fortune is the only power he has left, and he uses it to speak. He disinherits the granddaughter he loves to stop a marriage she does not want. In a society that listens to money, 900,000 francs is a louder voice than any words.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`59-3`,chapter:59,kind:`question`,title:`Why is she glad?`,text:`The chapter closes on three faces: Valentine weeps, Villefort bites his lips, and Madame de Villefort cannot hide her joy. The money is going to the poor, not to her son. So why is she so pleased?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`60-1`,chapter:60,kind:`question`,title:`Why hurry the wedding?`,text:`The Count advises Villefort to settle Valentine’s marriage the moment Franz returns, so that it cannot be undone. Villefort is grateful, and his wife changes color. Why should the Count care whom Valentine marries, or how quickly?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`60-2`,chapter:60,kind:`craft`,title:`“Some gloomy legend”`,text:`The Count names his address, Rue de la Fontaine, No. 28, and Villefort goes pale. Then the Count teases that if Villefort stays away he will suspect the house has a gloomy legend. Thanks to Bertuccio, we know the legend. Every light word in this scene lands on a man who has no idea how much his host knows.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`60-3`,chapter:60,kind:`question`,title:`A lonely tower on the Spanish line`,text:`The Count says he is going to see a telegraph, and he chooses an isolated station on the line to Spain rather than an official one. Why would a man of his tastes want to see the machine that carries the government’s messages, and why such an out-of-the-way one?`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`61-1`,chapter:61,kind:`craft`,title:`An idyll before the blow`,text:`Dumas spends most of the chapter on strawberries, dormice and a tiny garden, so the trap closes almost offstage. The gentle comedy is the point: the count's power works through ordinary people who never see the whole machine. The operator repeats signals he does not understand, and Danglars loses a fortune to a message no one meant.`,themes:[]},{id:`61-2`,chapter:61,kind:`context`,title:`News that travels faster than truth`,text:`The optical telegraph was a state monopoly, a chain of towers passing coded arm-signals across France in minutes. Government news reached a minister's office, and the minister's secretary, long before the public. Danglars profits from exactly this inside channel, which is why a single false signal can reach his pocket so quickly.`,themes:[]},{id:`61-3`,chapter:61,kind:`question`,title:`Has he wronged no one?`,text:`The count swears to the trembling operator that he has wronged no one. The man is made comfortable for life, and the loser is a banker speculating on secret tips. Is this harmless, or has the count simply chosen a victim whose losses are easy to cheer?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`62-1`,chapter:62,kind:`craft`,title:`Seen through Bertuccio's eyes`,text:`The guests arrive as a fashionable party, but Dumas lets us watch them through the door with Bertuccio, who knows the house's history. Each name lands twice: once as a social arrival, once as a ghost from the story he told in Chapter 44. The count stands beside him, testing and mocking, plainly enjoying the effect.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`62-2`,chapter:62,kind:`theme`,title:`The guest list as a verdict`,text:`Look at who is gathered under one roof: Danglars, Villefort, Madame Danglars, and the boy Bertuccio dug from this garden. None of them knows what links them, but the count does. Bringing them back to the scene of an old crime is itself a kind of accusation, made without a word being said.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`62-3`,chapter:62,kind:`motif`,title:`Small tells`,text:`Dumas gives each guest a gesture: Madame Danglars slipping a note to Debray, Danglars stripping the orange blossoms, Villefort's trembling hand. These are the details the count is watching for, and the reader is invited to watch the same way. In this book, what people do with their hands often says more than what they say.`,themes:[]},{id:`63-1`,chapter:63,kind:`craft`,title:`Telling a story to its own characters`,text:`The count narrates the house's secret as if he were inventing it, a novelist sketching an imaginary crime. Only two of his listeners know it is not imaginary, and we watch them break. It is a scene within a scene, close to the play Hamlet stages to catch the conscience of a king.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`63-2`,chapter:63,kind:`motif`,title:`Things brought back alive`,text:`The fish carried live across Europe, with spares to prove it, are a showman's boast about wealth. They also echo the dinner's buried subject: something put away for dead that might still be breathing. Villefort's faint question, about who said the child was buried alive, gives the echo away.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`63-3`,chapter:63,kind:`theme`,title:`The judge as the guilty man`,text:`The major's innocent question about child-murderers forces Villefort, the king's prosecutor, to state the penalty aloud. The law he serves is suddenly pointed at himself. Dumas keeps returning to men who sit in judgement while hiding what they have done.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`63-4`,chapter:63,kind:`question`,title:`The red liquid`,text:`Madame de Villefort offers the count her smelling bottle, filled with the red liquid he once used to revive Édouard, and says she has 'made the trial' he advised. Think back to what she and the count talked about at their earlier meeting. What does it tell you that she took his advice so seriously?`,themes:[]},{id:`64-1`,chapter:64,kind:`craft`,title:`Comedy with a knife in it`,text:`The ride back to Paris plays like a farce: a borrowed coat, a borrowed hat, a convict strolling past the barrier as a groom. Underneath, a pistol and a Spanish knife come out and go away again. Dumas often lets humour and menace share a scene, and the tension comes from not knowing which will win.`,themes:[]},{id:`64-2`,chapter:64,kind:`theme`,title:`No one escapes his past here`,text:`Andrea has a new name, a father, a fortune and a seat beside a banker. One hand on the shoulder and he is 'Master Benedetto' again. The past in this novel does not stay buried; it climbs into your carriage and asks for two hundred francs a month.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`64-3`,chapter:64,kind:`context`,title:`Life without papers`,text:`Caderousse cannot simply walk into Paris. The city was ringed by a customs wall with guarded gates, and a man without a passport could be stopped and identified. For an escaped convict, papers and clothes are survival, which is why disguise in this book is so often a practical matter before it is a dramatic one.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`65-1`,chapter:65,kind:`theme`,title:`A marriage run like a bank`,text:`Danglars does not rage about his wife's lovers; he audits them. Tips, percentages, a quarter share of the loss: the marriage is a business partnership, and adultery is tolerated as long as it pays. It is one of the book's coldest portraits of what money does to private life.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`65-2`,chapter:65,kind:`craft`,title:`The name that makes her turn white`,text:`In his long list of grievances, Danglars mentions Villefort almost in passing. Dumas lets one reaction, her sudden pallor, do the work. Danglars thinks he knows everything about his wife; the reader, fresh from the Auteuil dinner, can see the one thing he does not.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`65-3`,chapter:65,kind:`question`,title:`Who is the real spy?`,text:`Danglars boasts that he has watched every step his wife has taken for sixteen years. Yet he is blind to who actually faked the telegraph. How much does anyone in this Paris really see, and who is watching the watchers?`,themes:[]},{id:`66-1`,chapter:66,kind:`craft`,title:`A seed planted in plain sight`,text:`The count does not accuse Morcerf. He simply mentions a name and a place, and suggests Danglars write a letter. Watch how often his schemes work this way: he lets other people's greed or spite carry out the next step, and keeps his own hands clean.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`66-2`,chapter:66,kind:`theme`,title:`Three kinds of fortune`,text:`The count's polite lecture sorts wealth into land, solid capital and credit. Danglars's fortune belongs to the third, which is only as strong as people's belief in it. In a world built on credit, reputation is money, and losing one means losing the other.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`66-3`,chapter:66,kind:`theme`,title:`Old names under new ones`,text:`Danglars sneers that he really is called Danglars, while Morcerf was once Fernand Mondego, a fisherman. In a society full of newly made counts and barons, a humble origin is a weapon. The reader, who knew all three men at Marseilles, can see how much each has rewritten himself.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`67-1`,chapter:67,kind:`craft`,title:`The same night from the other side`,text:`In Chapter 44 Bertuccio told us about the garden at Auteuil from where he was hiding. Now Villefort tells the same night from his side: the birth, the chest, the knife. Dumas builds his mystery like a jigsaw, giving each witness one piece and letting the reader put the picture together first.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`67-2`,chapter:67,kind:`theme`,title:`Afraid of what is not there`,text:`Villefort's terror is that nothing was found, because nothing was there to find. The count's story means he knows more than any honest stranger could. Villefort now feels what he once made others feel: watched, exposed and at the mercy of someone with secret knowledge.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`67-3`,chapter:67,kind:`motif`,title:`Half a napkin`,text:`The baby left at the foundling hospital in half a marked napkin, later claimed by a woman holding the other half, is a classic token of melodrama. Torn objects that must be matched run through this kind of story as a sign of a lost identity.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`68-1`,chapter:68,kind:`craft`,title:`A sudden pallor`,text:`Albert says lightly that he wants a wife like his mother, and the count turns pale. Dumas lets the cool, unreadable count crack for a single moment and moves on without comment. The reader, who knows who Albert's mother is, feels the weight that Albert cannot.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`68-2`,chapter:68,kind:`motif`,title:`Vampire or magician`,text:`Paris cannot decide what the count is: Lord Ruthven, the vampire, or Cagliostro, the conjuror. Both labels come from popular fiction and legend, and both suggest something more or less than human. Notice how often others reach for the supernatural to explain a man whose real secret is far more ordinary and far more painful.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`69-1`,chapter:69,kind:`craft`,title:`Two disguises, one actor`,text:`Dumas stages the inquiry as a double performance: a soft-spoken priest who keeps his face in shadow, then a spiteful Englishman with red whiskers. The reveal at the end turns the chapter into a showcase of the count's craft. He does not just hide his identity; he writes the official version of it for the police to find.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`69-2`,chapter:69,kind:`theme`,title:`The investigator investigated`,text:`Villefort goes in disguise to unmask the count, and comes home satisfied. He has been handed a story, and he believes it because it came through official channels. The prosecutor who built his career on reading men is now being read, and managed, by one.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`69-3`,chapter:69,kind:`question`,title:`How much of the lie is true?`,text:`Zaccone's story mixes fiction with things that sound oddly close to the count's real life: a prison escape by swimming, years in the East, treasure found in a hidden place. Why might a master of disguise build his lies so near the truth?`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`70-1`,chapter:70,kind:`craft`,title:`Every eye turns`,text:`Dumas builds the count's entrance by delay: guest after guest asks whether he is coming, rumours circulate, the police 'secret' is passed around. By the time he walks in, the whole room is primed. It is a novelist's version of a star's entrance, and it shows how much of his power is reputation.`,themes:[]},{id:`70-2`,chapter:70,kind:`motif`,title:`The refused ice`,text:`The count will not take so much as a drink in this house, and Mercédès notices. A small social refusal becomes the centre of the scene. In a novel full of feasts, what a man will not eat, and where, can say a great deal.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`70-3`,chapter:70,kind:`motif`,title:`Forget-me-nots`,text:`Across a crowded room, Valentine raises her bouquet of forget-me-nots to her lips. It is a silent message in the language of flowers, the only kind of speech open to lovers watched by their families. Notice how much of the book's love is carried by signs rather than words.`,themes:[]},{id:`71-1`,chapter:71,kind:`theme`,title:`Friends, or not?`,text:`Mercédès asks twice whether they are friends. The first time he says yes in words while refusing the grapes. The second time he answers only that he is her most respectful servant. His actions and his words point in opposite directions, and she reads the actions.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`71-2`,chapter:71,kind:`craft`,title:`The past told as a parable`,text:`'At Malta', the count says, he loved a girl and lost her to war and a rival. He tells his own story with the places changed, to the one person who lived it with him. Every line is double, and Dumas trusts the reader to hear both meanings at once.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`71-3`,chapter:71,kind:`question`,title:`Does he mean it?`,text:`He says he has forgiven the girl and does not hate those who separated them. Given everything we have watched him set in motion, can we believe him? Or is he telling Mercédès what she needs to hear, and perhaps what he needs to believe?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`72-1`,chapter:72,kind:`craft`,title:`Details that refuse to settle`,text:`The marquis's lozenges and unnatural sleep, the marquise's bottle of orangeade, a white figure from the dressing-room door, a glass being moved in the night. The family reads these as grief and ghosts. Dumas sets them down plainly and lets the reader feel uneasy without being told why.`,themes:[]},{id:`72-2`,chapter:72,kind:`theme`,title:`Villefort counts his enemies`,text:`Villefort keeps a secret list of men he has wronged, and decides none of them would wait so long. It is a striking admission: he has made enough enemies to need a list. He reasons carefully and still cannot imagine an enemy patient enough to wait for years.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`72-3`,chapter:72,kind:`context`,title:`Rank against love`,text:`Valentine cannot confess her love to her grandmother because Maximilian is not noble. For an old royalist family, marrying a merchant's son meant sinking in the world, however honourable the man. Her marriage is being treated as a matter of family politics, settled by the old, and the dying.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`73-1`,chapter:73,kind:`theme`,title:`Who gains?`,text:`D'Avrigny's diagnosis turns grief into suspicion, and Villefort himself names the one person who profits: Valentine. The doctor and the prosecutor both reach for the logic of the courtroom. Notice how quickly an innocent-seeming household becomes a list of suspects.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`73-2`,chapter:73,kind:`craft`,title:`The listener in the garden`,text:`Maximilian, hiding by the wall, overhears a secret the family has agreed to bury. Dumas often uses an eavesdropper this way, so the reader learns something important alongside a character who cannot act on it. It ties Morrel into a mystery he must keep to himself.`,themes:[]},{id:`73-3`,chapter:73,kind:`motif`,title:`Speech without a voice`,text:`Noirtier cannot move or speak, yet he overrules both of Morrel's plans and promises help. Watch how Dumas makes the most powerless body in the house one of its strongest wills. His eyes and his secrets are his only tools, and they are enough to make everyone wait on him.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`74-1`,chapter:74,kind:`craft`,title:`Gossip in a mourning coach`,text:`Dumas lets four young men discuss the deaths on the way to the cemetery: inheritance, Valentine's fortune, the 'old Jacobin'. Their chatter shows what society sees, a convenient chain of deaths and a rich bride, while the reader knows what the doctor fears. The gap between the two is where the suspense lives.`,themes:[]},{id:`74-2`,chapter:74,kind:`theme`,title:`Haste over the graves`,text:`Villefort pushes the contract through on the day of the funeral, citing a dying wish. Grief is made to serve a plan. Notice how readily the respectable people of this book use the dead to get what they want from the living.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`74-3`,chapter:74,kind:`question`,title:`Who is the 'weak' old man?`,text:`Villefort assures Franz that Noirtier's mind is so weak he has forgotten Franz's name. Moments later Noirtier demands to see him by that very name. What does Villefort misunderstand about his father, and why might he want so badly to believe him harmless?`,themes:[]},{id:`75-1`,chapter:75,kind:`theme`,title:`A politics of blood`,text:`The statement shows a Bonapartist club, a royalist general, an oath and a duel on frozen steps. The same weeks of early 1815 that ruined Dantès also produced this death. Politics in this novel is never abstract: it decides who marries whom a generation later.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`75-2`,chapter:75,kind:`craft`,title:`One letter at a time`,text:`The name is revealed through the dictionary, letter M, word by word, until Noirtier stops at 'myself'. Dumas turns a physical limitation into a device for suspense. The slow spelling makes the revelation hit like a blow.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`75-3`,chapter:75,kind:`context`,title:`The Pharaon and the Elba letter`,text:`The club's letter from Elba promises more news when the Pharaon arrives: Morrel's ship, the one Dantès sailed home. The book's opening chapters and this Paris drawing room turn out to be parts of the same conspiracy. Noirtier was the man the letter Dantès carried was meant for.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`75-4`,chapter:75,kind:`question`,title:`Duel or murder?`,text:`The witnesses signed to prove it was an honourable fight, and Noirtier was wounded three times. Yet the general was killed and his body thrown into the river. Does the code of honour make it a fair duel, or only give a killing a respectable name?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`76-1`,chapter:76,kind:`theme`,title:`How Paris makes a prince`,text:`In two weeks Andrea becomes 'count', then 'Prince Cavalcanti', heir to riches rumoured in marble quarries. No one has checked anything; Paris has simply agreed to believe. Dumas shows how a title and a bank account create a person out of nothing.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`76-2`,chapter:76,kind:`craft`,title:`A Corsican song`,text:`The count smiles to hear Andrea sing a Corsican song, which reminds him of Benedetto. It is a tiny moment of private amusement. The reader shares a secret with the count that the singing room, and the whole Danglars family, does not.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`76-3`,chapter:76,kind:`craft`,title:`A look of pity`,text:`When the courier from Greece arrives, the count turns aside to hide 'a look of pity' and takes Albert away. Pity for whom? Dumas lets one guarded expression hint that the count's feelings about the Morcerfs are more mixed than his plans.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`77-1`,chapter:77,kind:`craft`,title:`A story with two names left out`,text:`Haydée tells the fall of Yanina but names neither the traitor nor the treason, on the count's orders. Albert asks twice and is told twice that she cannot remember. The reader, who has heard Fernand's name linked to Yanina, feels the gap far more than Albert does.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`77-2`,chapter:77,kind:`motif`,title:`Dagger or ring`,text:`Selim waits beside the gunpowder for one of two signs: the dagger to blow everything up, the ring to stand down. The ring arrives, and it is a lie. A sign of trust becomes the instrument of betrayal, an image that sums up much of the book.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`77-3`,chapter:77,kind:`theme`,title:`Another prisoner`,text:`Haydée was sold with her mother, passed from merchant to sultan, and bought with an emerald. Her story of slavery and loss runs parallel to Dantès's own, a life stolen by a betrayal she had no part in. The count has gathered around him people the world wronged.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`77-4`,chapter:77,kind:`context`,title:`Ali Pasha was real`,text:`Ali Tepelini, Pasha of Yanina, was a real Ottoman governor who ruled much of Albania and northern Greece almost as an independent prince. He rebelled against the sultan and was killed in 1822, and his head was sent to Constantinople. Dumas weaves his invented French officer into a story his readers would have known from the newspapers.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`78-1`,chapter:78,kind:`craft`,title:`Rumour moves first`,text:`A few lines in a newspaper, with only the first name 'Fernand', are enough to start everything. Beauchamp says no one would connect it to Morcerf, yet Albert does at once. Dumas shows how the press can wound a reputation before anyone knows whether the story is true.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`78-2`,chapter:78,kind:`motif`,title:`Cards shot into cards`,text:`At the shooting gallery the count has shot playing cards so precisely that they look like other cards. It is a showpiece of skill, and also a neat image of how he works: he takes something ordinary and, with exact aim, turns it into something else.`,themes:[]},{id:`78-3`,chapter:78,kind:`question`,title:`Why won't he be Albert's second?`,text:`The count refuses to act as Albert's second, refuses to give lessons, and says Albert may learn why 'at some future time'. He also suggests Albert ask Haydée what happened. What do his refusals and his one suggestion tell you about where he stands?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`79-1`,chapter:79,kind:`craft`,title:`A visitor on the stairs`,text:`In the middle of Barrois's agony, Madame de Villefort comes down slowly, looks first at Noirtier, asks whether the servant has eaten, and leaves. Dumas writes her behaviour precisely and without comment. The reader is left to weigh each gesture.`,themes:[]},{id:`79-2`,chapter:79,kind:`theme`,title:`Poison in the safest room`,text:`The lemonade is Noirtier's own, made by his loyal servant and carried in by his loving granddaughter. Poison is the opposite of the duel: hidden, patient, working through trust. It turns the ordinary care of a household, a drink offered to a tired man, into a threat.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`79-3`,chapter:79,kind:`context`,title:`Chemistry as detective work`,text:`D'Avrigny's test with syrup of violets reflects the new forensic chemistry of the 1830s and 1840s, when poison trials caused a sensation in France. The doctor proves a crime in a teacup. Science, not confession, now decides what happened in the room.`,themes:[]},{id:`80-1`,chapter:80,kind:`theme`,title:`The prosecutor's logic turned inward`,text:`'Look for whoever profits' is the rule Villefort has applied to others all his life. Now it points at his own daughter, and he is the one who first says her name. The question is whether he will apply the law to his own house as coldly as he once applied it to strangers.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`80-2`,chapter:80,kind:`craft`,title:`The faint smile`,text:`The chapter ends not on the doctor's accusation but on a glance: Villefort sees, or thinks he sees, a faint gloomy smile on his wife's lips. Dumas leaves it uncertain. The reader has been shown more than the doctor, and the gap is the source of dread.`,themes:[]},{id:`80-3`,chapter:80,kind:`question`,title:`What do the servants see?`,text:`Every servant leaves, saying death is in the house, while praising Valentine as they go. The doctor suspects her; those who live beside her do not. Whose judgement do you trust, and why?`,themes:[]},{id:`81-1`,chapter:81,kind:`craft`,title:`A slip of the tongue`,text:`'Very good, father-in-law' gives Andrea away for a moment: no young aristocrat would talk that way. Dumas uses speech as a marker of class, and lets the costume slip just enough for the reader to see the seams.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`81-2`,chapter:81,kind:`theme`,title:`The count's pattern, in miniature`,text:`Andrea now does to Caderousse what the count does to his enemies: he finds a man's weakness, his greed, and lets it lead him into a trap. The plan of the house and the hint about Auteuil are bait. The novel shows a second, much meaner kind of plotter at work.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`81-3`,chapter:81,kind:`question`,title:`Why the day after tomorrow?`,text:`Andrea gives Caderousse his ring and privately thinks he won't sell it, 'not at least till the day after tomorrow'. What does Andrea expect will happen before then? Read his calm thought again in light of the plan he has just drawn.`,themes:[]},{id:`82-1`,chapter:82,kind:`craft`,title:`The abbé returns`,text:`The count chooses to meet Caderousse as the Abbé Busoni, the face that gave Caderousse the diamond years ago. He puts on the cassock over a coat of mail: a priest's costume over a soldier's armour. The image sums up his double role as confessor and avenger.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`82-2`,chapter:82,kind:`question`,title:`Did he give Caderousse a fair chance?`,text:`The count promises to forgive Caderousse if he gets home safely, while watching the lookout move to where he will climb down. Is this a real second chance, or a sentence dressed as one? Notice what the count sees from the window and what he chooses not to say.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`82-3`,chapter:82,kind:`motif`,title:`The third betrayal`,text:`Caderousse betrayed Dantès by silence, the abbé's trust by murder, and now stabs the man he believes is a priest. Each time he is offered something, he reaches for more. Dumas builds his character on a single repeated choice.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`83-1`,chapter:83,kind:`theme`,title:`Leaving it to God`,text:`The count did not strike Caderousse; he simply did not warn him. He calls this God's judgement. Is standing aside the same as innocence, or is it just another way of acting? The chapter's title makes the claim; the scene invites you to test it.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`83-2`,chapter:83,kind:`craft`,title:`A life read back as a charge sheet`,text:`Over the dying man, the count recites Caderousse's history: drunken betrayal, the wasted diamond, the jeweller, the galleys, the rescue, the knife. It is structured like a prosecutor's summing-up. The novel's long middle stretch is gathered into one moment of reckoning.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`83-3`,chapter:83,kind:`motif`,title:`Removing the wig`,text:`For the first time the count strips away a disguise to show one of his enemies who he really is. The name is whispered, so even the reader does not hear it spoken. Unmasking is the climax he has been saving, and it terrifies more than any blow.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`83-4`,chapter:83,kind:`question`,title:`'One!'`,text:`The count counts Caderousse's death as the first, as if checking off a list. Yet Caderousse was not one of the men who wrote the letter. Does this single word make the count more frightening, or more honest about what he is doing?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`84-1`,chapter:84,kind:`theme`,title:`A friend who burns the proof`,text:`Beauchamp travels to Yanina, learns the truth, and gives it to Albert to destroy. In a book crowded with betrayals, this is pure loyalty: honour put above a scoop. It stands out sharply against Danglars and Fernand at their worst.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`84-2`,chapter:84,kind:`theme`,title:`The sins of the fathers`,text:`Beauchamp says children cannot be blamed for their fathers' faults. The whole chapter tests that idea. Albert has done nothing wrong, yet the shame lands on him, and his first fear is for his mother.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`84-3`,chapter:84,kind:`craft`,title:`Not over yet`,text:`The proofs are burned and the friends embrace, but Beauchamp warns that it may not be over, and asks about the broken Danglars engagement. Dumas closes the chapter on relief while keeping an uneasy question open.`,themes:[]},{id:`85-1`,chapter:85,kind:`motif`,title:`Like Pilate`,text:`The count says he forwards Cavalcanti's papers 'like Pilate', washing his hands of the marriage. Pilate washed his hands to disclaim a death he allowed. It is a telling comparison to choose, and it fits a man who prefers to let events take their course.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`85-2`,chapter:85,kind:`craft`,title:`The calm before`,text:`Three days of hunting and fishing by the sea, a gallop across France, a sloop flying the count's arms. Dumas slows the pace to near holiday. The newspaper that ends the idyll lands harder because of it.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`85-3`,chapter:85,kind:`question`,title:`Why bring Albert here?`,text:`The count invites Albert to the coast just before the second article appears. Is it kindness, keeping him away from Paris for a few calm days, or something else? Think about what the count knew and when.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`86-1`,chapter:86,kind:`craft`,title:`A trial seen from the gallery`,text:`We watch the hearing through Beauchamp, hidden in a gallery. Morcerf nearly wins with letters, a ring and a smooth story, and then a veiled woman walks in. Dumas builds the scene like a courtroom drama, with the surprise witness held back to the last moment.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`86-2`,chapter:86,kind:`theme`,title:`The victim speaks`,text:`Haydée testifies in person, with documents, and points to the scar on his hand. The man who sold her as a child is condemned by her voice. Revenge here takes the form of public truth-telling, and it is she, not the count, who stands up to do it.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`86-3`,chapter:86,kind:`question`,title:`Did the count know?`,text:`Haydée tells the peers the count is in Normandy and knows nothing of what she is doing. Do you believe her? Consider who bought her, who taught her to tell her story, and where the count happened to be that week.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`87-1`,chapter:87,kind:`theme`,title:`An enemy of flesh and blood`,text:`Beauchamp talks of Providence; Albert wants someone he can fight. That is the dilemma of the whole revenge plot seen from the other side: when a fall looks like fate, the victim looks for a hand behind it.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`87-2`,chapter:87,kind:`craft`,title:`Albert assembles the evidence`,text:`Albert lists what the count did: bought Haydée, advised Danglars to write, let Haydée tell her story, forbade the name, took him to Normandy. The reader has seen each of these steps. Dumas lets a character finally put together what we have been watching, which shifts how we see the count's earlier kindness.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`88-1`,chapter:88,kind:`motif`,title:`The meal refused, remembered`,text:`Albert reminds his mother that the count would eat nothing in their house, an Eastern sign of an enemy. The small detail from the ball now becomes evidence. Mercédès's reaction tells us she has understood more than she says.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`88-2`,chapter:88,kind:`context`,title:`Honour in public`,text:`The insult happens at the Opera, in front of society, which is the point. Under the code of honour, a public affront could only be answered by a duel, whatever the rights and wrongs. Both men are now trapped by the rules of their class.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`88-3`,chapter:88,kind:`theme`,title:`'I will make him suffer'`,text:`When Morrel pleads that Albert's father loves him, the count flares up. Killing the son is meant to wound the father. The line shows how revenge spreads past its target to the innocent people around him.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`89-1`,chapter:89,kind:`craft`,title:`His first name`,text:`'Edmond' is the first word Mercédès says. No one has called him that since he came to Paris. One name strips away every title and disguise, and the count has to answer as the man he was.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`89-2`,chapter:89,kind:`motif`,title:`The yellowed letter`,text:`The count produces the actual denunciation from 1815, taken from the prison file. The paper that began everything has been carried through the whole book. Placing it in Mercédès's hands makes the past physically present in the room.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`89-3`,chapter:89,kind:`theme`,title:`Two kinds of suffering`,text:`He had fourteen years in a dungeon; she had ten years of nightmares about his death. He has turned his pain into a plan; she has turned hers into guilt. The scene asks whether her suffering counts against his, and whether it changes what he is owed.`,themes:[`hope-despair`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`89-4`,chapter:89,kind:`question`,title:`Why give in?`,text:`The count has planned for years, yet one visit from Mercédès makes him promise to let Albert live and to die himself. What has she reached in him that no argument of Morrel's could? And why does he think he cannot simply decline the duel?`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`90-1`,chapter:90,kind:`theme`,title:`A will instead of a plan`,text:`Facing death, the count stops scheming and starts giving: twenty million for Morrel, the rest for Haydée, and a note that his enemies' punishment is only delayed. A will reveals what a person values. This one shows both his love and his refusal to let go of revenge.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`90-2`,chapter:90,kind:`craft`,title:`Haydée at the door`,text:`Finding Haydée asleep outside his room, the count realizes he forgot he had a daughter. Dumas lets the private world he neglected interrupt the public one he built. Her tearing up the will shows a love that does not need his money.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`90-3`,chapter:90,kind:`theme`,title:`Restored to certainty`,text:`Albert's apology, wrung from a family secret, convinces the count once more that he is God's instrument. One night he despaired; the next morning he feels sure again. Notice how quickly his faith in his mission returns, and what it takes to bring it back.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`90-4`,chapter:90,kind:`question`,title:`Who is braver?`,text:`Albert publicly takes back his challenge, and Château-Renaud and Debray sneer. By the code of honour he has disgraced himself; by another standard he has done the hardest thing in the scene. Which standard does the novel ask you to use?`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`91-1`,chapter:91,kind:`craft`,title:`Two packings, one gesture`,text:`In separate rooms, without consulting each other, Albert and Mercédès do exactly the same thing: they leave everything in order, make an inventory, and take almost nothing. Dumas mirrors the two scenes so that we see a shared moral instinct without a word of discussion. Set against them is the pale face of the father at the window, the man who gained all this property and now keeps none of his family.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`91-2`,chapter:91,kind:`motif`,title:`Treasure under a fig tree`,text:`Buried treasure has driven this whole story, from Spada’s hoard to the count’s limitless credit. Here it shrinks to 150 louis, a young sailor’s savings, hidden in his father’s garden for his bride and found untouched after twenty-four years. Of everything the count could give, the gift that reaches Mercédès is the one that belongs to the time before.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`91-3`,chapter:91,kind:`theme`,title:`Giving up a name`,text:`In this novel names are bought, invented and polished: Morcerf itself was acquired, and Paris is full of a “Prince” Cavalcanti. Albert does the opposite and takes his mother’s maiden name, Herrera, because the one he was born with is no longer honest. Notice that he and Mercédès are the first Parisians to choose a smaller identity rather than a grander one.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`92-1`,chapter:92,kind:`craft`,title:`Unmasking by costume`,text:`The count does not simply say who he is. He leaves the room and comes back in a sailor’s jacket and hat, and lets Morcerf say the name himself. Identity in this book has always been a matter of clothes and titles, so the great revelation is staged as a disguise worn in reverse.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`92-2`,chapter:92,kind:`theme`,title:`“Two Mercédès in the world”`,text:`Before Morcerf arrives, Dumas gives us a quiet scene: Haydée’s joy, and the count wondering whether he might still be happy. For hundreds of pages his life has seemed to be nothing but his mission. This is the first time he allows himself to imagine a future that is not about the past.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`92-3`,chapter:92,kind:`craft`,title:`Watching from behind the curtain`,text:`Dumas places us with Morcerf, hidden, as his wife and son walk down the stairs and away. Neither looks back, and Albert’s words, “this is no longer our home,” are the real sentence passed on him. The shot itself is reported from outside, at the moment the coach passes through the gate, so his death is tied to their leaving.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`92-4`,chapter:92,kind:`question`,title:`Whose hand?`,text:`The count never touched Morcerf. He exposed the truth, refused a duel, and named himself; Morcerf did the rest. Is the count responsible for this death, or did Morcerf’s own past simply catch up with him? Consider how the count himself would answer, and whether you agree.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`93-1`,chapter:93,kind:`craft`,title:`Poison among household things`,text:`The danger arrives through the most ordinary objects: a glass of sugared water, a decanter, spoonfuls of medicine. Dumas even lets little Édouard empty the decanter to make a pond for his ducks, a child’s game that destroys the evidence. Watch Noirtier, who can do nothing but fix his eyes on the door, and how much tension Dumas draws from a man who cannot move.`,themes:[]},{id:`93-2`,chapter:93,kind:`theme`,title:`Two daughters, two kinds of captivity`,text:`In the same room, Eugénie announces that she wants to be a free artist and cares nothing for the man she is to marry, while Valentine is shut in a house where she cannot even be sure what she drinks. Dumas places the two young women in the same scene so we feel how narrow the choices were for daughters of good family, and how differently each one meets them.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`94-1`,chapter:94,kind:`theme`,title:`“Between the culprit and the victim I have no choice”`,text:`The count admits he has been watching the Villefort house as if it were God’s justice at work, and he tells Morrel to look away. It is his clearest statement of the role he has given himself: not a judge who intervenes but a spectator of punishment. Then Morrel says Valentine’s name, and the spectator is suddenly involved.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`94-2`,chapter:94,kind:`craft`,title:`The lion roars`,text:`The count has been almost inhumanly composed for most of the book. Here he “roars like a wounded lion” and calls Valentine the daughter of an accursed race, before mastering himself within moments. Dumas rarely lets us see the passion under the mask, so moments like this show how much the calm costs him.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`94-3`,chapter:94,kind:`motif`,title:`Poison as medicine`,text:`Noirtier has been giving Valentine small doses of his own medicine so that her body grows used to it. This is the Mithridates method the count described to Madame de Villefort in their conversation about toxicology. The same knowledge that serves the poisoner now serves the protector, and the line between remedy and weapon is just a matter of dose.`,themes:[]},{id:`94-4`,chapter:94,kind:`craft`,title:`A name only the reader understands`,text:`The chapter ends with an Italian priest, Signor Busoni, renting the house next door to the Villeforts. Nobody in the house thinks twice about it. We know who Busoni is, and Dumas trusts us to feel the click without a word of comment.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`95-1`,chapter:95,kind:`craft`,title:`Stepping back in time`,text:`This chapter goes back to the morning of the same day, before the visit that ended with Valentine’s collapse. Dumas, writing for serial publication, often doubles back like this to follow a second thread up to the moment where the first one stopped. Notice how it changes Eugénie’s cool behaviour at the Villeforts’ once you know what she agreed to that morning.`,themes:[]},{id:`95-2`,chapter:95,kind:`context`,title:`A banker’s credit is his life`,text:`Danglars is nearly ruined, yet he can survive if people simply believe he is rich. In the speculative Paris of the 1830s and 1840s, with railway concessions and foreign bonds, a bank ran on confidence, and a rumour of three million could matter more than the money itself. Eugénie grasps this exactly when she tells him to rely on the rumour.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`95-3`,chapter:95,kind:`theme`,title:`A daughter as an asset`,text:`Danglars drops the mask of the indulgent father and states plainly that the marriage is business. Eugénie answers in the same language and bargains: she will sign, on condition that she stays free in her person. It is one of the most unsentimental father-daughter scenes in the novel, and both of them are better at it than at affection.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`96-1`,chapter:96,kind:`craft`,title:`The count withdraws his hand`,text:`When Andrea thanks the count for his success, the count calmly lists who vouched for Andrea: Lord Wilmore, the Abbé Busoni, a father’s name. Every one of those is a mask the reader knows. The count is stepping back from the young man he launched, in words that are technically true and entirely misleading.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`96-2`,chapter:96,kind:`craft`,title:`Exposure at the moment of signing`,text:`Dumas times the blow for maximum public effect: the contract read aloud, the baroness signing, the notary calling for “Prince Cavalcanti,” then soldiers at the doors. Compare Morcerf’s exposure in the Chamber of Peers. In both cases the fall is staged before an audience, because in this society shame only counts if it is witnessed.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`96-3`,chapter:96,kind:`question`,title:`What the reader knows about this marriage`,text:`We know, as the guests do not, who Andrea’s parents are, and so we know what kind of match Danglars was about to celebrate. Does that knowledge make this scene comic, horrifying, or both? Think about how much of the count’s plan seems to depend on secrets the people involved do not even know they share.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`97-1`,chapter:97,kind:`theme`,title:`Freedom in a man’s coat`,text:`Eugénie cuts off her hair and travels as “M. Léon d’Armilly, artist.” For a woman of her time, freedom of movement, money of one’s own and a career on the stage were far easier to claim as a man. In a novel full of disguises used to deceive and punish, this one is used simply to get out.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`97-2`,chapter:97,kind:`craft`,title:`“M. Danglars no longer had a daughter”`,text:`The chapter ends on a single dry sentence. Dumas could have shown us Danglars discovering the empty room; instead he closes the door on it and lets the sentence do the work. Notice how often a chapter here ends on a line built to be quoted.`,themes:[]},{id:`98-1`,chapter:98,kind:`craft`,title:`Coincidence as comedy`,text:`Of all the rooms in all the inns on the road north, Andrea falls down the chimney into the one where Eugénie is sleeping. Dumas does not pretend this is likely; he plays it for farce, down to the rooftop chase and the brigadier at the keyhole. The novel can move from tragedy to comic adventure in a single chapter, and this is one of its lightest moments.`,themes:[]},{id:`98-2`,chapter:98,kind:`question`,title:`“One has friends”`,text:`Andrea throws down his knife rather than use it on himself, confident that someone will protect him. Who does he think that friend is, and what has he done to deserve one? Consider how much of Andrea’s career so far has depended on a patron whose motives he has never understood.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`99-1`,chapter:99,kind:`craft`,title:`Pleading for a stranger who is not one`,text:`Madame Danglars begs Villefort to go easy on “the impostor” to spare her family scandal, and Villefort refuses. Neither of them knows what the reader knows: that this convict is their own child, the baby born at Auteuil and buried in the garden. Dumas builds the whole scene on that gap, so every word they say about Benedetto lands differently on us.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`99-2`,chapter:99,kind:`theme`,title:`A prosecutor who knows he is guilty`,text:`Villefort swears before a crucifix that the culprits in his house will die, and then admits he has sinned, perhaps more deeply than others. Every criminal he condemns, he says, proves he is not the only wicked man. Notice how his severity as a judge has become a way of managing his own guilt.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`99-3`,chapter:99,kind:`motif`,title:`The house as a plague hospital`,text:`The Villefort house now looks like a lazaretto, with a suspicious porter and neighbours wondering aloud about the next funeral. Dumas keeps returning to images of contagion for this family, as if guilt were a disease that spreads through the walls. Watch how the house itself becomes a character.`,themes:[]},{id:`100-1`,chapter:100,kind:`craft`,title:`Ghost story, then explanation`,text:`Dumas writes the count’s entrance as a Gothic apparition: a feverish girl, a door that opens without a sound, a figure at the bed. Only after Valentine, and we, have been frightened does he give the practical explanation of the hidden door and the swapped drinks. The scene gets both thrills, the supernatural and the rational, one after the other.`,themes:[]},{id:`100-2`,chapter:100,kind:`theme`,title:`The watcher behind the wall`,text:`For four sleepless nights the count has watched Valentine through a hidden door and replaced poison with a safe draught. It is the same power he has used everywhere, to see what others cannot and to arrange events unseen. Here, for once, that godlike watching is used to keep someone alive.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`101-1`,chapter:101,kind:`context`,title:`Why “Locusta”`,text:`Locusta was the poisoner of ancient Rome said to have supplied the drug that killed Emperor Claudius so that Nero could inherit. The title points at the motive the count spells out here: a mother clearing the way for her son’s inheritance. Remember the count’s remark, in the conversation about toxicology, about Lady Macbeth wanting a throne for her son.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`101-2`,chapter:101,kind:`question`,title:`The count’s share in this`,text:`The count reminds Valentine of the man in the brown cloak at Perugia whom her stepmother questioned about aqua tofana. We know that man was the count, and that he later talked poisons with Madame de Villefort and sent her a recipe. How much of what has happened in this house did he cause, and how much did he merely allow?`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`101-3`,chapter:101,kind:`craft`,title:`Seeing through half-closed eyes`,text:`Most of this chapter is filtered through Valentine pretending to sleep: a whisper, a white dressing-gown, a phial emptied into a glass. Dumas limits us to what she can glimpse, so the identity of the poisoner arrives as a shock of recognition. Then he hands her, and us, a strange instruction: whatever happens, even waking in a tomb, do not be afraid.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`102-1`,chapter:102,kind:`craft`,title:`The poisoner by the bed`,text:`Dumas lets us stay with Madame de Villefort as she inspects her work, caught “between fascination and remorse” until the lamp goes out. It is a rare scene inside the mind of a murderer, and she is not a cartoon villain. Then comes the detail that shakes her: the glass she emptied is a third full again.`,themes:[]},{id:`102-2`,chapter:102,kind:`question`,title:`The count’s part in this night`,text:`The house declares Valentine dead, and the doctor’s test proves poison. Yet only hours earlier the count was in this room, tasting the glass and giving her a pill from his emerald box. What do you make of his part in what happened that night?`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`103-1`,chapter:103,kind:`theme`,title:`An avenger, not a priest`,text:`Morrel refuses to say a quiet farewell: Valentine, he says, needs an avenger. The word puts this gentle young soldier on the same path the count has walked for years. Notice that the call for vengeance now comes from the side of the victims of a Villefort, and that Villefort himself is the one who must answer it.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`103-2`,chapter:103,kind:`craft`,title:`Noirtier’s silent verdict`,text:`Dumas keeps Noirtier’s accusation off the page. We see only Villefort coming back livid, holding the shreds of a quill pen, and swearing a terrible revenge within three days. The scene is more powerful for what is left out, and it shows the paralysed old man as the most formidable figure in the room.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`103-3`,chapter:103,kind:`craft`,title:`The abbé from next door`,text:`Villefort sends for the nearest priest and gets an Italian abbé who already knows Valentine’s name. We know who lives next door. Notice the look between the abbé and Noirtier, and what it seems to give the old man.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`104-1`,chapter:104,kind:`theme`,title:`Ruin by receipt`,text:`The count does not steal from Danglars. He simply draws on the credit Danglars himself opened for him, at the one moment the banker cannot afford it, and Danglars is too proud to refuse. As with Morcerf, the trap is built out of the victim’s own character: here, vanity about his wealth.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`104-2`,chapter:104,kind:`craft`,title:`Money set against money`,text:`In the same chapter Danglars is about to flee with money owed to the hospitals, and Boville mentions that Mercédès and Albert have given their whole fortune to those same hospitals. Dumas lets the two facts sit side by side without comment. The contrast tells us how to judge both families.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`104-3`,chapter:104,kind:`motif`,title:`The old names return`,text:`M. de Boville, the prison inspector the count bought the Morrel debts from long ago, reappears as Receiver-General of the charities. Thomson & French, the bank behind the Morrel rescue, is the bank on the receipt. Dumas is gathering threads from the Marseilles chapters into the Paris ending.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`105-1`,chapter:105,kind:`theme`,title:`Revealing himself to save, not to punish`,text:`Morcerf heard the name Edmond Dantès as a sentence. Maximilian hears it as a reason to live: the man who saved his father, sent the purse and returned the Pharaon. It is the same revelation, used for the opposite purpose, and the count stops Maximilian before he can say it aloud to Julie and Emmanuel.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`105-2`,chapter:105,kind:`motif`,title:`The red silk purse`,text:`Julie fetches the purse the unknown benefactor left long ago, kept as a family relic. In a novel of vast sums, it is one of the few objects that carries feeling rather than value. It ties this scene back to the count’s first act of gratitude, before any revenge began.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`loyalty-betrayal`]},{id:`105-3`,chapter:105,kind:`question`,title:`Why ask for a month?`,text:`The count does not argue Maximilian out of despair. He asks only for time, one month, until 5 October, and promises the means of a painless death if hope has not returned. Why would he bargain like this rather than simply forbid it? Think about what the count himself learned about waiting in his own years of despair.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`106-1`,chapter:106,kind:`craft`,title:`One staircase, two households`,text:`Dumas sets two scenes in the same plain building: downstairs, Debray coolly divides millions with his mistress and drops her; upstairs, Mercédès and Albert count out the last of their francs. The house is a cross-section of the novel’s moral world. Notice which household is richer by the end of the chapter, and which is happier.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`106-2`,chapter:106,kind:`theme`,title:`Earning a name`,text:`Albert has sold his watch and enlisted in the Spahis as a paid substitute. He means to earn a name of his own, in six months an officer or dead. His father bought a title and a fortune; Albert chooses to start where Edmond Dantès once started, with nothing but his work.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`106-3`,chapter:106,kind:`context`,title:`A wife’s money was never her own`,text:`Danglars’ parting letter leaves his wife “rich, but little respected,” and suspects her of hiding his fortune. Under the Napoleonic Code a married woman could not freely own or manage property, which is why Madame Danglars has speculated in secret through Debray. Her secret fortune is both her rescue and the measure of her disgrace.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`107-1`,chapter:107,kind:`craft`,title:`The reader knows more than the prisoner`,text:`Andrea hints that his secret protector, perhaps his real father, is the Count of Monte Cristo. We know better: we heard Bertuccio’s story of Auteuil, and Villefort’s confession to Madame Danglars. Dumas lets Andrea’s guess hang in the air, and Bertuccio’s stern refusal tells us how wrong it is.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`107-2`,chapter:107,kind:`motif`,title:`The prince in the lions’ den`,text:`Andrea’s fine clothes are torn but carefully brushed, and he still plays the Cavalcanti prince to keepers who laugh at him. He survives the convicts only with a bandits’ sign learned from Caderousse. The two identities, prince and galley slave, sit side by side in one cell.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`108-1`,chapter:108,kind:`theme`,title:`Justice for the family name`,text:`Villefort does not send his wife to trial. He tells her to poison herself, so that his name and his son will be spared the scaffold and the scandal. Notice what he is protecting: not the law he serves, but the reputation of the Villeforts.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`wealth-status`]},{id:`108-2`,chapter:108,kind:`craft`,title:`The cup of chocolate`,text:`Villefort drinks his wife’s chocolate in one gulp, almost hoping it is poisoned. One small gesture tells us how far gone he is: a man so weary of his own house that death would be a relief. Dumas often reveals character this way, through an action rather than a speech.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`108-3`,chapter:108,kind:`question`,title:`Is Villefort right?`,text:`Madame de Villefort begs for her life for Edward’s sake, and Villefort answers that if she lived she might one day kill the boy too. Is his sentence a judge’s cold logic, a husband’s cruelty, or a kind of mercy? Notice that he says nothing about his own secret, which no one in the room knows.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`109-1`,chapter:109,kind:`craft`,title:`The chorus in the gallery`,text:`Dumas makes us wait for the trial by sitting us beside Beauchamp, Debray and Château-Renaud as they gossip. These three young men have been his chorus all through the Paris chapters: they report what society thinks, and they are often wrong. Their rumours about Edward and the absent count show how Paris turns tragedy into entertainment.`,themes:[`wealth-status`]},{id:`109-2`,chapter:109,kind:`craft`,title:`A veiled lady in the crowd`,text:`Beauchamp is sure the veiled woman is Madame Danglars, and Debray blushes. We know she has every reason to be here, and more than the young men suspect: the man on trial is her son.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`110-1`,chapter:110,kind:`craft`,title:`Answering in his own order`,text:`Benedetto turns the court’s routine questions into a weapon. Name, age, birthplace, profession: he answers each in an order he chooses, so that “Auteuil” and “27 September 1817” land before the name of his father. Watch how Dumas uses the formal procedure of a French trial to build suspense one answer at a time.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`110-2`,chapter:110,kind:`theme`,title:`The prosecutor in the dock`,text:`Villefort has spent his career sending others to the scaffold, and in Madame Danglars’ presence he once said his place should be on the prisoner’s bench, not the judge’s seat. Now that is exactly where his own son puts him. The garden at Auteuil, the red damask room and the buried box all return in open court.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`110-3`,chapter:110,kind:`theme`,title:`“In the hands of an avenging God”`,text:`Villefort does not deny anything. He calls himself the prisoner of an avenging God and walks out. Notice that he does not blame any human enemy: he reads his fall as providence, which is how the count has asked us to read every fall in the book.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`110-4`,chapter:110,kind:`question`,title:`Who is the real criminal here?`,text:`Benedetto is a forger, thief and murderer, but he tells the court his adopted father taught him the crime was his father’s, not his. Is he right? Think about how much of Benedetto’s life was made by a single night at Auteuil, and how much by his own choices.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`]},{id:`111-1`,chapter:111,kind:`craft`,title:`Too late by half an hour`,text:`In the carriage Villefort changes his mind: his wife must live, they will flee together, she will repent and raise Edward. Dumas lets us share his hope for a few pages, then has him find the bolted door. The forgotten fan in the carriage is a small, painful touch, reminding him of the sentence he passed that morning.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`111-2`,chapter:111,kind:`theme`,title:`“Are you well avenged?”`,text:`The count’s unmasking before Villefort should be his triumph, and Dumas stages it as one: the wig pulled off, the wedding day in Marseilles recalled. But Villefort drags him upstairs to the bodies, and the question turns back on the avenger. For the first time the count’s vengeance has struck a child who did nothing.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`111-3`,chapter:111,kind:`theme`,title:`The limits of playing God`,text:`The count tries for a quarter of an hour to revive Edward, and none of his skill can do it. The man who has seemed to control life and death, who has spoken of himself as the hand of providence, meets something he cannot undo. Watch how this shakes his certainty that he had the right.`,themes:[`providence`]},{id:`111-4`,chapter:111,kind:`motif`,title:`Digging in the garden`,text:`Mad Villefort digs wildly in the garden, crying that he will find the child. It is the act of that night at Auteuil, repeated as madness. The buried child has come back to him in court, and now his mind returns to the burial.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`112-1`,chapter:112,kind:`craft`,title:`Returning to the first harbour`,text:`The count and Maximilian arrive at the quay in Marseilles where the Pharaon came in at the very start of the novel. Albert now sails from it for Africa, with a veiled woman waving. Dumas brings the story back to its opening setting so that we measure how much has changed.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`112-2`,chapter:112,kind:`theme`,title:`Mercédès blames herself`,text:`The count admits he caused her misfortunes, and Mercédès refuses to hate him. Instead she accuses herself: she was weak, she married Fernand, she let Edmond be lost. Of everyone the count has confronted, she is the only one who takes on guilt rather than deflecting it, and that changes the tone of their meeting.`,themes:[`loyalty-betrayal`,`hope-despair`]},{id:`112-3`,chapter:112,kind:`question`,title:`“Without pride or hatred”`,text:`Looking back over Paris from Villejuif, the count declares his mission complete and says he leaves without pride or hatred, though with many regrets. Do you believe him? Consider what he has seen in the last few days, and whether his words sound like a conclusion or a man trying to convince himself.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`113-1`,chapter:113,kind:`craft`,title:`Hearing his own story as legend`,text:`The guide at the Château d’If tells the tale of No. 34, the prisoner who took the dead priest’s place in the sack, as a local legend, and assumes he drowned. The count stands in his old cell and hears himself turned into a story. It is a neat way for Dumas to retell the novel’s founding events from the outside.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`,`identity-disguise`]},{id:`113-2`,chapter:113,kind:`motif`,title:`Faria’s manuscript`,text:`The great treatise on strips of cloth returns, and its epigraph, about trampling dragons and lions, seems to answer the count’s doubts. Faria was the first to give Edmond knowledge, a fortune and a purpose. Notice that the count looks for reassurance not in his own wealth but in the words of his old teacher.`,themes:[`providence`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`113-3`,chapter:113,kind:`craft`,title:`The name he murmurs`,text:`Passing the Catalans, the village where Mercédès lived, the count murmurs a woman’s name, and Dumas tells us it is Haydée’s. It is a tiny sentence placed exactly where we expect the other name. Pay attention to small turns like this; Dumas uses them to show change without explaining it.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`114-1`,chapter:114,kind:`craft`,title:`Rome again`,text:`Pastrini’s hotel, the catacombs of Saint Sebastian, Luigi Vampa reading Plutarch, even the cell where Albert was once held: the Roman carnival chapters come back almost scene for scene. Dumas uses the repetition for comedy, with Danglars, who knows no Italian beyond musical terms, stumbling through a setup the reader recognises at once.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`114-2`,chapter:114,kind:`theme`,title:`The banker who counts everything`,text:`Kidnapped and locked in a bandit’s cave, Danglars comforts himself with arithmetic: twice Albert’s ransom would still leave him over five million. Even in terror he thinks in sums. Dumas is setting up a punishment fitted precisely to the man’s mind.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`115-1`,chapter:115,kind:`craft`,title:`A punishment played as comedy`,text:`A hundred thousand francs for a chicken, and the same for dry bread, because the bandits keep “a fixed price for all our provisions.” Dumas writes the scene as broad farce, with Peppino unfailingly polite. The joke is that Danglars is being charged by the rules of his own trade.`,themes:[`wealth-status`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`115-2`,chapter:115,kind:`motif`,title:`Hunger`,text:`Watch how Dumas makes us feel the hunger: the guards’ bread, cheese and onions, which start to look surprisingly appetizing, and the chicken that seems very thin for its price. Remember who in this story once died of hunger, and whose doing it was.`,themes:[`the-past-returns`]},{id:`116-1`,chapter:116,kind:`theme`,title:`A punishment shaped to the crime`,text:`Danglars is not killed; his blood must not be shed. Instead he is made to starve with gold in his pocket, until he dreams of an old man dying of hunger on a pallet. The count’s revenge on each enemy has been fitted to the man: exposure for Morcerf, the law for Villefort, and for Danglars, money and hunger.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`the-past-returns`]},{id:`116-2`,chapter:116,kind:`theme`,title:`Forgiveness`,text:`For the first time the count says “I forgive you” to one of his enemies. He lets Danglars keep the last 50,000 francs and returns the stolen millions to the hospitals. Compare this ending with the deaths and madness of the last few chapters, and think about what changed in the count between Edward’s death and this cave.`,themes:[`revenge-justice`,`providence`]},{id:`116-3`,chapter:116,kind:`craft`,title:`“Not the Count of Monte Cristo, but Edmond Dantès”`,text:`Each enemy has now heard the same name: Morcerf from a sailor’s costume, Villefort from a priest without his wig, Danglars from a man in a cloak. Dumas uses the unmasking three times, each in a different key. Here it comes only after Danglars repents, as part of a pardon rather than a sentence.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`]},{id:`116-4`,chapter:116,kind:`motif`,title:`Hair turned white`,text:`At dawn Danglars bends over a stream and sees that his hair has turned completely white. It is the oldest mark of terror in storytelling, a sign that something has aged him beyond his years. He is free and alive, but changed in a way that shows on his face.`,themes:[`prison-freedom`]},{id:`117-1`,chapter:117,kind:`craft`,title:`A death scene turned inside out`,text:`Dumas builds the chapter as a suicide: the date kept, the jewelled box, the greenish drug, Morrel’s sight fading. Then a door opens. The whole novel’s pattern of burial and return, from the sack thrown into the sea onward, is replayed one last time for someone else.`,themes:[`hope-despair`,`prison-freedom`]},{id:`117-2`,chapter:117,kind:`theme`,title:`Saving as atonement`,text:`The count hopes God will accept the saving of these two lives as his atonement. After Edward, he no longer speaks as the sure agent of providence; he speaks as a man asking to be forgiven. Haydée’s confession of love is the answer he chooses to read as God’s pardon.`,themes:[`providence`,`revenge-justice`]},{id:`117-3`,chapter:117,kind:`theme`,title:`“Wait and hope”`,text:`The novel ends on two words, which the count says sum up all human wisdom. Think back to the prisoner who despaired and wanted to starve himself in his cell, and to the month he asked of Maximilian. The phrase gathers up the book’s long argument between hope and despair.`,themes:[`hope-despair`]},{id:`117-4`,chapter:117,kind:`question`,title:`A white sail on the horizon`,text:`The count leaves his fortune, his houses and his name in Paris behind, and sails away with Haydée. Is this a happy ending for Edmond Dantès, or a man escaping a world he can no longer live in? Consider what he gained, what he lost, and what he chose to give away.`,themes:[`identity-disguise`,`wealth-status`]}]};export{e as default};